bba8b661\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Your Skin, But Make It Private Sale.\", \"The Glow-Up No One Saw Coming.\", \"Skincare That Knows Your Humidity.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 and Variant 2 are not genuinely different along the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both use the same soft-sand negative space, matte product placement, natural daylight, and unretouched skin — the image briefs are near-identical in mood, composition, and styling. The copy also overlaps in tone and idea: both lead with a quiet, intimate, \'secret deal\' angle (\'little kawan-kawan exclusive\' vs \'kept it quiet... secret she\'s happy to share\'), and both rely on the same Manglish/code-switch pattern (\'kawan-kawan\' / \'Review jujur\'). To fix: make the three variants truly distinct in aesthetic treatment — e.g., one warm & intimate (keep Variant 1), one bold & graphic (flat-lay, high-contrast colour blocks, no model), one editorial/lifestyle (on-location, environmental context like a mamak or commute). Copy must shift accordingly: Variant 2 should drop the \'quiet/secret\' hook and adopt a visually and tonally different angle (e.g., playful, graphic, price-led per marketplace learnings); Variant 3 can keep the humidity hook but needs a visually distinct treatment from the other two (currently it still uses the same soft-sand wall and matte surface). Also tighten Variant 3\'s body copy — \'sebum-smart essentials without the markup\' is fine, but \'these prices vanish at midnight\' is a fear-based urgency line that brushes against the \'no fear-based copy\' guardrail; rephrase to a plainspoken deadline (\'Sale ends at midnight\') instead.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 and Variant 2 are not genuinely different along the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both use the same soft-sand negative space, matte product placement, natural daylight, and unretouched skin — the image briefs are near-identical in mood, composition, and styling. The copy also overlaps in tone and idea: both lead with a quiet, intimate, \'secret deal\' angle (\'little kawan-kawan exclusive\' vs \'kept it quiet... secret she\'s happy to share\'), and both rely on the same Manglish/code-switch pattern (\'kawan-kawan\' / \'Review jujur\'). To fix: make the three variants truly distinct in aesthetic treatment — e.g., one warm & intimate (keep Variant 1), one bold & graphic (flat-lay, high-contrast colour blocks, no model), one editorial/lifestyle (on-location, environmental context like a mamak or commute). Copy must shift accordingly: Variant 2 should drop the \'quiet/secret\' hook and adopt a visually and tonally different angle (e.g., playful, graphic, price-led per marketplace learnings); Variant 3 can keep the humidity hook but needs a visually distinct treatment from the other two (currently it still uses the same soft-sand wall and matte surface). Also tighten Variant 3\'s body copy — \'sebum-smart essentials without the markup\' is fine, but \'these prices vanish at midnight\' is a fear-based urgency line that brushes against the \'no fear-based copy\' guardrail; rephrase to a plainspoken deadline (\'Sale ends at midnight\') instead.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"For our regulars only.\", \"RM99. Full routine.\", \"Your commute, but kinder on kulit.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"## Problems\\n\\n### 1. Brand — Variant 2 copy overclaims the bundle scope\\n- **Headline says \\\"RM99. Full routine.\\\"** but the company knowledge defines the **Kulit Sihat starter** (cleanser + serum) as the RM99 bundle and the **Full routine** (all four products) as RM249. The body copy then correctly names the \\\"Kulit Sihat starter bundle,\\\" so the headline contradicts it and misstates the offer. **Fix:** change the headline to something like \\\"RM99. Starter routine.\\\" or \\\"RM99. Dua langkah.\\\" so it matches the actual bundle.\\n\\n### 2. Diversity — All three variants are assigned the same axis (\\\"aesthetic treatment\\\") and the execution does not create real variation\\n- The axis was repeated three times, and the resulting variants are essentially three different product angles (intimate friends, price-led flat-lay, commute environment) rather than three distinct *aesthetic treatments* of one idea. Because the axis itself is duplicated, the set cannot pass the diversity test — there is no single idea being treated three ways; there are three separate concepts. **Fix:** the creative director should assign three genuinely different aesthetic treatments (e.g., grainy phone-cam candour vs. clean graphic flat-lay vs. warm editorial daylight) applied to the *same* core message, or re-assign three different axes (e.g., hook, audience, offer framing) and rewrite accordingly.\\n\\n### 3. Execution — Variant 2 image brief asks the generator to produce readable text\\n- The brief specifies \\\"A bold, oversized price tag in soft sand (#EDE3D3) reading \'RM99\'\\\" inside the generated image. Image generators cannot reliably produce legible, correctly spelled text, and the brand\'s text placement plan already puts the headline and CTA as overlay type. **Fix:** remove the in-image \'RM99\' price tag from the prompt; let the overlay headline carry the price, and keep the image purely product + props.\\n\\n### 4. Minor — Variant 3 body copy uses \\\"sebum-smart\\\"\\n- The brand owns \\\"sebum-humidity interaction\\\" language, so \\\"sebum-smart\\\" is acceptable, but the phrase is not in the company knowledge glossary. Keep it only if it reads naturally; otherwise prefer \\\"humidity-first\\\" or \\\"made for Malaysian weather\\\" (which the body already says).\\n\\n## Required actions\\n1. **Rewrite Variant 2 headline** to correctly describe the RM99 Kulit Sihat starter bundle — do not call it the \\\"Full routine.\\\"\\n2. **Re-plan the variation axes.** Decide whether the three variants should explore three aesthetic treatments of one hook, or three different axes entirely, and rewrite/re-brief so the variants are genuinely distinct along the chosen axes.\\n3. **Remove the in-image \'RM99\' text** from Variant 2\'s image prompt; rely on overlay type for the price.\\n\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — ## Problems\\n\\n### 1. Brand — Variant 2 copy overclaims the bundle scope\\n- **Headline says \\\"RM99. Full routine.\\\"** but the company knowledge defines the **Kulit Sihat starter** (cleanser + serum) as the RM99 bundle and the **Full routine** (all four products) as RM249. The body copy then correctly names the \\\"Kulit Sihat starter bundle,\\\" so the headline contradicts it and misstates the offer. **Fix:** change the headline to something like \\\"RM99. Starter routine.\\\" or \\\"RM99. Dua langkah.\\\" so it matches the actual bundle.\\n\\n### 2. Diversity — All three variants are assigned the same axis (\\\"aesthetic treatment\\\") and the execution does not create real variation\\n- The axis was repeated three times, and the resulting variants are essentially three different product angles (intimate friends, price-led flat-lay, commute environment) rather than three distinct *aesthetic treatments* of one idea. Because the axis itself is duplicated, the set cannot pass the diversity test — there is no single idea being treated three ways; there are three separate concepts. **Fix:** the creative director should assign three genuinely different aesthetic treatments (e.g., grainy phone-cam candour vs. clean graphic flat-lay vs. warm editorial daylight) applied to the *same* core message, or re-assign three different axes (e.g., hook, audience, offer framing) and rewrite accordingly.\\n\\n### 3. Execution — Variant 2 image brief asks the generator to produce readable text\\n- The brief specifies \\\"A bold, oversized price tag in soft sand (#EDE3D3) reading \'RM99\'\\\" inside the generated image. Image generators cannot reliably produce legible, correctly spelled text, and the brand\'s text placement plan already puts the headline and CTA as overlay type. **Fix:** remove the in-image \'RM99\' price tag from the prompt; let the overlay headline carry the price, and keep the image purely product + props.\\n\\n### 4. Minor — Variant 3 body copy uses \\\"sebum-smart\\\"\\n- The brand owns \\\"sebum-humidity interaction\\\" language, so \\\"sebum-smart\\\" is acceptable, but the phrase is not in the company knowledge glossary. Keep it only if it reads naturally; otherwise prefer \\\"humidity-first\\\" or \\\"made for Malaysian weather\\\" (which the body already says).\\n\\n## Required actions\\n1. **Rewrite Variant 2 headline** to correctly describe the RM99 Kulit Sihat starter bundle — do not call it the \\\"Full routine.\\\"\\n2. **Re-plan the variation axes.** Decide whether the three variants should explore three aesthetic treatments of one hook, or three different axes entirely, and rewrite/re-brief so the variants are genuinely distinct along the chosen axes.\\n3. **Remove the in-image \'RM99\' text** from Variant 2\'s image prompt; rely on overlay type for the price.\\n\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Private Sale. Your skin, sorted.\", \"RM99. Starter routine.\", \"Insider access. Real results.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 and Variant 3 are not meaningfully different on the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both are lifestyle portraits of a Malaysian woman with visible skin texture, natural light, and a plainspoken \'older sister\' mood — Variant 1 is \'bright daylight by a window\', Variant 3 is \'golden-hour at a mamak stall\'. That is a lighting/location swap, not a distinct aesthetic treatment. Pick one portrait direction and replace the other with a genuinely different treatment (e.g. graphic product-led, UGC demo, split-face, or macro texture). Variant 2 is fine as the product-led/graphic axis but needs a small copy fix: the body says \'the serum that keeps kulit berminyak in check\' — the brand\'s competitive claim is humidity-first formulation / sebum-humidity interaction, not oil-control; reframe as \'the serum that handles our humidity\' or similar. Also note Variant 3\'s headline \'Real results\' edges toward before/after implication the guardrails forbid — if you keep a portrait variant, change the headline to avoid results language (e.g. \'Insider access. Humidity-first.\').\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 and Variant 3 are not meaningfully different on the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both are lifestyle portraits of a Malaysian woman with visible skin texture, natural light, and a plainspoken \'older sister\' mood — Variant 1 is \'bright daylight by a window\', Variant 3 is \'golden-hour at a mamak stall\'. That is a lighting/location swap, not a distinct aesthetic treatment. Pick one portrait direction and replace the other with a genuinely different treatment (e.g. graphic product-led, UGC demo, split-face, or macro texture). Variant 2 is fine as the product-led/graphic axis but needs a small copy fix: the body says \'the serum that keeps kulit berminyak in check\' — the brand\'s competitive claim is humidity-first formulation / sebum-humidity interaction, not oil-control; reframe as \'the serum that handles our humidity\' or similar. Also note Variant 3\'s headline \'Real results\' edges toward before/after implication the guardrails forbid — if you keep a portrait variant, change the headline to avoid results language (e.g. \'Insider access. Humidity-first.\').\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"concept_id\": \"17\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “The Art of the Deal”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"campaigns.md#3-b2c3e00cda085e89\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279\", \"products.md#4-b9423d548dbcfac4\", \"products.md#2-1fd832e6cfacf871\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Art of the Deal\", \"Smart Moves, Glowing Skin\", \"Worth Every Ringgit\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"1. **Variant 1 Copy (Brand Violation):** The body copy mentions the \'Kulit Sihat starter kit\'. According to `products.md#4-b9423d548dbcfac4`, the Kulit Sihat starter kit is priced at RM99. The campaign theme is \'The Art of the Deal\', and the copy implies a discount or special offer (\'grab... before it\'s gone\'). The brand does not discount the main acquisition offer (RM99) outside of platform mega-sales (where the Full Routine is pushed). This creates a false claim. The copy must either remove the \'deal\' implication or change the product reference to a sale-eligible item (though the starter kit is not one).\\n2. **Variant 2 Copy (Brand Violation):** The headline \'Smart Moves, Glowing Skin\' uses the term \'Glowing Skin\'. According to `campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78`, the \'Glass skin\' trend-chasing campaign failed and drew accusations of copying Korean brands. The lesson learned was to translate trends into the \'humidity-first\' angle. \'Glowing skin\' is effectively the same trend-chasing language and violates the brand\'s established learning. The copy must be rewritten to focus on the \'humidity-first\' benefit (e.g., \'Smart Moves, Humidity-Proof Skin\').\\n3. **Variant 3 Copy (Execution/Logic Error):** The body copy mentions \'Humidity-first formulas that work as hard as you do\'. However, the image brief and CTA (\'Claim Your Bundle\') focus entirely on the price/bundle aspect. While leading with price works on marketplace surfaces (`campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb3bba`), the copy\'s mention of \'humidity-first\' contradicts the purely price-led visual strategy for this specific variant. The copy should align with the \'deal\' angle without introducing conflicting benefit messaging, or the visual strategy should be adjusted (though the axis is aesthetic treatment, not message).\\n4. **Variant 3 Visual (Execution Error):** The image prompt describes a \'handwritten-style price tag is visible\'. The brand\'s visual identity (`brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279`) specifies \'Type is set in Söhne or a close humanist sans\'. A handwritten price tag violates the brand\'s typography guidelines and introduces an uncontrolled visual element that could look messy or off-brand. The price tag should be removed or styled in the brand\'s typeface.\\n5. **Diversity (Axis Failure):** All three variants are assigned the axis \'aesthetic treatment\'. However, the variants are not actually different in aesthetic treatment. They all use the same \'bright, natural daylight\' photography style, the same \'soft sand\' and \'dew green\' palette, and the same \'visible skin texture\' approach. The only difference is the subject matter (mamak stall vs. split-face demo vs. product bundle). This is a failure of the variation axis; the aesthetics are not distinct enough to be considered different \'treatments\'. The visual planner must create genuinely distinct aesthetic treatments (e.g., one high-contrast studio look, one gritty street-style look, one soft pastel look) while maintaining the brand\'s core identity.\\n\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — 1. **Variant 1 Copy (Brand Violation):** The body copy mentions the \'Kulit Sihat starter kit\'. According to `products.md#4-b9423d548dbcfac4`, the Kulit Sihat starter kit is priced at RM99. The campaign theme is \'The Art of the Deal\', and the copy implies a discount or special offer (\'grab... before it\'s gone\'). The brand does not discount the main acquisition offer (RM99) outside of platform mega-sales (where the Full Routine is pushed). This creates a false claim. The copy must either remove the \'deal\' implication or change the product reference to a sale-eligible item (though the starter kit is not one).\\n2. **Variant 2 Copy (Brand Violation):** The headline \'Smart Moves, Glowing Skin\' uses the term \'Glowing Skin\'. According to `campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78`, the \'Glass skin\' trend-chasing campaign failed and drew accusations of copying Korean brands. The lesson learned was to translate trends into the \'humidity-first\' angle. \'Glowing skin\' is effectively the same trend-chasing language and violates the brand\'s established learning. The copy must be rewritten to focus on the \'humidity-first\' benefit (e.g., \'Smart Moves, Humidity-Proof Skin\').\\n3. **Variant 3 Copy (Execution/Logic Error):** The body copy mentions \'Humidity-first formulas that work as hard as you do\'. However, the image brief and CTA (\'Claim Your Bundle\') focus entirely on the price/bundle aspect. While leading with price works on marketplace surfaces (`campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb3bba`), the copy\'s mention of \'humidity-first\' contradicts the purely price-led visual strategy for this specific variant. The copy should align with the \'deal\' angle without introducing conflicting benefit messaging, or the visual strategy should be adjusted (though the axis is aesthetic treatment, not message).\\n4. **Variant 3 Visual (Execution Error):** The image prompt describes a \'handwritten-style price tag is visible\'. The brand\'s visual identity (`brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279`) specifies \'Type is set in Söhne or a close humanist sans\'. A handwritten price tag violates the brand\'s typography guidelines and introduces an uncontrolled visual element that could look messy or off-brand. The price tag should be removed or styled in the brand\'s typeface.\\n5. **Diversity (Axis Failure):** All three variants are assigned the axis \'aesthetic treatment\'. However, the variants are not actually different in aesthetic treatment. They all use the same \'bright, natural daylight\' photography style, the same \'soft sand\' and \'dew green\' palette, and the same \'visible skin texture\' approach. The only difference is the subject matter (mamak stall vs. split-face demo vs. product bundle). This is a failure of the variation axis; the aesthetics are not distinct enough to be considered different \'treatments\'. The visual planner must create genuinely distinct aesthetic treatments (e.g., one high-contrast studio look, one gritty street-style look, one soft pastel look) while maintaining the brand\'s core identity.\\n\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Deal, Elevated\", \"Humidity-Proof, Wallet-Friendly\", \"The Art of the Smart Buy\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"All three variants fail the BRAND test on claims and voice, and the DIVERSITY test because the axis assignment is broken. Fix the copy first; visuals must be replanned around the new copy.\\n\\n1) Variant 1 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"choosing the right formula at the right price\\\" is a price/value claim we do not support; the bundle is RM249 and is pushed only during mega-sales. Reframe around humidity-first benefit, not price.\\n- \\\"Jom grab your Full Routine bundle while it lasts\\\" implies scarcity we cannot substantiate. Replace with a plainspoken, humidity-first benefit line and a clear bundle CTA without urgency.\\n- Voice is too polished and premium for our brand (\\\"The Deal, Elevated\\\"). Rewrite in our warm, plainspoken, slightly funny older-sister voice with one natural code-switch at most.\\n\\n2) Variant 2 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"Humidity-Proof, Wallet-Friendly\\\" and \\\"price that makes sense\\\" are price-led claims. On TikTok this underperforms for us; only use price-led framing on Shopee/Lazada surfaces. Rewrite to lead with humidity-first benefit.\\n- \\\"Memang berbaloi\\\" is fine as a single code-switch, but pair it with a concrete humidity benefit (e.g., no white cast, sebum control in heat) rather than a price claim.\\n- CTA \\\"Claim Your Deal\\\" is deal-led; switch to a benefit-led CTA such as \\\"Get the Full Routine\\\" or \\\"Shop Humidity-First\\\".\\n\\n3) Variant 3 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"Quality skincare doesn\'t have to break the bank\\\" and \\\"respects your budget\\\" are price-led claims we do not support. Rewrite to focus on humidity-first formulation and the bundle as a complete routine.\\n- Headline \\\"The Art of the Smart Buy\\\" is generic and not on-brand. Rewrite to a short, plainspoken line that names the humidity benefit or the routine.\\n- Body is too long and clinical. Shorten to 1–2 sentences, ask a question, use \\\"you\\\", and include one natural code-switch.\\n\\n4) DIVERSITY failure — axis assignment\\n- All three variants are assigned the same axis (\\\"aesthetic treatment\\\") repeated three times. That is not a valid variation plan. Assign three distinct axes (e.g., hook angle, cast/representation, format/length, proof type, surface-specific CTA) and ensure the copy actually differs along those axes, not just the image mood.\\n- As written, the three bodies are near-interchangeable price/value messages; they do not represent meaningfully different ideas. Rewrite so each variant has a distinct hook (e.g., humidity-first benefit demo, routine-completeness, founder candour) and a distinct CTA matched to surface.\\n\\n5) General copy guardrails to apply on rewrite\\n- Lead with humidity-first formulation; do not chase \\\"glass skin\\\" or trend aesthetics.\\n- Avoid price-led claims on TikTok/IG; reserve price-led framing for Shopee/Lazada.\\n- Keep sentences short, use \\\"you\\\", one Manglish/BM code-switch per caption max, no forced slang.\\n- Name the bundle correctly: \\\"Full Routine\\\" (all four) RM249; do not imply scarcity or time-limited availability unless tied to a confirmed mega-sale.\\n\\nOnce the copy is rewritten with distinct, on-brand hooks and correct claims, replan the visuals to match each new variant\'s axis and surface.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — All three variants fail the BRAND test on claims and voice, and the DIVERSITY test because the axis assignment is broken. Fix the copy first; visuals must be replanned around the new copy.\\n\\n1) Variant 1 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"choosing the right formula at the right price\\\" is a price/value claim we do not support; the bundle is RM249 and is pushed only during mega-sales. Reframe around humidity-first benefit, not price.\\n- \\\"Jom grab your Full Routine bundle while it lasts\\\" implies scarcity we cannot substantiate. Replace with a plainspoken, humidity-first benefit line and a clear bundle CTA without urgency.\\n- Voice is too polished and premium for our brand (\\\"The Deal, Elevated\\\"). Rewrite in our warm, plainspoken, slightly funny older-sister voice with one natural code-switch at most.\\n\\n2) Variant 2 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"Humidity-Proof, Wallet-Friendly\\\" and \\\"price that makes sense\\\" are price-led claims. On TikTok this underperforms for us; only use price-led framing on Shopee/Lazada surfaces. Rewrite to lead with humidity-first benefit.\\n- \\\"Memang berbaloi\\\" is fine as a single code-switch, but pair it with a concrete humidity benefit (e.g., no white cast, sebum control in heat) rather than a price claim.\\n- CTA \\\"Claim Your Deal\\\" is deal-led; switch to a benefit-led CTA such as \\\"Get the Full Routine\\\" or \\\"Shop Humidity-First\\\".\\n\\n3) Variant 3 — copy claims and voice\\n- \\\"Quality skincare doesn\'t have to break the bank\\\" and \\\"respects your budget\\\" are price-led claims we do not support. Rewrite to focus on humidity-first formulation and the bundle as a complete routine.\\n- Headline \\\"The Art of the Smart Buy\\\" is generic and not on-brand. Rewrite to a short, plainspoken line that names the humidity benefit or the routine.\\n- Body is too long and clinical. Shorten to 1–2 sentences, ask a question, use \\\"you\\\", and include one natural code-switch.\\n\\n4) DIVERSITY failure — axis assignment\\n- All three variants are assigned the same axis (\\\"aesthetic treatment\\\") repeated three times. That is not a valid variation plan. Assign three distinct axes (e.g., hook angle, cast/representation, format/length, proof type, surface-specific CTA) and ensure the copy actually differs along those axes, not just the image mood.\\n- As written, the three bodies are near-interchangeable price/value messages; they do not represent meaningfully different ideas. Rewrite so each variant has a distinct hook (e.g., humidity-first benefit demo, routine-completeness, founder candour) and a distinct CTA matched to surface.\\n\\n5) General copy guardrails to apply on rewrite\\n- Lead with humidity-first formulation; do not chase \\\"glass skin\\\" or trend aesthetics.\\n- Avoid price-led claims on TikTok/IG; reserve price-led framing for Shopee/Lazada.\\n- Keep sentences short, use \\\"you\\\", one Manglish/BM code-switch per caption max, no forced slang.\\n- Name the bundle correctly: \\\"Full Routine\\\" (all four) RM249; do not imply scarcity or time-limited availability unless tied to a confirmed mega-sale.\\n\\nOnce the copy is rewritten with distinct, on-brand hooks and correct claims, replan the visuals to match each new variant\'s axis and surface.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"No white cast. No heavy feel.\", \"One routine. Four steps.\", \"Real skin. Real heat.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') three times, but they don\'t actually vary along that axis — they vary along **message/offer** (single-product benefit vs. bundle vs. demo). That\'s a failure on DIVERSITY: pick one true variation axis (e.g., three different aesthetic treatments applied to the same message) or re-assign distinct axes.\\n\\nOn top of that, the copy itself has issues:\\n\\n• **Variant 2** — \'One routine. Four steps.\' is fine, but the body \'Why guess when you can have the full set?\' is generic and doesn\'t lean into the humidity-first positioning that the brand owns. It also pushes the Full Routine bundle, which company knowledge says is \'pushed only during platform mega-sales\' — if this isn\'t a mega-sale window, the CTA contradicts product strategy.\\n\\n• **Variant 3** — \'Watch the Demo\' CTA implies a video, but the format is listed as \'video\' for the whole set, so this is redundant. The body copy also breaks the brand voice rule: it reads like a generic social caption, not the warm, plainspoken older-sister voice. No Manglish or BM code-switch, no question, no \'you\'.\\n\\n• **All three variants** — none of the copy uses any bilingual code-switching, which the brand voice guidelines say is \'by default\'. At least one or two variants should include a natural Manglish/BM phrase.\\n\\nFix: Re-assign genuinely different axes (e.g., message angle, proof type, or audience segment) OR keep the same message and vary only the visual aesthetic treatment. Rewrite the copy to match the brand voice (short sentences, questions, \'you\', one code-switch per caption). Remove the bundle push unless it\'s a mega-sale window. Make sure each variant\'s CTA matches the actual format.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') three times, but they don\'t actually vary along that axis — they vary along **message/offer** (single-product benefit vs. bundle vs. demo). That\'s a failure on DIVERSITY: pick one true variation axis (e.g., three different aesthetic treatments applied to the same message) or re-assign distinct axes.\\n\\nOn top of that, the copy itself has issues:\\n\\n• **Variant 2** — \'One routine. Four steps.\' is fine, but the body \'Why guess when you can have the full set?\' is generic and doesn\'t lean into the humidity-first positioning that the brand owns. It also pushes the Full Routine bundle, which company knowledge says is \'pushed only during platform mega-sales\' — if this isn\'t a mega-sale window, the CTA contradicts product strategy.\\n\\n• **Variant 3** — \'Watch the Demo\' CTA implies a video, but the format is listed as \'video\' for the whole set, so this is redundant. The body copy also breaks the brand voice rule: it reads like a generic social caption, not the warm, plainspoken older-sister voice. No Manglish or BM code-switch, no question, no \'you\'.\\n\\n• **All three variants** — none of the copy uses any bilingual code-switching, which the brand voice guidelines say is \'by default\'. At least one or two variants should include a natural Manglish/BM phrase.\\n\\nFix: Re-assign genuinely different axes (e.g., message angle, proof type, or audience segment) OR keep the same message and vary only the visual aesthetic treatment. Rewrite the copy to match the brand voice (short sentences, questions, \'you\', one code-switch per caption). Remove the bundle push unless it\'s a mega-sale window. Make sure each variant\'s CTA matches the actual format.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"concept_id\": \"18\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “Golden Hour Luxury”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\", \"brand.md#4-43ba466ad3eb4506\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279\", \"products.md#3-09de9fb1f5dd796b\", \"brand.md#3-b27c3b7212dcf820\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Golden hour, zero white cast.\", \"Sunset skin, no filter needed.\", \"Catch the light, not the cast.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 2 copy violates the voice guardrails: \'brighter\' is used to describe baseline skin, not post-acne marks. Rewrite the body to avoid implying a change in overall skin tone — e.g. \'just looks like you—glowing\' or similar. Variants 1 and 3 are fine on claims. On diversity: all three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') and are not meaningfully different — they all use the same golden-hour portrait concept with a model, warm light, and sand-colored negative space. The axis should produce genuinely distinct visual treatments (e.g. flat-lay product shot, close-up texture macro, motion-blur lifestyle). Rewrite the copy to support three clearly different aesthetic directions, not three rewordings of the same idea.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 2 copy violates the voice guardrails: \'brighter\' is used to describe baseline skin, not post-acne marks. Rewrite the body to avoid implying a change in overall skin tone — e.g. \'just looks like you—glowing\' or similar. Variants 1 and 3 are fine on claims. On diversity: all three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') and are not meaningfully different — they all use the same golden-hour portrait concept with a model, warm light, and sand-colored negative space. The axis should produce genuinely distinct visual treatments (e.g. flat-lay product shot, close-up texture macro, motion-blur lifestyle). Rewrite the copy to support three clearly different aesthetic directions, not three rewordings of the same idea.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Golden hour, zero white cast.\", \"Sunscreen that survives the mamak.\", \"Your skin, but sun-safe.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"DIVERSITY FAIL: All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but the copy is not differentiated along that axis — they are three different product claims reworded. Variant 1 leads with \'zero white cast\', Variant 2 with \'water-resistant / survives the mamak\', and Variant 3 with \'chemical filters / dermatologist-reviewed\'. These are distinct benefit angles, not aesthetic treatments. The brief promised variation in aesthetic treatment (visual/editorial style), but the copy itself changes the core hook each time. If the axis is aesthetic treatment, the copy\'s core message and hook should remain consistent while the visual treatment shifts. Please rewrite so all three variants share the same core claim/hook (e.g., the humidity-first no-white-cast angle, which is your proven winner), and let the differentiation live in the visual treatment and tone of the imagery. Specific fixes: Variant 1 copy is fine as-is (it anchors on no-white-cast + humidity-first). Variant 2 should drop the \'survives the mamak / water-resistant\' hook and re-anchor on the same no-white-cast / humidity-first message, just voiced for the Shopee/Lazada functional tone (lead with the bundle price per your marketplace rules). Variant 3 should drop \'chemical filters / dermatologist-reviewed\' as the lead and re-anchor on the same core claim, voiced for the Watsons retail context. Also: Variant 2 body says \'Grab the bundle before it\'s gone\' — your marketplace learning says lead with the bundle *price*, not scarcity; state the price. Variant 3 says \'dermatologist-reviewed\' which is correct per guardrails, but it should not be the lead hook — humidity-first and no-white-cast are your proven performers.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — DIVERSITY FAIL: All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but the copy is not differentiated along that axis — they are three different product claims reworded. Variant 1 leads with \'zero white cast\', Variant 2 with \'water-resistant / survives the mamak\', and Variant 3 with \'chemical filters / dermatologist-reviewed\'. These are distinct benefit angles, not aesthetic treatments. The brief promised variation in aesthetic treatment (visual/editorial style), but the copy itself changes the core hook each time. If the axis is aesthetic treatment, the copy\'s core message and hook should remain consistent while the visual treatment shifts. Please rewrite so all three variants share the same core claim/hook (e.g., the humidity-first no-white-cast angle, which is your proven winner), and let the differentiation live in the visual treatment and tone of the imagery. Specific fixes: Variant 1 copy is fine as-is (it anchors on no-white-cast + humidity-first). Variant 2 should drop the \'survives the mamak / water-resistant\' hook and re-anchor on the same no-white-cast / humidity-first message, just voiced for the Shopee/Lazada functional tone (lead with the bundle price per your marketplace rules). Variant 3 should drop \'chemical filters / dermatologist-reviewed\' as the lead and re-anchor on the same core claim, voiced for the Watsons retail context. Also: Variant 2 body says \'Grab the bundle before it\'s gone\' — your marketplace learning says lead with the bundle *price*, not scarcity; state the price. Variant 3 says \'dermatologist-reviewed\' which is correct per guardrails, but it should not be the lead hook — humidity-first and no-white-cast are your proven performers.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"No White Cast. No Kompromi.\", \"2 for RM149. Humidity-Proof.\", \"Golden Hour, Zero Cast.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails on DIVERSITY and partially on BRAND. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but are not actually different in treatment — they are all golden-hour editorial portraits with the same sand-colour negative space, the same warm directional light, and the same matte product placement. The only real differences are the subject\'s ethnicity and the CTA destination, which are not aesthetic treatments. The copywriter must rewrite the three variants so each genuinely explores a different aesthetic treatment (for example: Variant 1 = editorial portrait with shallow DOF; Variant 2 = functional marketplace still-life with product as hero; Variant 3 = candid motion/lifestyle shot showing humidity in action). The current Variant 2 copy is already marketplace-functional and its visual brief is marketplace-functional, but the other two variants must be rewritten to match a genuinely different aesthetic axis. Additionally, Variant 1\'s body copy uses the word \'glow\' (\'Your glow stays yours\'), which edges close to the \'glass skin\' trend the brand explicitly rejected in Jan 2026 — change this to a humidity-first phrasing such as \'Your skin stays you\' or \'No chalky mask, just clean protection in the heat\'. Variant 2\'s headline \'2 for RM149\' is fine for a Shopee/Lazada surface but the body copy says \'Watsons shelves or delivered\' while the CTA says \'Find it at Watsons\' — pick one channel and stick to it, and ensure the price claim is accurate (RM89 each means 2 for RM149 is a real bundle, but confirm it is a current live offer). Variant 3\'s copy is the strongest and most on-brand; keep its humidity-first angle but ensure its visual brief describes a genuinely different aesthetic from Variant 1 (e.g. a candid mid-motion shot of the subject walking outdoors in golden light, with visible humidity on skin, rather than another static editorial portrait).\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails on DIVERSITY and partially on BRAND. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but are not actually different in treatment — they are all golden-hour editorial portraits with the same sand-colour negative space, the same warm directional light, and the same matte product placement. The only real differences are the subject\'s ethnicity and the CTA destination, which are not aesthetic treatments. The copywriter must rewrite the three variants so each genuinely explores a different aesthetic treatment (for example: Variant 1 = editorial portrait with shallow DOF; Variant 2 = functional marketplace still-life with product as hero; Variant 3 = candid motion/lifestyle shot showing humidity in action). The current Variant 2 copy is already marketplace-functional and its visual brief is marketplace-functional, but the other two variants must be rewritten to match a genuinely different aesthetic axis. Additionally, Variant 1\'s body copy uses the word \'glow\' (\'Your glow stays yours\'), which edges close to the \'glass skin\' trend the brand explicitly rejected in Jan 2026 — change this to a humidity-first phrasing such as \'Your skin stays you\' or \'No chalky mask, just clean protection in the heat\'. Variant 2\'s headline \'2 for RM149\' is fine for a Shopee/Lazada surface but the body copy says \'Watsons shelves or delivered\' while the CTA says \'Find it at Watsons\' — pick one channel and stick to it, and ensure the price claim is accurate (RM89 each means 2 for RM149 is a real bundle, but confirm it is a current live offer). Variant 3\'s copy is the strongest and most on-brand; keep its humidity-first angle but ensure its visual brief describes a genuinely different aesthetic from Variant 1 (e.g. a candid mid-motion shot of the subject walking outdoors in golden light, with visible humidity on skin, rather than another static editorial portrait).\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}]',NULL,3,9,9,2,'qwen','2026-08-26 09:15:22','2026-08-26 09:15:22'),(19,11,'Merdeka 2026 Hot Sales','generate','succeeded','2026-08-26 08:51:04',2096335,'9 variants across 3 concepts — 9 flagged for you','[{\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"concept_id\": \"16\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “Private Sale Invitation”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"products.md#4-b9423d548dbcfac4\", \"campaigns.md#3-b2c3e00cda085e89\", \"brand.md#3-b27c3b7212dcf820\", \"brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"products.md#1-ff70df38bba8b661\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Kulit Sihat Starter — RM99\", \"Private Sale: Full Routine RM249\", \"Members Only: RM99 Starter\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 and Variant 3 are not meaningfully different on the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both are portrait shots of a young Malaysian woman in natural daylight with visible skin texture, holding or near products, with negative space for text. The only real differences are ethnicity (Malay tudung vs. Chinese woman) and setting (window vs. mamak). That is casting variation, not aesthetic treatment — and casting is already mandated as diverse by the visual identity guidelines, so it cannot count as the creative axis. To pass, Variant 3 needs a genuinely different aesthetic treatment (e.g., a macro product-texture shot, a graphic colour-block layout, a motion-blur street scene, a split-frame demo, etc.), or the axis assignment needs to change. Variant 2 is fine and distinct (flat-lay still life). Copy itself is clean: no forbidden claims, prices and bundle contents match company knowledge, \'dermatologist-reviewed\' is used correctly, and the Manglish code-switching is within the one-or-twice limit. Once Variant 3\'s aesthetic is rethought, the image brief should be rewritten to match the new treatment.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 and Variant 3 are not meaningfully different on the assigned axis (aesthetic treatment). Both are portrait shots of a young Malaysian woman in natural daylight with visible skin texture, holding or near products, with negative space for text. The only real differences are ethnicity (Malay tudung vs. Chinese woman) and setting (window vs. mamak). That is casting variation, not aesthetic treatment — and casting is already mandated as diverse by the visual identity guidelines, so it cannot count as the creative axis. To pass, Variant 3 needs a genuinely different aesthetic treatment (e.g., a macro product-texture shot, a graphic colour-block layout, a motion-blur street scene, a split-frame demo, etc.), or the axis assignment needs to change. Variant 2 is fine and distinct (flat-lay still life). Copy itself is clean: no forbidden claims, prices and bundle contents match company knowledge, \'dermatologist-reviewed\' is used correctly, and the Manglish code-switching is within the one-or-twice limit. Once Variant 3\'s aesthetic is rethought, the image brief should be rewritten to match the new treatment.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Private Sale: RM99 for Kulit Sihat\", \"Your Private Sale Invitation\", \"RM99. Private Access Only.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails on DIVERSITY and BRAND voice. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but the copy is nearly identical in structure and message: all lead with \'Private Sale\' / \'Private Access\', all mention the RM99 price point, all reference the \'Kulit Sihat starter\' bundle, and all use the same CTA pattern. The headlines are functionally interchangeable — \'Private Sale: RM99 for Kulit Sihat\', \'Your Private Sale Invitation\', and \'RM99. Private Access Only.\' are the same idea reworded three times. The body copy also repeats the same information (cleanser + serum, dermatologist-reviewed, RM99, limited time) without meaningful variation in angle, tone, or hook.\\n\\nAdditionally, the copy lacks the brand\'s characteristic voice. The brand voice is \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' with \'natural Manglish and Bahasa Melayu code-switching\' and \'short sentence rhythm\'. All three variants read as generic e-commerce copy — none include any Malay/Manglish code-switching, none ask questions, none use \'you\' directly, and none have the \'knowledgeable older sister\' warmth. The copy is clinical and transactional rather than conversational.\\n\\nTo fix: Rewrite the three variants with genuinely different angles or hooks (e.g., one leads with the humidity-first benefit, one with the price/value, one with a relatable scenario like the \'lepak tapi glowing\' approach). Inject the brand voice — add Malay/Manglish code-switching, use shorter sentences, ask a question, speak directly to \'you\'. Make the variants feel like three different conversations, not three versions of the same announcement.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails on DIVERSITY and BRAND voice. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but the copy is nearly identical in structure and message: all lead with \'Private Sale\' / \'Private Access\', all mention the RM99 price point, all reference the \'Kulit Sihat starter\' bundle, and all use the same CTA pattern. The headlines are functionally interchangeable — \'Private Sale: RM99 for Kulit Sihat\', \'Your Private Sale Invitation\', and \'RM99. Private Access Only.\' are the same idea reworded three times. The body copy also repeats the same information (cleanser + serum, dermatologist-reviewed, RM99, limited time) without meaningful variation in angle, tone, or hook.\\n\\nAdditionally, the copy lacks the brand\'s characteristic voice. The brand voice is \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' with \'natural Manglish and Bahasa Melayu code-switching\' and \'short sentence rhythm\'. All three variants read as generic e-commerce copy — none include any Malay/Manglish code-switching, none ask questions, none use \'you\' directly, and none have the \'knowledgeable older sister\' warmth. The copy is clinical and transactional rather than conversational.\\n\\nTo fix: Rewrite the three variants with genuinely different angles or hooks (e.g., one leads with the humidity-first benefit, one with the price/value, one with a relatable scenario like the \'lepak tapi glowing\' approach). Inject the brand voice — add Malay/Manglish code-switching, use shorter sentences, ask a question, speak directly to \'you\'. Make the variants feel like three different conversations, not three versions of the same announcement.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Kulit Berpeluh, Tapi Nak Glowing?\", \"RM99 for a Full Routine Starter?\", \"Lepak Late, Skin Still Okay\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 copy claims the starter is \'formulated for sebum-humidity interaction\' — the company knowledge says the brand *talks about* sebum-humidity interaction as a positioning claim, but does not state the product is formulated for it; soften to the supported phrasing (\'humidity-first formulation\' or \'talks about sebum-humidity interaction\'). Variant 2 headline \'RM99 for a Full Routine Starter?\' is ambiguous and risks overclaim — the company knowledge defines \'Full routine\' as the RM249 four-product bundle, so \'Full Routine Starter\' conflates two named offers; rewrite to \'RM99 for the Kulit Sihat starter?\' or \'RM99 for a full starter routine?\' to stay accurate. Variant 3 body copy says \'keeps your routine simple and effective\' — \'effective\' is an efficacy claim the guardrails don\'t support; replace with a plainspoken benefit like \'keeps your skin calm and glowing\' (glowing is allowed per the Raya campaign precedent). On diversity: all three variants are assigned the same axis \'aesthetic treatment\' and the briefs do differentiate visually (humidity bead / price card / mamak night), but the copy hooks (humidity / price / lepak late) are actually three different axes (benefit, price, lifestyle), so the axis label is wrong and the set is not truly a single-axis aesthetic variation — relabel the axis or reassign variants so they genuinely vary only aesthetic treatment while keeping one hook.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 copy claims the starter is \'formulated for sebum-humidity interaction\' — the company knowledge says the brand *talks about* sebum-humidity interaction as a positioning claim, but does not state the product is formulated for it; soften to the supported phrasing (\'humidity-first formulation\' or \'talks about sebum-humidity interaction\'). Variant 2 headline \'RM99 for a Full Routine Starter?\' is ambiguous and risks overclaim — the company knowledge defines \'Full routine\' as the RM249 four-product bundle, so \'Full Routine Starter\' conflates two named offers; rewrite to \'RM99 for the Kulit Sihat starter?\' or \'RM99 for a full starter routine?\' to stay accurate. Variant 3 body copy says \'keeps your routine simple and effective\' — \'effective\' is an efficacy claim the guardrails don\'t support; replace with a plainspoken benefit like \'keeps your skin calm and glowing\' (glowing is allowed per the Raya campaign precedent). On diversity: all three variants are assigned the same axis \'aesthetic treatment\' and the briefs do differentiate visually (humidity bead / price card / mamak night), but the copy hooks (humidity / price / lepak late) are actually three different axes (benefit, price, lifestyle), so the axis label is wrong and the set is not truly a single-axis aesthetic variation — relabel the axis or reassign variants so they genuinely vary only aesthetic treatment while keeping one hook.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"concept_id\": \"17\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “The Art of the Deal”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"campaigns.md#3-b2c3e00cda085e89\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279\", \"products.md#4-b9423d548dbcfac4\", \"products.md#2-1fd832e6cfacf871\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Art of the Deal\", \"Worth Every Sen\", \"Harga Right, Results Real\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but they are not actually different. They are all the same idea — \'premium quality at a sensible price\' — reworded three times. \'The Art of the Deal\', \'Worth Every Sen\', and \'Harga Right, Results Real\' all communicate the same value proposition with no real variation in tone, angle, or approach. The visuals are nearly identical in composition and aesthetic (natural daylight, sand-colored backgrounds, negative space layouts). To fix: the copywriter needs to create genuinely distinct variants. For example, one could lead with the humidity-first formulation angle, another with a specific skin concern (oily skin, no white cast), and a third with a social proof or founder-led angle. The visuals should then be replanned to support these different approaches.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but they are not actually different. They are all the same idea — \'premium quality at a sensible price\' — reworded three times. \'The Art of the Deal\', \'Worth Every Sen\', and \'Harga Right, Results Real\' all communicate the same value proposition with no real variation in tone, angle, or approach. The visuals are nearly identical in composition and aesthetic (natural daylight, sand-colored backgrounds, negative space layouts). To fix: the copywriter needs to create genuinely distinct variants. For example, one could lead with the humidity-first formulation angle, another with a specific skin concern (oily skin, no white cast), and a third with a social proof or founder-led angle. The visuals should then be replanned to support these different approaches.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Humidity-Proof Your Deal\", \"No White Cast. No White-Knuckle Prices.\", \"I Break Out. This Doesn\'t.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 (Aesthetic Treatment 1): Copy is on-brand — \'Humidity-Proof Your Deal\' and the RM99 Kulit Sihat starter reference are accurate and match the humidity-first positioning. However, the assigned axis is \'aesthetic treatment\' but the copy does not vary in tone, structure, or voice from Variant 2 or 3 — it is a standard benefit-led promo like Variant 2. The visual is the only differentiator here, which violates the axis assignment. Variant 2 (Aesthetic Treatment 2): Copy is strong and on-brand — \'No White Cast. No White-Knuckle Prices.\' is plainspoken and funny, and the no-white-cast demo is a proven winner. But again, this is a benefit-led promo variant, not an aesthetic treatment variant. The copy does not shift tone, rhythm, or voice to reflect a different aesthetic treatment. Variant 3 (Aesthetic Treatment 3): Copy is excellent and on-brand — \'I Break Out. This Doesn\'t.\' is confessional, founder-led, and matches the proven \'lepak tapi glowing\' winner. This is the only variant that actually reflects a distinct aesthetic treatment (intimate, unpolished, confessional). The problem is that Variants 1 and 2 are not really different from each other in terms of aesthetic treatment — they are both standard benefit-led promos with different product hooks. The axis assignment was \'aesthetic treatment\' for all three, meaning the copy should vary in tone, voice, or structure to reflect different aesthetic approaches. Variant 3 does this; Variants 1 and 2 do not. The copywriter needs to rewrite Variants 1 and 2 to reflect genuinely different aesthetic treatments — e.g., Variant 1 could be more clinical/educational, Variant 2 could be more playful/trend-led, while Variant 3 remains confessional. The visuals will need to be replanned around the new copy.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 (Aesthetic Treatment 1): Copy is on-brand — \'Humidity-Proof Your Deal\' and the RM99 Kulit Sihat starter reference are accurate and match the humidity-first positioning. However, the assigned axis is \'aesthetic treatment\' but the copy does not vary in tone, structure, or voice from Variant 2 or 3 — it is a standard benefit-led promo like Variant 2. The visual is the only differentiator here, which violates the axis assignment. Variant 2 (Aesthetic Treatment 2): Copy is strong and on-brand — \'No White Cast. No White-Knuckle Prices.\' is plainspoken and funny, and the no-white-cast demo is a proven winner. But again, this is a benefit-led promo variant, not an aesthetic treatment variant. The copy does not shift tone, rhythm, or voice to reflect a different aesthetic treatment. Variant 3 (Aesthetic Treatment 3): Copy is excellent and on-brand — \'I Break Out. This Doesn\'t.\' is confessional, founder-led, and matches the proven \'lepak tapi glowing\' winner. This is the only variant that actually reflects a distinct aesthetic treatment (intimate, unpolished, confessional). The problem is that Variants 1 and 2 are not really different from each other in terms of aesthetic treatment — they are both standard benefit-led promos with different product hooks. The axis assignment was \'aesthetic treatment\' for all three, meaning the copy should vary in tone, voice, or structure to reflect different aesthetic approaches. Variant 3 does this; Variants 1 and 2 do not. The copywriter needs to rewrite Variants 1 and 2 to reflect genuinely different aesthetic treatments — e.g., Variant 1 could be more clinical/educational, Variant 2 could be more playful/trend-led, while Variant 3 remains confessional. The visuals will need to be replanned around the new copy.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Science of the Deal.\", \"Smart girls don\'t overpay for skincare.\", \"I overpaid for my last serum.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The voice is clinical and technical (\\\"bind to sebum, not water\\\", \\\"climate-calibrated\\\", \\\"dermatologist-reviewed\\\") which directly contradicts the brand voice guideline: \\\"never clinical, never breathless\\\" and \\\"write the way a knowledgeable older sister explains things\\\". The headline \\\"The Science of the Deal\\\" also misaligns with the warm, plainspoken tone. Fix: rewrite Variant 1 in the brand\'s conversational voice — explain the humidity-first benefit in plain language without jargon, and keep the clinical claims (dermatologist-reviewed, etc.) only if they can be stated naturally. Variants 2 and 3 are strong and on-voice, but the set needs all three variants to pass the brand test before shipping.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The voice is clinical and technical (\\\"bind to sebum, not water\\\", \\\"climate-calibrated\\\", \\\"dermatologist-reviewed\\\") which directly contradicts the brand voice guideline: \\\"never clinical, never breathless\\\" and \\\"write the way a knowledgeable older sister explains things\\\". The headline \\\"The Science of the Deal\\\" also misaligns with the warm, plainspoken tone. Fix: rewrite Variant 1 in the brand\'s conversational voice — explain the humidity-first benefit in plain language without jargon, and keep the clinical claims (dermatologist-reviewed, etc.) only if they can be stated naturally. Variants 2 and 3 are strong and on-voice, but the set needs all three variants to pass the brand test before shipping.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"concept_id\": \"18\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “Golden Hour Luxury”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\", \"brand.md#4-43ba466ad3eb4506\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"brand.md#5-ced846224b2b1279\", \"products.md#3-09de9fb1f5dd796b\", \"brand.md#3-b27c3b7212dcf820\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Golden hour, zero white cast.\", \"Sunscreen that disappears, not your glow.\", \"Your skin, but sun-safe.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned the axis \'aesthetic treatment\' but the copy is nearly identical in concept, structure, and message: each leads with a \'no white cast / disappears\' hook, follows with a dewy-finish payoff, and ends with a golden-hour/glow CTA. The only real variation is the model\'s ethnicity and the text placement zone in the image brief — which is a visual axis, not a copy axis. The copy itself must be rewritten so each variant takes a genuinely different angle (e.g., humidity-first performance, water-resistant durability, reapplication education, or the no-white-cast demo itself) while staying in the warm, plainspoken, slightly funny \'older sister\' voice.\\n\\nAdditional copy fixes required across all variants:\\n• Variant 2 headline \'Sunscreen that disappears, not your glow\' — fine, but the body copy must stop leaning on the same \'no white cast, dewy finish\' idea as Variants 1 and 3. Rewrite to a different axis (e.g., humidity-first formulation, which is the brand\'s owned competitive claim and is absent from all three variants).\\n• Variant 3 body copy: \'Reapply every 40 minutes\' is incorrect. The product is water-resistant for 40 minutes; reapply *after* 40 minutes of water exposure or sweating, not on a fixed 40-minute clock. Correct to \'Reapply after 40 minutes in the water\' or similar.\\n• All three variants miss the brand\'s humidity-first positioning. At least one variant should lead with sebum-humidity interaction, which is the competitive claim the company knowledge says we own and always talk about.\\n• Tone check: the copy is clean of forbidden claims (no whitening, no medical claims, no fear), but it reads slightly editorial/poetic for a Shopee/Lazada carousel. Remember the channel rule: marketplace surfaces want functional, scannable copy that leads with the problem solved. Keep the golden-hour mood but make the benefit sharper and less lyrical.\\n\\nOnce the copy is rewritten along genuinely different axes, the visual planner should replan the image briefs to match the new angles — the current briefs all describe the same golden-hour portrait with negative-space text zones, which only works because the copy is identical.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned the axis \'aesthetic treatment\' but the copy is nearly identical in concept, structure, and message: each leads with a \'no white cast / disappears\' hook, follows with a dewy-finish payoff, and ends with a golden-hour/glow CTA. The only real variation is the model\'s ethnicity and the text placement zone in the image brief — which is a visual axis, not a copy axis. The copy itself must be rewritten so each variant takes a genuinely different angle (e.g., humidity-first performance, water-resistant durability, reapplication education, or the no-white-cast demo itself) while staying in the warm, plainspoken, slightly funny \'older sister\' voice.\\n\\nAdditional copy fixes required across all variants:\\n• Variant 2 headline \'Sunscreen that disappears, not your glow\' — fine, but the body copy must stop leaning on the same \'no white cast, dewy finish\' idea as Variants 1 and 3. Rewrite to a different axis (e.g., humidity-first formulation, which is the brand\'s owned competitive claim and is absent from all three variants).\\n• Variant 3 body copy: \'Reapply every 40 minutes\' is incorrect. The product is water-resistant for 40 minutes; reapply *after* 40 minutes of water exposure or sweating, not on a fixed 40-minute clock. Correct to \'Reapply after 40 minutes in the water\' or similar.\\n• All three variants miss the brand\'s humidity-first positioning. At least one variant should lead with sebum-humidity interaction, which is the competitive claim the company knowledge says we own and always talk about.\\n• Tone check: the copy is clean of forbidden claims (no whitening, no medical claims, no fear), but it reads slightly editorial/poetic for a Shopee/Lazada carousel. Remember the channel rule: marketplace surfaces want functional, scannable copy that leads with the problem solved. Keep the golden-hour mood but make the benefit sharper and less lyrical.\\n\\nOnce the copy is rewritten along genuinely different axes, the visual planner should replan the image briefs to match the new angles — the current briefs all describe the same golden-hour portrait with negative-space text zones, which only works because the copy is identical.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Golden hour, no white cast.\", \"Lembap? Not on our watch.\", \"Swim, sweat, repeat.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test: all three variants are assigned the same axis (aesthetic treatment) but do not actually differ in aesthetic treatment — they are three different product benefits (no white cast, humidity/matte T-zone, water resistance) illustrated with different models and settings. This is one idea (benefit demo) reworded three times, not a variation of aesthetic treatment. The copywriter must rewrite three variants that genuinely explore different aesthetic treatments of the same core idea (e.g., warm golden-hour glow, high-contrast editorial shadow play, soft diffused haze) while keeping the product benefit consistent. The visual planner will then replan around the new copy. Variant 2 copy also uses overly clinical language (\'tackles sebum-sweat interaction\') that breaks the \'knowledgeable older sister\' voice and risks sounding like a medical claim — simplify to plain, plainspoken language about staying matte in humidity. Variant 3 copy is fine but must be repositioned under the new shared benefit angle once the axis is fixed.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test: all three variants are assigned the same axis (aesthetic treatment) but do not actually differ in aesthetic treatment — they are three different product benefits (no white cast, humidity/matte T-zone, water resistance) illustrated with different models and settings. This is one idea (benefit demo) reworded three times, not a variation of aesthetic treatment. The copywriter must rewrite three variants that genuinely explore different aesthetic treatments of the same core idea (e.g., warm golden-hour glow, high-contrast editorial shadow play, soft diffused haze) while keeping the product benefit consistent. The visual planner will then replan around the new copy. Variant 2 copy also uses overly clinical language (\'tackles sebum-sweat interaction\') that breaks the \'knowledgeable older sister\' voice and risks sounding like a medical claim — simplify to plain, plainspoken language about staying matte in humidity. Variant 3 copy is fine but must be repositioned under the new shared benefit angle once the axis is fixed.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\", \"aesthetic treatment\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Golden hour, but make it matte.\", \"Sharp light. Soft skin.\", \"Dreamy haze, zero white cast.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The headline \'Dreamy haze, zero white cast\' and body copy \'That soft-focus golden hour look? Embun makes it real\' chase the \'glass skin\' aesthetic the company knowledge explicitly says has not worked (\'Borrowing the aesthetic wholesale reads as inauthentic\'). The visual brief also describes a \'soft-focus, dreamy glow\' which contradicts the brand\'s visual identity of \'bright, natural daylight, visible skin texture\' and the voice guardrail against \'retouched skin texture\'. The copy also lacks the required bilingual code-switching (no Manglish or Bahasa Melayu) and doesn\'t reflect the \'humidity-first\' positioning that is the brand\'s competitive claim. Variants 1 and 2 are stronger but all three variants need rewrite to: (1) avoid glass-skin/dreamy aesthetic language, (2) incorporate natural code-switching, (3) emphasize humidity-first formulation, and (4) maintain plainspoken \'older sister\' voice. Once copy is revised, visuals will need replanning to match.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The headline \'Dreamy haze, zero white cast\' and body copy \'That soft-focus golden hour look? Embun makes it real\' chase the \'glass skin\' aesthetic the company knowledge explicitly says has not worked (\'Borrowing the aesthetic wholesale reads as inauthentic\'). The visual brief also describes a \'soft-focus, dreamy glow\' which contradicts the brand\'s visual identity of \'bright, natural daylight, visible skin texture\' and the voice guardrail against \'retouched skin texture\'. The copy also lacks the required bilingual code-switching (no Manglish or Bahasa Melayu) and doesn\'t reflect the \'humidity-first\' positioning that is the brand\'s competitive claim. Variants 1 and 2 are stronger but all three variants need rewrite to: (1) avoid glass-skin/dreamy aesthetic language, (2) incorporate natural code-switching, (3) emphasize humidity-first formulation, and (4) maintain plainspoken \'older sister\' voice. Once copy is revised, visuals will need replanning to match.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}]',NULL,3,9,9,2,'qwen','2026-08-26 09:25:59','2026-08-26 09:25:59'),(20,11,'Merdeka 2026 Hot Sales','render','failed','2026-08-26 09:26:17',612837,'Creative for variant 34 flagged for you','[{\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 25}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 25\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Background rendered and headline composited at top-left\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Checking the finished creative\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The CTA overlay overlaps the subject\'s face, covering part of her cheek and jawline, and the dew on her forehead looks like sweat droplets rather than a natural skin glow.\", \"status\": \"flagged\"}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"QA: flagged — The CTA overlay overlaps the subject\'s face, covering part of her cheek and jawline, and the dew on her forehead looks like sweat droplets rather than a natural skin glow.\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": true, \"variant_id\": 25}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Re-rendering variant 25\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Background rendered and headline composited at top-left\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Checking the finished creative\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The text overlay box overlaps the subject\'s face, covering her forehead, eye, and cheek, and the prod