','2026-08-27 03:10:05'),(24,12,'Speed check — Merdeka 2026','generate','succeeded','2026-08-27 03:10:05',87631,'9 variants across 3 concepts — 9 flagged for you','[{\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"time_of_day_setting\", \"commute_mode\", \"skin_concern_focus\"], \"concept_id\": \"19\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “The Humidity-Proof Commute”\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"comparison_angle\", \"visual_metaphor\", \"cta_urgency\"], \"concept_id\": \"20\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “Local Made, Local Logic”\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"demonstration_method\", \"cast_demographic\", \"text_overlay_style\"], \"concept_id\": \"21\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “The \'Gel-Cream\' Reveal”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"products.md#0-9acf1c4f15ecbab2\", \"campaigns.md#3-b2c3e00cda085e89\", \"products.md#2-1fd832e6cfacf871\", \"brand.md#0-76302cee87b2c58a\", \"products.md#3-09de9fb1f5dd796b\", \"products.md#1-ff70df38bba8b661\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"time_of_day_setting\", \"commute_mode\", \"skin_concern_focus\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"products.md#2-1fd832e6cfacf871\", \"brand.md#2-0304363243e2d334\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"campaigns.md#3-b2c3e00cda085e89\", \"brand.md#0-76302cee87b2c58a\", \"campaigns.md#0-ac2fa8295ccb2bba\", \"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\", \"products.md#0-9acf1c4f15ecbab2\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"comparison_angle\", \"visual_metaphor\", \"cta_urgency\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"products.md#2-1fd832e6cfacf871\", \"products.md#0-9acf1c4f15ecbab2\", \"products.md#1-ff70df38bba8b661\", \"brand.md#0-76302cee87b2c58a\", \"products.md#3-09de9fb1f5dd796b\", \"brand.md#1-9eae976fa1da0c9c\", \"brand.md#3-b27c3b7212dcf820\", \"campaigns.md#1-de048ddcf1406c78\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"demonstration_method\", \"cast_demographic\", \"text_overlay_style\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Watch it vanish.\", \"Tudung-friendly hydration.\", \"Not a cream. A gel-cream.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Not built for London fog.\", \"Like morning dew, not thick butter.\", \"Merdeka sale ends soon.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"7AM vs 7PM: Still matte?\", \"LRT air-con? More like steam room.\", \"Oily outside, thirsty inside.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails on two fronts: Brand Compliance and Diversity.\\n\\n1. BRAND (Variant 3): The copy claims \'Ceramide and squalane protection.\' While the product contains these ingredients, the Company Knowledge defines its function as a \'barrier cream\' formulated to be light. It does not support a specific claim of \'protection\' in the sense of SPF or active defense, which could be confused with the Sunscreen product. More importantly, the CTA \'Try It Risk-Free\' is not supported by any policy in the Company Knowledge (no mention of money-back guarantees or free trials). This is an unsupported commercial claim.\\n\\n2. DIVERSITY (Variants 1 & 3): These variants are not sufficiently different. Variant 1 focuses on the texture \'vanishing\' (demonstration_method). Variant 3 focuses on the texture being \'not a cream\' but a \'gel-cream\' (text_overlay_style). However, the core message of both is \'It is light/gel-like, not heavy.\' The visual briefs also overlap significantly (macro texture vs. macro texture/spatula). They feel like the same idea reworded. Variant 2 is distinct (tudung demographic), but Variants 1 and 3 cannibalize each other.\\n\\nFix:\\n- Rewrite Variant 3 to focus on a different angle, such as \'Layering\' (e.g., \'Sits perfectly under sunscreen/makeup\') or \'Speed\' (e.g., \'Absorbs before you finish tying your shoes\'), ensuring the CTA is standard (\'Shop Now\').\\n- Ensure Variant 1 remains focused purely on the visual demonstration of absorption without making unsupported efficacy claims.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails on two fronts: Brand Compliance and Diversity.\\n\\n1. BRAND (Variant 3): The copy claims \'Ceramide and squalane protection.\' While the product contains these ingredients, the Company Knowledge defines its function as a \'barrier cream\' formulated to be light. It does not support a specific claim of \'protection\' in the sense of SPF or active defense, which could be confused with the Sunscreen product. More importantly, the CTA \'Try It Risk-Free\' is not supported by any policy in the Company Knowledge (no mention of money-back guarantees or free trials). This is an unsupported commercial claim.\\n\\n2. DIVERSITY (Variants 1 & 3): These variants are not sufficiently different. Variant 1 focuses on the texture \'vanishing\' (demonstration_method). Variant 3 focuses on the texture being \'not a cream\' but a \'gel-cream\' (text_overlay_style). However, the core message of both is \'It is light/gel-like, not heavy.\' The visual briefs also overlap significantly (macro texture vs. macro texture/spatula). They feel like the same idea reworded. Variant 2 is distinct (tudung demographic), but Variants 1 and 3 cannibalize each other.\\n\\nFix:\\n- Rewrite Variant 3 to focus on a different angle, such as \'Layering\' (e.g., \'Sits perfectly under sunscreen/makeup\') or \'Speed\' (e.g., \'Absorbs before you finish tying your shoes\'), ensuring the CTA is standard (\'Shop Now\').\\n- Ensure Variant 1 remains focused purely on the visual demonstration of absorption without making unsupported efficacy claims.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"demonstration_method\", \"cast_demographic\", \"text_overlay_style\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 2 are effectively the same idea (Embun is light/gel vs. competitors are heavy) reworded, rather than differing along their assigned axes. Additionally, Variant 3 fails the BRAND test regarding voice consistency.\\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n\\n1. **Variant 1 (Axis: comparison_angle):** The copy is acceptable, but ensure the \'comparison\' is distinct from Variant 2. Currently, both variants compare texture. To satisfy the \'comparison_angle\' axis, this variant should focus on the *origin/formulation logic* comparison (Global Winter Logic vs. Local Humidity Logic) rather than just the physical texture. Keep the \'London fog\' angle but sharpen the body copy to emphasize the *formulation intent* difference more aggressively than Variant 2.\\n\\n2. **Variant 2 (Axis: visual_metaphor):** This variant is too similar to Variant 1 in its core message (light vs. heavy). Since the axis is \'visual_metaphor\', the copy should lean harder into the metaphor established in the headline (\'morning dew\'). The body copy currently reads like a standard ingredient list (\'swapped heavy waxes for ceramide...\'). Rewrite the body to extend the metaphor—describe the *experience* of the dew (cooling, vanishing, fresh) rather than just the chemical swap. This will differentiate it from Variant 1\'s functional comparison.\\n\\n3. **Variant 3 (Axis: cta_urgency):** The headline \'Merdeka sale ends soon.\' is too generic and lacks the brand\'s \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' voice. It sounds like a standard marketplace banner, not Embun. The body copy is also dry. Rewrite to inject the \'knowledgeable older sister\' persona. Use Manglish or a more conversational tone to create urgency that feels personal rather than corporate. For example, instead of \'Stock up...\', try something like \'Jangan tunggu last minute. Heat waits for no one, and neither does this promo.\' Ensure the urgency feels native to the brand voice.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 2 are effectively the same idea (Embun is light/gel vs. competitors are heavy) reworded, rather than differing along their assigned axes. Additionally, Variant 3 fails the BRAND test regarding voice consistency.\\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n\\n1. **Variant 1 (Axis: comparison_angle):** The copy is acceptable, but ensure the \'comparison\' is distinct from Variant 2. Currently, both variants compare texture. To satisfy the \'comparison_angle\' axis, this variant should focus on the *origin/formulation logic* comparison (Global Winter Logic vs. Local Humidity Logic) rather than just the physical texture. Keep the \'London fog\' angle but sharpen the body copy to emphasize the *formulation intent* difference more aggressively than Variant 2.\\n\\n2. **Variant 2 (Axis: visual_metaphor):** This variant is too similar to Variant 1 in its core message (light vs. heavy). Since the axis is \'visual_metaphor\', the copy should lean harder into the metaphor established in the headline (\'morning dew\'). The body copy currently reads like a standard ingredient list (\'swapped heavy waxes for ceramide...\'). Rewrite the body to extend the metaphor—describe the *experience* of the dew (cooling, vanishing, fresh) rather than just the chemical swap. This will differentiate it from Variant 1\'s functional comparison.\\n\\n3. **Variant 3 (Axis: cta_urgency):** The headline \'Merdeka sale ends soon.\' is too generic and lacks the brand\'s \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' voice. It sounds like a standard marketplace banner, not Embun. The body copy is also dry. Rewrite to inject the \'knowledgeable older sister\' persona. Use Manglish or a more conversational tone to create urgency that feels personal rather than corporate. For example, instead of \'Stock up...\', try something like \'Jangan tunggu last minute. Heat waits for no one, and neither does this promo.\' Ensure the urgency feels native to the brand voice.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"comparison_angle\", \"visual_metaphor\", \"cta_urgency\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are essentially three different product pitches rather than variations of a single concept.\\n\\n1. Variant 2 (assigned axis: commute_mode) is actually pitching the **Embun Barrier Cream** (\'gel-cream barrier\'), while Variants 1 and 3 pitch the **Embun Serum**. You cannot mix hero products in a variant set intended to test specific axes for a single campaign theme. The copy must focus on the Serum to maintain consistency with V1 and V3, or the entire set needs to be re-scoped.\\n\\n2. Even if we ignore the product mismatch, the \'variation\' is weak. \\n   - V1 tests Time (Morning vs Night).\\n   - V3 tests Skin Concern (Oil vs Dehydration).\\n   - V2 tests Commute (LRT). \\n   However, V2\'s copy focuses entirely on the *product texture* (\'locks in moisture\', \'sits perfectly\') rather than the *commute experience* relative to the other variants. It feels like a standalone ad for the cream, not a variation of the \'Humidity-Proof Commute\' theme anchored by the Serum.\\n\\nFix: Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the **Embun Serum** (not the cream) and ensure the copy specifically addresses the **commute_mode** axis (e.g., how the serum survives the LRT/traffic jam) without changing the core product benefit established in V1 and V3. Ensure all three variants are selling the same hero product (Serum) so the axes (Time, Commute, Concern) are the only variables changing.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are essentially three different product pitches rather than variations of a single concept.\\n\\n1. Variant 2 (assigned axis: commute_mode) is actually pitching the **Embun Barrier Cream** (\'gel-cream barrier\'), while Variants 1 and 3 pitch the **Embun Serum**. You cannot mix hero products in a variant set intended to test specific axes for a single campaign theme. The copy must focus on the Serum to maintain consistency with V1 and V3, or the entire set needs to be re-scoped.\\n\\n2. Even if we ignore the product mismatch, the \'variation\' is weak. \\n   - V1 tests Time (Morning vs Night).\\n   - V3 tests Skin Concern (Oil vs Dehydration).\\n   - V2 tests Commute (LRT). \\n   However, V2\'s copy focuses entirely on the *product texture* (\'locks in moisture\', \'sits perfectly\') rather than the *commute experience* relative to the other variants. It feels like a standalone ad for the cream, not a variation of the \'Humidity-Proof Commute\' theme anchored by the Serum.\\n\\nFix: Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the **Embun Serum** (not the cream) and ensure the copy specifically addresses the **commute_mode** axis (e.g., how the serum survives the LRT/traffic jam) without changing the core product benefit established in V1 and V3. Ensure all three variants are selling the same hero product (Serum) so the axes (Time, Commute, Concern) are the only variables changing.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"time_of_day_setting\", \"commute_mode\", \"skin_concern_focus\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Watch it vanish.\", \"Tudung-friendly hydration.\", \"The 10-second rule.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"London Fog vs KL Haze\", \"Like morning dew, not mud\", \"Jangan tunggu last minute\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"7:45 AM. Humidity already winning?\", \"LRT packed. Skin still breathing.\", \"Oily surface, thirsty underneath?\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 3 fails the DIVERSITY test and contains a BRAND violation regarding product facts.\\n\\n1. DIVERSITY FAILURE: Variant 3 is assigned the axis \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy (\'The 10-second rule\') introduces a completely new conceptual angle (speed/layering) that does not exist in Variants 1 or 2. It is not a stylistic variation of an existing message; it is a third distinct idea. This violates the instruction to judge if variants are just one idea reworded vs. actually differing along assigned axes. The axis \'text_overlay_style\' implies the *message* should remain consistent while the *visual presentation of text* changes, or at least that the variants differ primarily by that axis. Here, the copy has drifted into a new theme entirely.\\n\\n2. BRAND VIOLATION: Variant 3 claims the cream \'layers perfectly under sunscreen without pilling.\' The Company Knowledge for Embun Barrier Cream states it is \'deliberately light\' and \'slowest seller,\' with no mention of testing for pilling under sunscreen. The *Serum* is noted as \'Suitable under sunscreen,\' but the Cream\'s specific compatibility with sunscreen (especially regarding pilling) is not supported by the provided product facts. We cannot claim \'no pilling\' without data.\\n\\nFix: Rewrite Variant 3 to align with the \'Gel-Cream Reveal\' theme used in V1 and V2, ensuring the core message remains consistent across all three variants so that the \'text_overlay_style\' axis can be properly tested (e.g., same headline, different font/placement/animation styles). Remove the unsupported \'no pilling\' claim.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 3 fails the DIVERSITY test and contains a BRAND violation regarding product facts.\\n\\n1. DIVERSITY FAILURE: Variant 3 is assigned the axis \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy (\'The 10-second rule\') introduces a completely new conceptual angle (speed/layering) that does not exist in Variants 1 or 2. It is not a stylistic variation of an existing message; it is a third distinct idea. This violates the instruction to judge if variants are just one idea reworded vs. actually differing along assigned axes. The axis \'text_overlay_style\' implies the *message* should remain consistent while the *visual presentation of text* changes, or at least that the variants differ primarily by that axis. Here, the copy has drifted into a new theme entirely.\\n\\n2. BRAND VIOLATION: Variant 3 claims the cream \'layers perfectly under sunscreen without pilling.\' The Company Knowledge for Embun Barrier Cream states it is \'deliberately light\' and \'slowest seller,\' with no mention of testing for pilling under sunscreen. The *Serum* is noted as \'Suitable under sunscreen,\' but the Cream\'s specific compatibility with sunscreen (especially regarding pilling) is not supported by the provided product facts. We cannot claim \'no pilling\' without data.\\n\\nFix: Rewrite Variant 3 to align with the \'Gel-Cream Reveal\' theme used in V1 and V2, ensuring the core message remains consistent across all three variants so that the \'text_overlay_style\' axis can be properly tested (e.g., same headline, different font/placement/animation styles). Remove the unsupported \'no pilling\' claim.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"demonstration_method\", \"cast_demographic\", \"text_overlay_style\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The headline \'London Fog vs KL Haze\' and body copy (\'Global brands formulate for dry winters\') are too clinical, academic, and distant. The brand voice is \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' like an \'older sister,\' not a formulation chemist or a geopolitical analyst. This reads like a whitepaper, not a conversation. Additionally, Variant 3\'s CTA urgency is weak; \'Shop the sale now\' is generic. It needs to leverage the specific \'Merdeka promo\' mentioned in the body or use more urgent, Manglish-inflected language consistent with the \'jom\' vibe. \\n\\nVariant 2 is strong on brand and metaphor, but Variant 1 and 3 need significant rewrites to match the \'older sister\' persona. Variant 1 should focus on the *feeling* of heaviness vs. lightness (e.g., \'Stop wearing a winter coat in July\') rather than the geography of London. Variant 3 needs a punchier CTA that matches the \'last minute\' panic (e.g., \'Grab before price naik\'). Since the copy tone is fundamentally off in V1 and weak in V3, the visuals will need replanning once the copy is fixed.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The headline \'London Fog vs KL Haze\' and body copy (\'Global brands formulate for dry winters\') are too clinical, academic, and distant. The brand voice is \'warm, plainspoken, a little funny\' like an \'older sister,\' not a formulation chemist or a geopolitical analyst. This reads like a whitepaper, not a conversation. Additionally, Variant 3\'s CTA urgency is weak; \'Shop the sale now\' is generic. It needs to leverage the specific \'Merdeka promo\' mentioned in the body or use more urgent, Manglish-inflected language consistent with the \'jom\' vibe. \\n\\nVariant 2 is strong on brand and metaphor, but Variant 1 and 3 need significant rewrites to match the \'older sister\' persona. Variant 1 should focus on the *feeling* of heaviness vs. lightness (e.g., \'Stop wearing a winter coat in July\') rather than the geography of London. Variant 3 needs a punchier CTA that matches the \'last minute\' panic (e.g., \'Grab before price naik\'). Since the copy tone is fundamentally off in V1 and weak in V3, the visuals will need replanning once the copy is fixed.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"comparison_angle\", \"visual_metaphor\", \"cta_urgency\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Watch it vanish.\", \"Light enough for KL heat.\", \"Gel-Cream, Not Heavy Cream.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are three rewordings of the same \'humidity is bad, serum is good\' concept.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 (assigned: time_of_day_setting) and Variant 2 (assigned: commute_mode) are functionally identical in message structure. Both present a humid scenario and offer the serum as the solution for a matte finish. Neither variant leverages its specific axis to create a distinct narrative hook. Variant 1 mentions \'7:45 AM\' but doesn\'t explore morning-specific skin needs (e.g., layering under sunscreen/makeup). Variant 2 mentions \'LRT\' but focuses on the same general benefit as V1.\\n\\n2. Variant 3 (assigned: skin_concern_focus) is the only one that actually addresses a specific skin mechanism (oily surface/dehydrated underneath), but it is isolated. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n- Rewrite Variant 1 to focus strictly on the MORNING ROUTINE. Mention how it sits under sunscreen or makeup without pilling, or how it saves time in the AM rush. The headline should reflect the urgency or ritual of the morning.\\n- Rewrite Variant 2 to focus strictly on the COMMUTE EXPERIENCE. Focus on the heat trap of the LRT/traffic and the feeling of freshness *after* the commute, or the confidence of not wiping sweat off your face in public. \\n- Keep Variant 3\'s focus on the skin concern but ensure the copy clearly distinguishes it from the situational benefits of V1 and V2.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are three rewordings of the same \'humidity is bad, serum is good\' concept.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 (assigned: time_of_day_setting) and Variant 2 (assigned: commute_mode) are functionally identical in message structure. Both present a humid scenario and offer the serum as the solution for a matte finish. Neither variant leverages its specific axis to create a distinct narrative hook. Variant 1 mentions \'7:45 AM\' but doesn\'t explore morning-specific skin needs (e.g., layering under sunscreen/makeup). Variant 2 mentions \'LRT\' but focuses on the same general benefit as V1.\\n\\n2. Variant 3 (assigned: skin_concern_focus) is the only one that actually addresses a specific skin mechanism (oily surface/dehydrated underneath), but it is isolated. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n- Rewrite Variant 1 to focus strictly on the MORNING ROUTINE. Mention how it sits under sunscreen or makeup without pilling, or how it saves time in the AM rush. The headline should reflect the urgency or ritual of the morning.\\n- Rewrite Variant 2 to focus strictly on the COMMUTE EXPERIENCE. Focus on the heat trap of the LRT/traffic and the feeling of freshness *after* the commute, or the confidence of not wiping sweat off your face in public. \\n- Keep Variant 3\'s focus on the skin concern but ensure the copy clearly distinguishes it from the situational benefits of V1 and V2.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"time_of_day_setting\", \"commute_mode\", \"skin_concern_focus\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 3 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Winter coat in July? Tak logik.\", \"Like morning dew, not thick paint.\", \"Merdeka promo won’t wait.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"7:45 AM. No time to wait.\", \"LRT air-con? More like steam room.\", \"Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 3 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 3 are not distinct; they both rely on the exact same \'demonstration of absorption\' concept (Variant 1: \'Watch it vanish... See the difference\'; Variant 3: \'Watch how quickly it vanishes\'). The assigned axis for Variant 3 was \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy and visual brief simply repeat the macro-absorption demo from Variant 1, merely adding a graphic layout. This is the \'one idea reworded\' failure mode.\\n\\nTo fix this:\\n1. Keep Variant 1 (Demonstration Method) as is—it effectively shows the texture change.\\n2. Keep Variant 2 (Cast Demographic) as is—it effectively targets the KL heat context.\\n3. Rewrite Variant 3 completely. Since the axis is \'text_overlay_style\', the copy should lean into a bold, typographic, or comparative angle that doesn\'t require a macro skin demo to make sense. For example, focus on the \'Gel vs. Cream\' distinction using a split-screen product shot or a text-heavy kinetic typography approach, rather than repeating the \'vanishing\' claim. The current Variant 3 copy (\'We fixed the texture...\') is weak and redundant.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 3 are not distinct; they both rely on the exact same \'demonstration of absorption\' concept (Variant 1: \'Watch it vanish... See the difference\'; Variant 3: \'Watch how quickly it vanishes\'). The assigned axis for Variant 3 was \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy and visual brief simply repeat the macro-absorption demo from Variant 1, merely adding a graphic layout. This is the \'one idea reworded\' failure mode.\\n\\nTo fix this:\\n1. Keep Variant 1 (Demonstration Method) as is—it effectively shows the texture change.\\n2. Keep Variant 2 (Cast Demographic) as is—it effectively targets the KL heat context.\\n3. Rewrite Variant 3 completely. Since the axis is \'text_overlay_style\', the copy should lean into a bold, typographic, or comparative angle that doesn\'t require a macro skin demo to make sense. For example, focus on the \'Gel vs. Cream\' distinction using a split-screen product shot or a text-heavy kinetic typography approach, rather than repeating the \'vanishing\' claim. The current Variant 3 copy (\'We fixed the texture...\') is weak and redundant.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"3 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 3 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The copy claims \'before harga naik back to normal,\' which implies a temporary price increase or that the current RM79 is a discount off a higher standard price. Company Knowledge states the product is RM79/50ml flat; there is no mention of a standard price higher than this, nor a promo structure that involves raising prices later. This is a forbidden claim. Additionally, the tone in Variant 3 (\'Merdeka promo won’t wait\') feels breathless and salesy, violating the \'never breathless\' guardrail in the Brand Voice section. Variant 1 and 2 are acceptable in voice and factuality, but because Variant 3 requires a rewrite of the offer/hook, the whole set needs `revise_copy` to ensure the visual planner can adjust the imagery (currently tied to a specific \'grabbing/shopping\' urgency) to match the new angle.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The copy claims \'before harga naik back to normal,\' which implies a temporary price increase or that the current RM79 is a discount off a higher standard price. Company Knowledge states the product is RM79/50ml flat; there is no mention of a standard price higher than this, nor a promo structure that involves raising prices later. This is a forbidden claim. Additionally, the tone in Variant 3 (\'Merdeka promo won’t wait\') feels breathless and salesy, violating the \'never breathless\' guardrail in the Brand Voice section. Variant 1 and 2 are acceptable in voice and factuality, but because Variant 3 requires a rewrite of the offer/hook, the whole set needs `revise_copy` to ensure the visual planner can adjust the imagery (currently tied to a specific \'grabbing/shopping\' urgency) to match the new angle.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned different axes (time, commute, skin concern), but they all describe the exact same product benefit: fast absorption and matte finish. \\n\\nSpecifically, Variant 3 is assigned the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis but the copy (\'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\') and visual brief focus entirely on texture balance and hydration, ignoring the \'humidity-first\' positioning required by the brand. More critically, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are functionally identical in their promise (speed/matte). Variant 1 focuses on \'absorbs in 30 seconds\' and Variant 2 focuses on \'keeps you matte\'. These are not distinct enough to justify separate ad spend; they are rewording the same \'fast/lightweight\' benefit. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n1. Rewrite Variant 3 to strictly follow the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis by highlighting the specific mechanism of \'sebum-humidity interaction\' mentioned in Company Knowledge, rather than just generic oily/dry balance. The current copy is too generic.\\n2. Ensure Variant 1 (Time) and Variant 2 (Commute) have distinctly different hooks. Variant 1 should lean heavily into the \'30-second absorption\' speed claim for the morning rush. Variant 2 should lean into the \'humidity resistance/staying power\' during the commute, perhaps referencing the \'8 in 10 felt less greasy\' stat or the specific feeling of sweat/humidity trap, rather than just repeating \'matte\'.\\n\\nThe visuals are generally strong and executable, but they must be replanned once the copy distinguishes the benefits clearly.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned different axes (time, commute, skin concern), but they all describe the exact same product benefit: fast absorption and matte finish. \\n\\nSpecifically, Variant 3 is assigned the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis but the copy (\'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\') and visual brief focus entirely on texture balance and hydration, ignoring the \'humidity-first\' positioning required by the brand. More critically, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are functionally identical in their promise (speed/matte). Variant 1 focuses on \'absorbs in 30 seconds\' and Variant 2 focuses on \'keeps you matte\'. These are not distinct enough to justify separate ad spend; they are rewording the same \'fast/lightweight\' benefit. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n1. Rewrite Variant 3 to strictly follow the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis by highlighting the specific mechanism of \'sebum-humidity interaction\' mentioned in Company Knowledge, rather than just generic oily/dry balance. The current copy is too generic.\\n2. Ensure Variant 1 (Time) and Variant 2 (Commute) have distinctly different hooks. Variant 1 should lean heavily into the \'30-second absorption\' speed claim for the morning rush. Variant 2 should lean into the \'humidity resistance/staying power\' during the commute, perhaps referencing the \'8 in 10 felt less greasy\' stat or the specific feeling of sweat/humidity trap, rather than just repeating \'matte\'.\\n\\nThe visuals are generally strong and executable, but they must be replanned once the copy distinguishes the benefits clearly.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"3 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}]',NULL,3,9,9,2,'qwen','2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(25,12,'Speed check — Merdeka 2026','render','failed','2026-08-27 03:11:33',260,'DashScope refused the request (403): {\"code\":\"AllocationQuota.FreeTierOnly\",\"message\":\"The free quota has been exhausted. To continue accessing the model on a paid basis, please complete your payment information （or disable the \\\"use free tier only\\\" mode in the management console if already completed).\",\"request_id\":\"faa6541a-43d9-9bf3-80d0-0d0d15d71676\"}','[{\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 43}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 43\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 44}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 44\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 45}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 45\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 46}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 46\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 47}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 47\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 48}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 48\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 49}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 49\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 50}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 50\"}]','DashScope refused the request (403): {\"code\":\"AllocationQuota.FreeTierOnly\",\"message\":\"The free quota has been exhausted. To continue accessing the model on a paid basis, please complete your payment information （or disable the \\\"use free tier only\\\" mode in the management console if already completed).\",\"request_id\":\"faa6541a-43d9-9bf3-80d0-0d0d15d71676\"}',0