n\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Velvet Matte Finish\", \"Boardroom Ready\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Sunday Ritual\", \"Soft Petals, Sharp Nails\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes (Finish Focus and Styling Mood), but the submitted variants do not form a coherent matrix or distinct pairs along these axes. \\n\\n1. Variant 1 focuses on \'Matte Finish\' with a \'Cashmere/Sensory\' mood.\\n2. Variant 2 focuses on \'Office Chic\' styling with a \'Glossy/Nude\' finish.\\n\\nThese are two completely different ideas (Texture vs. Occasion) rather than variations of a single concept. To properly test the axes, we need variants that isolate these variables. For example:\\n- Variant A: Matte Finish + Office Chic\\n- Variant B: Glossy Finish + Office Chic\\n- Variant C: Matte Finish + Evening Glam\\n- Variant D: Glossy Finish + Evening Glam\\n\\nCurrently, we cannot determine if \'Matte\' or \'Office Chic\' is the stronger driver because they are conflated in separate, unrelated variants. The copywriter must restructure the variants to strictly follow the assigned axes, ensuring that for each axis, there are clear counterparts (e.g., same styling, different finish; or same finish, different styling). Additionally, ensure the \'Evening Glam\' option is represented as promised in the concept.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes (Finish Focus and Styling Mood), but the submitted variants do not form a coherent matrix or distinct pairs along these axes. \\n\\n1. Variant 1 focuses on \'Matte Finish\' with a \'Cashmere/Sensory\' mood.\\n2. Variant 2 focuses on \'Office Chic\' styling with a \'Glossy/Nude\' finish.\\n\\nThese are two completely different ideas (Texture vs. Occasion) rather than variations of a single concept. To properly test the axes, we need variants that isolate these variables. For example:\\n- Variant A: Matte Finish + Office Chic\\n- Variant B: Glossy Finish + Office Chic\\n- Variant C: Matte Finish + Evening Glam\\n- Variant D: Glossy Finish + Evening Glam\\n\\nCurrently, we cannot determine if \'Matte\' or \'Office Chic\' is the stronger driver because they are conflated in separate, unrelated variants. The copywriter must restructure the variants to strictly follow the assigned axes, ensuring that for each axis, there are clear counterparts (e.g., same styling, different finish; or same finish, different styling). Additionally, ensure the \'Evening Glam\' option is represented as promised in the concept.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\", \"Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\' and \'Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\'. However, the submitted variants do not reflect these axes correctly.\\n\\n1. Missing Axis Content: There is no variant addressing the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both variants focus on the self/personal ritual.\\n2. Lack of Differentiation: Variant 1 is a \'Hand-Held/Lifestyle\' shot focused on Self-Gifting. Variant 2 is a \'Flat Lay\' shot, but the copy (\'Details in Focus\', \'considered collection\') still frames it as a personal styling choice rather than a distinct \'Bestie Bundle\' or a clearly differentiated visual axis counterpart that contrasts with Variant 1\'s narrative. Essentially, we have one idea (Personal Luxury) presented twice with different images, missing the required \'Bestie Bundle\' angle entirely.\\n\\nTo fix: \\n- Rewrite Variant 2 to explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing (e.g., Headline: \'The Perfect Pair\', Body: \'Curated for sharing...\').\\n- Ensure the visual brief for Variant 2 supports this new copy (e.g., showing two sets, or a gift-ready presentation) while maintaining the \'Flat Lay\' composition axis if desired, or swap one variant to ensure both axes are covered distinctly.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\' and \'Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\'. However, the submitted variants do not reflect these axes correctly.\\n\\n1. Missing Axis Content: There is no variant addressing the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both variants focus on the self/personal ritual.\\n2. Lack of Differentiation: Variant 1 is a \'Hand-Held/Lifestyle\' shot focused on Self-Gifting. Variant 2 is a \'Flat Lay\' shot, but the copy (\'Details in Focus\', \'considered collection\') still frames it as a personal styling choice rather than a distinct \'Bestie Bundle\' or a clearly differentiated visual axis counterpart that contrasts with Variant 1\'s narrative. Essentially, we have one idea (Personal Luxury) presented twice with different images, missing the required \'Bestie Bundle\' angle entirely.\\n\\nTo fix: \\n- Rewrite Variant 2 to explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing (e.g., Headline: \'The Perfect Pair\', Body: \'Curated for sharing...\').\\n- Ensure the visual brief for Variant 2 supports this new copy (e.g., showing two sets, or a gift-ready presentation) while maintaining the \'Flat Lay\' composition axis if desired, or swap one variant to ensure both axes are covered distinctly.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\", \"Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Soft-Touch Matte\", \"Boardroom Ready\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"1. BRAND VIOLATION (Variant 1): The copy claims a \'salon-inspired finish.\' The Company Knowledge explicitly forbids \'salon-equivalent\' claims unless substantiated, which is not indicated here. Change to \'polished, editorial finish\' or similar approved language.\\n2. BRAND VIOLATION (Variant 1): The copy refers to \'our guided mannequin kit\' as if it is the primary product being sold in this context, but the CTA is \'Shop the Ritual Kit\' (which implies a bundle not clearly defined in Company Knowledge) while Variant 2 sells \'Press-On Sets\'. Ensure the product being sold is clear. More importantly, the claim \'turning a simple application into a moment of mindful self-care\' is acceptable, but the \'salon-inspired\' claim is the hard fail.\\n3. DIVERSITY FAILURE: The variants do not differ along the assigned axes in a meaningful way for a carousel set. \\n   - Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep)\' but only presents \'Sunday Reset\'. It lacks the \'Pre-Event Prep\' counterpart to complete the axis assignment, or the set is missing a variant. \\n   - Variant 2 is assigned \'Prop styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent)\' but only presents \'Floral Accent\'. \\n   - Crucially, these two variants are not distinct enough in *concept* to form a cohesive A/B test or carousel narrative because they are testing completely different products/features (Mannequin Kit vs. Press-on Sets with flowers) rather than varying the *same* core message along the specified axes. \\n   - However, the primary issue is that the prompt asks for a set of variants based on specific axes. Variant 1 ignores the \'Pre-Event Prep\' half of its axis. Variant 2 ignores the \'Minimalist Tray\' half of its axis. We are missing two variants entirely, or the copywriter has misunderstood the assignment to produce single instances instead of pairs. \\n   - Even if we judge just these two: They are not variations of one idea. One sells the Mannequin Kit (tool), the other sells the Nails (product) with a floral prop. This is a category error, not a variation. \\n   - Fix: Rewrite to ensure all variants adhere to the brand guardrails (remove \'salon-inspired\') and actually represent the assigned axes. If the goal is to test \'Setting\', we need a Sunday version AND a Pre-Event version. If the goal is \'Prop Styling\', we need Minimalist AND Floral. Currently, we have one of each mixed up. The copywriter needs to clarify the product focus (Kit vs. Nails) and generate the missing counterparts for the axes, ensuring no forbidden claims.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — 1. BRAND VIOLATION (Variant 1): The copy claims a \'salon-inspired finish.\' The Company Knowledge explicitly forbids \'salon-equivalent\' claims unless substantiated, which is not indicated here. Change to \'polished, editorial finish\' or similar approved language.\\n2. BRAND VIOLATION (Variant 1): The copy refers to \'our guided mannequin kit\' as if it is the primary product being sold in this context, but the CTA is \'Shop the Ritual Kit\' (which implies a bundle not clearly defined in Company Knowledge) while Variant 2 sells \'Press-On Sets\'. Ensure the product being sold is clear. More importantly, the claim \'turning a simple application into a moment of mindful self-care\' is acceptable, but the \'salon-inspired\' claim is the hard fail.\\n3. DIVERSITY FAILURE: The variants do not differ along the assigned axes in a meaningful way for a carousel set. \\n   - Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep)\' but only presents \'Sunday Reset\'. It lacks the \'Pre-Event Prep\' counterpart to complete the axis assignment, or the set is missing a variant. \\n   - Variant 2 is assigned \'Prop styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent)\' but only presents \'Floral Accent\'. \\n   - Crucially, these two variants are not distinct enough in *concept* to form a cohesive A/B test or carousel narrative because they are testing completely different products/features (Mannequin Kit vs. Press-on Sets with flowers) rather than varying the *same* core message along the specified axes. \\n   - However, the primary issue is that the prompt asks for a set of variants based on specific axes. Variant 1 ignores the \'Pre-Event Prep\' half of its axis. Variant 2 ignores the \'Minimalist Tray\' half of its axis. We are missing two variants entirely, or the copywriter has misunderstood the assignment to produce single instances instead of pairs. \\n   - Even if we judge just these two: They are not variations of one idea. One sells the Mannequin Kit (tool), the other sells the Nails (product) with a floral prop. This is a category error, not a variation. \\n   - Fix: Rewrite to ensure all variants adhere to the brand guardrails (remove \'salon-inspired\') and actually represent the assigned axes. If the goal is to test \'Setting\', we need a Sunday version AND a Pre-Event version. If the goal is \'Prop Styling\', we need Minimalist AND Floral. Currently, we have one of each mixed up. The copywriter needs to clarify the product focus (Kit vs. Nails) and generate the missing counterparts for the axes, ensuring no forbidden claims.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep)\", \"Prop styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Art of Self-Indulgence\", \"Gifts That Speak Volumes\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Sunday Reset\", \"Pre-Event Polish\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes (Finish Focus and Styling Mood), but only two variants are provided, each addressing only one axis. This results in a fragmented presentation rather than a cohesive set of variants that explore the combinations or distinct themes properly. Specifically:\\n\\n1. Variant 1 addresses \'Finish focus (Matte)\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis.\\n2. Variant 2 addresses \'Styling mood (Office Chic)\' but ignores the \'Finish focus\' axis (it mentions glossy, but doesn\'t contrast it or define it as the primary variable against matte).\\n\\nTo fix this, you need to provide variants that clearly differentiate along the assigned axes. Ideally, you should have variants that represent the extremes of both axes or combine them (e.g., Matte + Office, Glossy + Evening, etc.), or at least ensure that if you are showing one axis, the other is held constant or explicitly defined. Currently, these are just two different product shots without a clear comparative structure that fulfills the \'variation axes\' promise. \\n\\nAdditionally, the copy for Variant 1 claims \'A velvet finish that catches the light without the glare.\' While poetic, \'velvet\' might be interpreted as a texture claim that could be misleading if the product is just standard matte. Ensure \'velvet\' is an approved descriptive term for the matte finish in your brand guidelines. If not, stick to \'matte\'.\\n\\nPlease rewrite the copy to ensure the variants clearly demonstrate the promised variations (Matte vs Glossy AND Office vs Evening). For example, you might need 4 variants to cover all combinations, or 2 variants that clearly pit Matte/Office against Glossy/Evening if those are the intended pairings. As it stands, they are not comparable on the same axes.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes (Finish Focus and Styling Mood), but only two variants are provided, each addressing only one axis. This results in a fragmented presentation rather than a cohesive set of variants that explore the combinations or distinct themes properly. Specifically:\\n\\n1. Variant 1 addresses \'Finish focus (Matte)\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis.\\n2. Variant 2 addresses \'Styling mood (Office Chic)\' but ignores the \'Finish focus\' axis (it mentions glossy, but doesn\'t contrast it or define it as the primary variable against matte).\\n\\nTo fix this, you need to provide variants that clearly differentiate along the assigned axes. Ideally, you should have variants that represent the extremes of both axes or combine them (e.g., Matte + Office, Glossy + Evening, etc.), or at least ensure that if you are showing one axis, the other is held constant or explicitly defined. Currently, these are just two different product shots without a clear comparative structure that fulfills the \'variation axes\' promise. \\n\\nAdditionally, the copy for Variant 1 claims \'A velvet finish that catches the light without the glare.\' While poetic, \'velvet\' might be interpreted as a texture claim that could be misleading if the product is just standard matte. Ensure \'velvet\' is an approved descriptive term for the matte finish in your brand guidelines. If not, stick to \'matte\'.\\n\\nPlease rewrite the copy to ensure the variants clearly demonstrate the promised variations (Matte vs Glossy AND Office vs Evening). For example, you might need 4 variants to cover all combinations, or 2 variants that clearly pit Matte/Office against Glossy/Evening if those are the intended pairings. As it stands, they are not comparable on the same axes.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\", \"Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Matte: Quiet Luxury\", \"Evening: High Shine\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The assigned variation axes are \'Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\' and \'Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\'. However, the variants do not follow this matrix.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is correctly framed as a Self-Gift but uses a \'Hand-Held/Lifestyle Portrait\' composition.\\n2. Variant 2 is correctly framed as a Gift/Bundle but uses a \'Flat Lay\' composition.\\n\\nWhile they differ in both axes, they represent only two corners of the potential 2x2 matrix, and more importantly, the prompt implies these are the *only* two variants being shipped for a concept that promised specific axes. If the intention was to test the axes independently, we are missing the cross-variants (e.g., Self-Gift as Flat Lay, or Gift as Portrait). \\n\\nHowever, the critical failure is in the COPY of Variant 2 regarding the BRAND test. The body copy states: \'These sets are designed for gifting, offering a stylish and considered beauty experience for two.\' This implies the product is a shared experience or contains enough for two people simultaneously, which contradicts the Company Knowledge defining them as \'Curated press-on nail sets\' (singular use per set) and a \'disposable self-service manicure mannequin kit\' (singular tool). While gifting is allowed, phrasing it as an \'experience for two\' is ambiguous and potentially misleading about the product quantity/nature. It should be framed as \'a gift for your confidante to enjoy her own ritual,\' not a joint experience.\\n\\nAdditionally, the visual brief for Variant 2 mentions \'two champagne flutes clinking gently in the blurred background periphery.\' An image generator cannot produce \'clinking\' (an action/sound) in a static image, and \'blurred background periphery\' with specific items often results in artifacts. This is an EXECUTION issue, but since the copy also needs tightening for brand accuracy, `revise_copy` is the primary action. The copywriter must clarify the gifting angle to ensure it doesn\'t imply a shared single-use product, and then the visuals can be replanned to strictly adhere to the assigned axes if more variants are needed, or fix the prompt issues.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The assigned variation axes are \'Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\' and \'Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\'. However, the variants do not follow this matrix.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is correctly framed as a Self-Gift but uses a \'Hand-Held/Lifestyle Portrait\' composition.\\n2. Variant 2 is correctly framed as a Gift/Bundle but uses a \'Flat Lay\' composition.\\n\\nWhile they differ in both axes, they represent only two corners of the potential 2x2 matrix, and more importantly, the prompt implies these are the *only* two variants being shipped for a concept that promised specific axes. If the intention was to test the axes independently, we are missing the cross-variants (e.g., Self-Gift as Flat Lay, or Gift as Portrait). \\n\\nHowever, the critical failure is in the COPY of Variant 2 regarding the BRAND test. The body copy states: \'These sets are designed for gifting, offering a stylish and considered beauty experience for two.\' This implies the product is a shared experience or contains enough for two people simultaneously, which contradicts the Company Knowledge defining them as \'Curated press-on nail sets\' (singular use per set) and a \'disposable self-service manicure mannequin kit\' (singular tool). While gifting is allowed, phrasing it as an \'experience for two\' is ambiguous and potentially misleading about the product quantity/nature. It should be framed as \'a gift for your confidante to enjoy her own ritual,\' not a joint experience.\\n\\nAdditionally, the visual brief for Variant 2 mentions \'two champagne flutes clinking gently in the blurred background periphery.\' An image generator cannot produce \'clinking\' (an action/sound) in a static image, and \'blurred background periphery\' with specific items often results in artifacts. This is an EXECUTION issue, but since the copy also needs tightening for brand accuracy, `revise_copy` is the primary action. The copywriter must clarify the gifting angle to ensure it doesn\'t imply a shared single-use product, and then the visuals can be replanned to strictly adhere to the assigned axes if more variants are needed, or fix the prompt issues.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)\", \"Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Ritual, Reserved for You\", \"Curated Details, Elevated Style\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The body copy states: \'Slow down and savour the guided fit of your NailIt Mannequin Kit.\' This implies the Mannequin Kit is a personal, reusable possession (\'your... Kit\'). However, Company Knowledge [brand-profile.md#5-5f27cb60970364d2] and [brand-profile.md#0-883977e0a77743e2] explicitly define the Mannequin Kit as \'disposable\' and a \'self-service\' tool. Claiming ownership or implying reusability violates the product facts. \\n\\nFix for Variant 1: Rewrite the body copy to position the Mannequin Kit as a temporary application companion or tool that facilitates the ritual, rather than an object to be savored as a permanent possession. For example: \'Use the disposable Mannequin Kit to master the fit, turning a quiet afternoon into a polished, editorial moment of self-care.\'\\n\\nVariant 2 passes all tests. The setting (Pre-Event) and prop styling (Dark/Moody vs Light/Airy) provide sufficient diversity from Variant 1.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The body copy states: \'Slow down and savour the guided fit of your NailIt Mannequin Kit.\' This implies the Mannequin Kit is a personal, reusable possession (\'your... Kit\'). However, Company Knowledge [brand-profile.md#5-5f27cb60970364d2] and [brand-profile.md#0-883977e0a77743e2] explicitly define the Mannequin Kit as \'disposable\' and a \'self-service\' tool. Claiming ownership or implying reusability violates the product facts. \\n\\nFix for Variant 1: Rewrite the body copy to position the Mannequin Kit as a temporary application companion or tool that facilitates the ritual, rather than an object to be savored as a permanent possession. For example: \'Use the disposable Mannequin Kit to master the fit, turning a quiet afternoon into a polished, editorial moment of self-care.\'\\n\\nVariant 2 passes all tests. The setting (Pre-Event) and prop styling (Dark/Moody vs Light/Airy) provide sufficient diversity from Variant 1.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep)\", \"Prop styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent)\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Sunday Reset\", \"Botanical Elegance\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\' and \'Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\'. However, only two variants were produced, and they do not cover the assigned axes comprehensively or distinctly as a set.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Matte\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis entirely (it defaults to a neutral/minimalist look, not explicitly \'Office Chic\').\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Evening Glam\' and \'Glossy\', but conflates the axes. It does not provide a counterpart for \'Office Chic\' or a \'Matte Evening\' option.\\n\\nTo properly test these axes, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least two variants that clearly isolate the differences. Currently, we have one Matte variant and one Glossy/Evening variant. We are missing an \'Office Chic\' variant (which could be Matte or Glossy) and the comparison is uneven. \\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n- Rename/Refine Variant 1 to explicitly hit the \'Office Chic\' mood if it is meant to represent that axis, or keep it as \'Matte\' and add a \'Glossy Office\' variant.\\n- Create distinct variants that isolate the variables. For example:\\n  - Variant A: Matte + Office Chic\\n  - Variant B: Glossy + Office Chic\\n  - Variant C: Matte + Evening Glam\\n  - Variant D: Glossy + Evening Glam\\n  \\nSince the prompt implies a limited set of \'variants under review\' (likely expecting a smaller number like 2-4), the copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to ensure the assigned axes are actually varied. If the intention was to show just two extremes, the axes assignment in the brief was misleading. Assuming the brief\'s promise of \'Finish focus\' AND \'Styling mood\' is the goal, the current copy fails to demonstrate the \'Office Chic\' mood entirely. \\n\\nAction: Rewrite copy to include an \'Office Chic\' variant. Ensure the headlines clearly reflect the specific axis being tested (e.g., \'Office: Polished Precision\' vs \'Evening: High Shine\').\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\' and \'Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\'. However, only two variants were produced, and they do not cover the assigned axes comprehensively or distinctly as a set.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Matte\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis entirely (it defaults to a neutral/minimalist look, not explicitly \'Office Chic\').\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Evening Glam\' and \'Glossy\', but conflates the axes. It does not provide a counterpart for \'Office Chic\' or a \'Matte Evening\' option.\\n\\nTo properly test these axes, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least two variants that clearly isolate the differences. Currently, we have one Matte variant and one Glossy/Evening variant. We are missing an \'Office Chic\' variant (which could be Matte or Glossy) and the comparison is uneven. \\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n- Rename/Refine Variant 1 to explicitly hit the \'Office Chic\' mood if it is meant to represent that axis, or keep it as \'Matte\' and add a \'Glossy Office\' variant.\\n- Create distinct variants that isolate the variables. For example:\\n  - Variant A: Matte + Office Chic\\n  - Variant B: Glossy + Office Chic\\n  - Variant C: Matte + Evening Glam\\n  - Variant D: Glossy + Evening Glam\\n  \\nSince the prompt implies a limited set of \'variants under review\' (likely expecting a smaller number like 2-4), the copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to ensure the assigned axes are actually varied. If the intention was to show just two extremes, the axes assignment in the brief was misleading. Assuming the brief\'s promise of \'Finish focus\' AND \'Styling mood\' is the goal, the current copy fails to demonstrate the \'Office Chic\' mood entirely. \\n\\nAction: Rewrite copy to include an \'Office Chic\' variant. Ensure the headlines clearly reflect the specific axis being tested (e.g., \'Office: Polished Precision\' vs \'Evening: High Shine\').\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"2 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two axes of variation: Recipient Framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle) and Visual Composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the required axes effectively.\\n\\n1. Missing \'Bestie Bundle\' Variant: There is no variant that addresses the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both current variants focus on the self/personal styling.\\n2. Lack of Distinct Variation: \\n   - Variant 1 is a Hand-Held Portrait focused on Self-Gift. This is valid for one axis combination.\\n   - Variant 2 is a Flat Lay. However, its copy (\'Curated Details, Elevated Style\', \'complement your occasion styling\') is still framed around personal use/self-expression, not gifting to a bestie. It fails to utilize the \'Recipient Framing\' axis.\\n   - To pass, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least clear differentiation along the promised axes. Currently, we have one Self/Portrait and one Self/Flatlay. We are missing the \'Bestie\' framing entirely.\\n\\nAction: The copywriter must create variants that explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' audience (e.g., headlines about sharing, gifting, dual sets) to satisfy the Recipient Framing axis. Once the copy reflects the distinct audiences, the visuals can be adjusted to match the Flat Lay vs. Portrait composition requirements for each audience.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two axes of variation: Recipient Framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle) and Visual Composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the required axes effectively.\\n\\n1. Missing \'Bestie Bundle\' Variant: There is no variant that addresses the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both current variants focus on the self/personal styling.\\n2. Lack of Distinct Variation: \\n   - Variant 1 is a Hand-Held Portrait focused on Self-Gift. This is valid for one axis combination.\\n   - Variant 2 is a Flat Lay. However, its copy (\'Curated Details, Elevated Style\', \'complement your occasion styling\') is still framed around personal use/self-expression, not gifting to a bestie. It fails to utilize the \'Recipient Framing\' axis.\\n   - To pass, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least clear differentiation along the promised axes. Currently, we have one Self/Portrait and one Self/Flatlay. We are missing the \'Bestie\' framing entirely.\\n\\nAction: The copywriter must create variants that explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' audience (e.g., headlines about sharing, gifting, dual sets) to satisfy the Recipient Framing axis. Once the copy reflects the distinct audiences, the visuals can be adjusted to match the Flat Lay vs. Portrait composition requirements for each audience.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"2 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep) and Prop Styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent). However, the submitted variants do not cover these axes correctly or distinctly.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Setting: Sunday Reset\' but ignores the prop styling axis entirely (using generic linen/magazine props).\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Prop Styling: Floral Accent\' but ignores the setting axis (it is a generic beauty shot, not specifically \'Pre-Event Prep\').\\n3. Crucially, there is no variant for \'Setting: Pre-Event Prep\'. The copy for Variant 2 mentions \'occasion styling\' in the body implicitly via \'content-worthy\', but the headline \'Botanical Elegance\' and the visual brief focus on the flowers, not the event preparation context. \\n4. There is no variant for \'Prop Styling: Minimalist Tray\'. Variant 2 uses a tray, but it is styled with flowers, so it doesn\'t represent the \'Minimalist\' pole of that axis distinct from the \'Floral\' pole.\\n\\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to produce four distinct variants (or at least two that clearly represent the missing poles) that actually differ in meaning and context:\\n- Variant A: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Calm, solitary, clean lines).\\n- Variant B: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Floral Accent (Calm, solitary, soft/organic).\\n- Variant C: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Focused, sharp, ready-for-action, clean).\\n- Variant D: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Floral Accent (Glamorous, celebratory, lush).\\n\\nCurrently, we have one \'Sunday\' variant and one \'Floral\' variant. They are not comparable along the same axes, and half the promised variations are missing. The copy must be rewritten to ensure each variant explicitly targets one specific combination of the assigned axes.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep) and Prop Styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent). However, the submitted variants do not cover these axes correctly or distinctly.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Setting: Sunday Reset\' but ignores the prop styling axis entirely (using generic linen/magazine props).\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Prop Styling: Floral Accent\' but ignores the setting axis (it is a generic beauty shot, not specifically \'Pre-Event Prep\').\\n3. Crucially, there is no variant for \'Setting: Pre-Event Prep\'. The copy for Variant 2 mentions \'occasion styling\' in the body implicitly via \'content-worthy\', but the headline \'Botanical Elegance\' and the visual brief focus on the flowers, not the event preparation context. \\n4. There is no variant for \'Prop Styling: Minimalist Tray\'. Variant 2 uses a tray, but it is styled with flowers, so it doesn\'t represent the \'Minimalist\' pole of that axis distinct from the \'Floral\' pole.\\n\\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to produce four distinct variants (or at least two that clearly represent the missing poles) that actually differ in meaning and context:\\n- Variant A: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Calm, solitary, clean lines).\\n- Variant B: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Floral Accent (Calm, solitary, soft/organic).\\n- Variant C: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Focused, sharp, ready-for-action, clean).\\n- Variant D: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Floral Accent (Glamorous, celebratory, lush).\\n\\nCurrently, we have one \'Sunday\' variant and one \'Floral\' variant. They are not comparable along the same axes, and half the promised variations are missing. The copy must be rewritten to ensure each variant explicitly targets one specific combination of the assigned axes.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"2 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}]',NULL,3,6,6,2,'qwen','2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(43,15,'Merdeka 2026 Promotion Sales','render','succeeded','2026-08-28 09:44:18',824723,'6 creatives rendered — 6 flagged for you','[{\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 62}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 62\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 61}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 61\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 63}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 63\"}, {\"data\": {\"redoing\": false, \"variant_id\": 64}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rendering variant 64\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Background rendered and headline composited at top-left\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Checking the finished creative\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"renderer\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Background rendered and headline composited at top-left\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Checking the finished creative\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The image contains a prominent, unrequested rectangular overlay on the right side displaying casual daytime nail products, which contradicts the \'Evening Glam\' theme and obscures the intended subject.\", \"status\": \"flagged\"}, \"agent\": \"vision_qa\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"QA: flagged — The image contains a prominent, unrequested rectangular overlay on the right side displaying casual daytime nail pr