visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Nude Edit: Five Tones, One Mood\", \"Your Next Manicure, In Hand\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept defines two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' (Monochromatic Nude vs. Bold Occasion) and \'Visual Layout\' (Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not properly isolate or cross these axes to create distinct options.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Monochromatic Nude\' + \'Flatlay\'.\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Bold Occasion\' + \'Hand-Held\'.\\n\\nThis means we are missing the other two combinations: \'Monochromatic Nude\' + \'Hand-Held\' and \'Bold Occasion\' + \'Flatlay\'. Furthermore, Variant 2\'s copy (\'Fan out your options... five distinct styles\') is tied specifically to the hand-held visual, making it impossible to reuse for a flatlay of bold nails without rewriting. \\n\\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to provide four distinct variants (or two broader variants that can adapt to both layouts, though four is preferred for true axis testing) that cover all combinations:\\n- Variant A: Nude Palette / Flatlay\\n- Variant B: Nude Palette / Hand-Held\\n- Variant C: Bold Mix / Flatlay\\n- Variant D: Bold Mix / Hand-Held\\n\\nCurrently, the submission is incomplete regarding the promised variation axes.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept defines two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' (Monochromatic Nude vs. Bold Occasion) and \'Visual Layout\' (Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not properly isolate or cross these axes to create distinct options.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Monochromatic Nude\' + \'Flatlay\'.\\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Bold Occasion\' + \'Hand-Held\'.\\n\\nThis means we are missing the other two combinations: \'Monochromatic Nude\' + \'Hand-Held\' and \'Bold Occasion\' + \'Flatlay\'. Furthermore, Variant 2\'s copy (\'Fan out your options... five distinct styles\') is tied specifically to the hand-held visual, making it impossible to reuse for a flatlay of bold nails without rewriting. \\n\\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to provide four distinct variants (or two broader variants that can adapt to both layouts, though four is preferred for true axis testing) that cover all combinations:\\n- Variant A: Nude Palette / Flatlay\\n- Variant B: Nude Palette / Hand-Held\\n- Variant C: Bold Mix / Flatlay\\n- Variant D: Bold Mix / Hand-Held\\n\\nCurrently, the submission is incomplete regarding the promised variation axes.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix\", \"Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The two variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are effectively the same creative concept reworded.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\', but the copy and image brief describe a split composition showing BOTH colors simultaneously (\'contrast between... Hibiscus Red and... Royal Blue\'). It does not isolate one color story to contrast against the other, nor does it offer a distinct variant focused on just one. It tries to show the whole collection in one frame.\\n\\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\', but the copy and image brief include BOTH props simultaneously (\'From the intricate weave of Songket to the clean lines of marble... Songket fabric draped... on a pristine white marble surface\'). It does not isolate one styling prop to contrast against the other.\\n\\nTo pass, the variants must actually vary. For example:\\n- Variant A should feature ONLY Hibiscus Red/Gold on Songket.\\n- Variant B should feature ONLY Royal Blue/Silver on Marble.\\n\\nCurrently, both variants are cluttered \'kitchen sink\' approaches that ignore the specific variation axes requested. The copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to focus on the specific single attribute assigned to each variant (e.g., Variant 1 focuses purely on the warmth of Hibiscus, Variant 2 focuses purely on the cool elegance of Royal Blue, OR Variant 1 focuses purely on the tradition of Songket, Variant 2 on the modernity of Marble). The current copy attempts to explain the entire range in each ad, defeating the purpose of A/B testing specific aesthetic drivers.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The two variants do not differ along their assigned axes; they are effectively the same creative concept reworded.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\', but the copy and image brief describe a split composition showing BOTH colors simultaneously (\'contrast between... Hibiscus Red and... Royal Blue\'). It does not isolate one color story to contrast against the other, nor does it offer a distinct variant focused on just one. It tries to show the whole collection in one frame.\\n\\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\', but the copy and image brief include BOTH props simultaneously (\'From the intricate weave of Songket to the clean lines of marble... Songket fabric draped... on a pristine white marble surface\'). It does not isolate one styling prop to contrast against the other.\\n\\nTo pass, the variants must actually vary. For example:\\n- Variant A should feature ONLY Hibiscus Red/Gold on Songket.\\n- Variant B should feature ONLY Royal Blue/Silver on Marble.\\n\\nCurrently, both variants are cluttered \'kitchen sink\' approaches that ignore the specific variation axes requested. The copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to focus on the specific single attribute assigned to each variant (e.g., Variant 1 focuses purely on the warmth of Hibiscus, Variant 2 focuses purely on the cool elegance of Royal Blue, OR Variant 1 focuses purely on the tradition of Songket, Variant 2 on the modernity of Marble). The current copy attempts to explain the entire range in each ad, defeating the purpose of A/B testing specific aesthetic drivers.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\", \"Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Nude Edit: 5 Shades, One Mood\", \"Fan Out Your Style\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not adhere to their assigned variation axes, resulting in two distinct concepts that do not form a coherent A/B test pair for the specified dimensions.\\n\\n1. Axis Mismatch (Variant 1): Assigned axis is \'Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix\'. The copy and image correctly execute the \'Monochromatic Nude Palette\' composition. However, the Visual Layout is \'Flatlay Grid\', which was not the variable for this variant (it should have been paired with the other layout to test composition, or the layout should have been fixed while composition varied).\\n2. Axis Mismatch (Variant 2): Assigned axis is \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\'. The copy and image correctly execute the \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout. However, the Bundle Composition is \'Bold Occasion Mix\'. \\n\\nThe core failure is that the variants change BOTH composition AND layout simultaneously, rather than isolating one axis. \\n- Variant 1 is Nude + Flatlay.\\n- Variant 2 is Bold + Fan.\\n\\nTo properly test \'Bundle Composition\', we need:\\n- Variant A: Nude Palette + [Fixed Layout, e.g., Flatlay]\\n- Variant B: Bold Mix + [Same Fixed Layout, e.g., Flatlay]\\n\\nTo properly test \'Visual Layout\', we need:\\n- Variant A: [Fixed Composition, e.g., Nude] + Flatlay\\n- Variant B: [Same Fixed Composition, e.g., Nude] + Fan\\n\\nCurrently, if Variant 2 performs better, we won\'t know if it\'s because of the \'Bold\' colors or the \'Fan\' layout. The copywriter must rewrite the variants to isolate the assigned axes. If the intent was to test Composition, both variants must use the same layout. If the intent was to test Layout, both variants must use the same composition. Given the prompt lists \'Promised variation axes\' as two separate items for two variants, it implies a 2x2 matrix was intended but only two corners were produced, or the assignment was misunderstood. \\n\\nSpecific Fix:\\n- Clarify which single axis is being tested in this specific batch. \\n- If testing Composition: Keep Layout constant (e.g., Flatlay). Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the \'Bold Occasion Mix\' but described/shown in a \'Flatlay Grid\' to match Variant 1\'s layout. \\n- If testing Layout: Keep Composition constant (e.g., Nude). Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the \'Nude Palette\' but shown in a \'Hand-Held Fan Display\'.\\n\\nAdditionally, Variant 2\'s headline \'Fan Out Your Style\' is slightly casual compared to the \'quietly confident\' brand voice, but the structural diversity error is the primary blocker.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The variants do not adhere to their assigned variation axes, resulting in two distinct concepts that do not form a coherent A/B test pair for the specified dimensions.\\n\\n1. Axis Mismatch (Variant 1): Assigned axis is \'Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix\'. The copy and image correctly execute the \'Monochromatic Nude Palette\' composition. However, the Visual Layout is \'Flatlay Grid\', which was not the variable for this variant (it should have been paired with the other layout to test composition, or the layout should have been fixed while composition varied).\\n2. Axis Mismatch (Variant 2): Assigned axis is \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\'. The copy and image correctly execute the \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout. However, the Bundle Composition is \'Bold Occasion Mix\'. \\n\\nThe core failure is that the variants change BOTH composition AND layout simultaneously, rather than isolating one axis. \\n- Variant 1 is Nude + Flatlay.\\n- Variant 2 is Bold + Fan.\\n\\nTo properly test \'Bundle Composition\', we need:\\n- Variant A: Nude Palette + [Fixed Layout, e.g., Flatlay]\\n- Variant B: Bold Mix + [Same Fixed Layout, e.g., Flatlay]\\n\\nTo properly test \'Visual Layout\', we need:\\n- Variant A: [Fixed Composition, e.g., Nude] + Flatlay\\n- Variant B: [Same Fixed Composition, e.g., Nude] + Fan\\n\\nCurrently, if Variant 2 performs better, we won\'t know if it\'s because of the \'Bold\' colors or the \'Fan\' layout. The copywriter must rewrite the variants to isolate the assigned axes. If the intent was to test Composition, both variants must use the same layout. If the intent was to test Layout, both variants must use the same composition. Given the prompt lists \'Promised variation axes\' as two separate items for two variants, it implies a 2x2 matrix was intended but only two corners were produced, or the assignment was misunderstood. \\n\\nSpecific Fix:\\n- Clarify which single axis is being tested in this specific batch. \\n- If testing Composition: Keep Layout constant (e.g., Flatlay). Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the \'Bold Occasion Mix\' but described/shown in a \'Flatlay Grid\' to match Variant 1\'s layout. \\n- If testing Layout: Keep Composition constant (e.g., Nude). Rewrite Variant 2 to feature the \'Nude Palette\' but shown in a \'Hand-Held Fan Display\'.\\n\\nAdditionally, Variant 2\'s headline \'Fan Out Your Style\' is slightly casual compared to the \'quietly confident\' brand voice, but the structural diversity error is the primary blocker.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix\", \"Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The assigned variation axes are \'Setting: Morning vs. Evening\' and \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi vs. ASMR\'. However, Variant 1 is explicitly a \'Morning\' setting, while Variant 2\'s image brief describes an \'Evening\' setting (\'dimly lit\', \'evening lighting\', \'vanity mirror\'). This means both variants are varying along the SAME axis (Setting/Time of Day), ignoring the second assigned axis (Audio). Furthermore, because the copy in Variant 2 (\'The Sound of Style\') attempts to address the Audio axis but the visual contradicts it by being an Evening scene, the variants are not distinct along the promised dimensions. \\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n1. Variant 2 Copy: The headline \'The Sound of Style\' and body \'Immerse yourself in the sensory details... Let the calm guide your hands\' are weak and vague. They do not clearly differentiate from Variant 1\'s \'quiet moment\'. \\n2. Variant 2 Visuals: The brief describes an Evening scene. If the axis is truly \'Audio\', the visual should not necessarily change time of day unless tied to that specific audio vibe. However, since the prompt assigned \'Evening Vanity Prep\' to Variant 1\'s axis partner, there is confusion in the assignment. \\n\\nActually, looking closer at the prompt structure: \\nVariant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning...\'. \\nVariant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi...\'. \\nBut Variant 2\'s *image* is an Evening scene. \\nVariant 1\'s *image* is a Morning scene. \\n\\nThe core failure is that the variants do not differ along the *assigned* axes in a meaningful way that creates two distinct concepts. Variant 1 is Morning/Quiet. Variant 2 is Evening/Sensory. The \'Audio\' axis is completely ignored in the visual execution of Variant 2 (which focuses on lighting/time instead) and the copy of Variant 2 is too abstract (\'Sound of Style\') to carry the audio theme without a video component (which is the format, but the static ad review must judge the keyframe/copy pair). \\n\\nMore critically, the COPY in Variant 2 does not reflect the \'Lo-fi Beats\' or \'ASMR\' distinction clearly. It just says \'sensory details\'. \\n\\nLet\'s look at BRAND. \\nVariant 1: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' - Good. \\nVariant 2: \'The Sound of Style.\' - A bit cliché, but acceptable. \\n\\nLet\'s look at DIVERSITY again. \\nVariant 1: Morning, Quiet, Linen. \\nVariant 2: Evening, Sensory, Velvet/Dark. \\nThese are two different settings. But the assigned axes were \'Setting: Morning vs Evening\' AND \'Audio: Lo-fi vs ASMR\'. \\nIt seems the planner mixed up the axes. Variant 1 took the \'Morning\' setting. Variant 2 took the \'Evening\' setting (implied by the visual brief) but was *assigned* the Audio axis. \\n\\nIf Variant 2 is supposed to vary by AUDIO, the visual should ideally be consistent with Variant 1\'s setting (Morning) but differ in mood/audio implication, OR the copy should heavily lean into the audio aspect. \\n\\nHowever, the biggest issue is that Variant 2\'s image brief describes an EVENING scene. If the axis for Variant 2 was \'Audio\', why did the visual planner change the time of day? This suggests the planner ignored the assigned axis and just did \'Morning vs Evening\' for both, or got confused. \\n\\nBut wait, the prompt says: \'Promised variation axes: Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep, Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\'. \\nThis implies a 2x2 matrix or two separate pairs? No, there are only two variants. \\nUsually, this means Variant 1 is (Morning + Lo-fi) and Variant 2 is (Evening + ASMR)? Or Variant 1 is Morning, Variant 2 is Evening? \\nThe assignment says: \\nVariant 1 — assigned axis: Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep \\nVariant 2 — assigned axis: Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds \\n\\nThis is a malformed test setup. Variant 1 is assigned a Setting axis. Variant 2 is assigned an Audio axis. \\nVariant 1 delivers a Morning Setting. \\nVariant 2 delivers an Evening Setting (in visuals) and vague \'Sound\' copy. \\n\\nThey are different (Morning vs Evening). So they pass Diversity in terms of being different images. \\nBUT, Variant 2 was *assigned* the Audio axis. Did it deliver on the Audio axis? \\nCopy: \'The Sound of Style.\' \'Immerse yourself in the sensory details...\' \\nVisual: Dark, moody, macro shot. \\nDoes this convey \'Lo-fi\' or \'ASMR\'? It\'s ambiguous. \\n\\nLet\'s look at EXECUTION. \\nVariant 2 Text Placement: \'centered horizontally in the lower third... overlaying the darker, blurred velvet surface.\' \\nText Treatment: \'glass-panel\'. \\nImage Prompt: \'blurred glass bottles... bokeh\'. \\nIf the background is busy bokeh, a glass panel might be hard to read or look cluttered. But it\'s a valid attempt. \\n\\nLet\'s look at BRAND again. \\nVariant 2 Body: \'Let the calm guide your hands as you curate your look.\' \\nIs \'curate your look\' accurate? You are applying nails. \'Curate\' usually refers to choosing the set. The kit is for *application*. \\nCompany Knowledge: \'guided fitting kit... application companion\'. \\n\'Curate your look\' is slightly off for the application step, but acceptable in a broad sense. \\n\\nHowever, the main issue is the mismatch between Assigned Axis and Delivered Content for Variant 2. \\nAssigned: Audio Vibe. \\nDelivered: Evening Setting (Visual) + Vague Sensory Copy. \\n\\nIf I pass this, the client gets one Morning ad and one Evening ad. The \'Audio\' variation is lost. \\nIf the goal was to test Setting AND Audio, we need 4 variants or a clearer split. \\nGiven only 2 variants, and Variant 1 is \'Morning\', Variant 2 *should* have been \'Evening\' IF the axis was Setting. But Variant 2 was *assigned* Audio. \\n\\nThis is a strategic failure in the brief/assignment. \\n\\nLet\'s assume the intention was: \\nVariant 1: Morning / Lo-fi \\nVariant 2: Evening / ASMR \\n\\nVariant 1: \\nCopy: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' \\nVisual: Morning. \\nAudio: Not visible, but implied \'quiet\'. \\n\\nVariant 2: \\nCopy: \'The Sound of Style.\' \\nVisual: Evening. \\nAudio: Implied \'ASMR\' via \'sensory details\'. \\n\\nAre they distinct? Yes. \\nDo they follow the axes? Partially. \\n\\nBut look at Variant 2 Copy again. \'The Sound of Style\' is a very weak headline for a nail product. It doesn\'t say what it is. \\nVariant 1 Headline: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' -> Clear benefit + context. \\nVariant 2 Headline: \'The Sound of Style.\' -> Abstract. \\n\\nAlso, Variant 2 CTA: \'Find Your Set\'. \\nVariant 1 CTA: \'Shop the Morning Edit\'. \\n\\nThe copy in Variant 2 is significantly weaker and less on-brand (\'concise, sensory, aspirational\'). \'The Sound of Style\' is not concise or clear. \\n\\nI will flag `revise_copy` because Variant 2\'s copy is weak and doesn\'t effectively communicate the value prop or the assigned audio theme, making it a poor partner to Variant 1. The visual planner also confused the axes by changing the setting when assigned audio, but since the copy is the primary carrier of the \'Audio\' message in a static ad (or even video thumbnail), the copy needs to do more heavy lifting. \\n\\nActually, if the format is VIDEO, the audio *will* be heard. So the copy doesn\'t need to say \'Listen to this\'. \\nBut the *Headline* in the video (text overlay) still needs to be strong. \\n\'The Sound of Style\' is a bad headline for nails. \\n\\nVerdict: revise_copy. \\nNotes: Variant 2 copy is weak and abstract. \'The Sound of Style\' does not clearly connect to the product benefit (polished nails/easy application). It relies entirely on the audio track to make sense, which is risky for silent viewing or quick scanning. Rewrite Variant 2 headline to focus on the Evening/ASMR ritual benefit, e.g., \'Evening Ritual, Perfectly Applied.\' or similar, to match the visual setting and brand voice. Ensure the body copy highlights the tactile/ASMR nature of the kit more concretely than just \'sensory details\'.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The assigned variation axes are \'Setting: Morning vs. Evening\' and \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi vs. ASMR\'. However, Variant 1 is explicitly a \'Morning\' setting, while Variant 2\'s image brief describes an \'Evening\' setting (\'dimly lit\', \'evening lighting\', \'vanity mirror\'). This means both variants are varying along the SAME axis (Setting/Time of Day), ignoring the second assigned axis (Audio). Furthermore, because the copy in Variant 2 (\'The Sound of Style\') attempts to address the Audio axis but the visual contradicts it by being an Evening scene, the variants are not distinct along the promised dimensions. \\n\\nSpecific fixes required:\\n1. Variant 2 Copy: The headline \'The Sound of Style\' and body \'Immerse yourself in the sensory details... Let the calm guide your hands\' are weak and vague. They do not clearly differentiate from Variant 1\'s \'quiet moment\'. \\n2. Variant 2 Visuals: The brief describes an Evening scene. If the axis is truly \'Audio\', the visual should not necessarily change time of day unless tied to that specific audio vibe. However, since the prompt assigned \'Evening Vanity Prep\' to Variant 1\'s axis partner, there is confusion in the assignment. \\n\\nActually, looking closer at the prompt structure: \\nVariant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning...\'. \\nVariant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi...\'. \\nBut Variant 2\'s *image* is an Evening scene. \\nVariant 1\'s *image* is a Morning scene. \\n\\nThe core failure is that the variants do not differ along the *assigned* axes in a meaningful way that creates two distinct concepts. Variant 1 is Morning/Quiet. Variant 2 is Evening/Sensory. The \'Audio\' axis is completely ignored in the visual execution of Variant 2 (which focuses on lighting/time instead) and the copy of Variant 2 is too abstract (\'Sound of Style\') to carry the audio theme without a video component (which is the format, but the static ad review must judge the keyframe/copy pair). \\n\\nMore critically, the COPY in Variant 2 does not reflect the \'Lo-fi Beats\' or \'ASMR\' distinction clearly. It just says \'sensory details\'. \\n\\nLet\'s look at BRAND. \\nVariant 1: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' - Good. \\nVariant 2: \'The Sound of Style.\' - A bit cliché, but acceptable. \\n\\nLet\'s look at DIVERSITY again. \\nVariant 1: Morning, Quiet, Linen. \\nVariant 2: Evening, Sensory, Velvet/Dark. \\nThese are two different settings. But the assigned axes were \'Setting: Morning vs Evening\' AND \'Audio: Lo-fi vs ASMR\'. \\nIt seems the planner mixed up the axes. Variant 1 took the \'Morning\' setting. Variant 2 took the \'Evening\' setting (implied by the visual brief) but was *assigned* the Audio axis. \\n\\nIf Variant 2 is supposed to vary by AUDIO, the visual should ideally be consistent with Variant 1\'s setting (Morning) but differ in mood/audio implication, OR the copy should heavily lean into the audio aspect. \\n\\nHowever, the biggest issue is that Variant 2\'s image brief describes an EVENING scene. If the axis for Variant 2 was \'Audio\', why did the visual planner change the time of day? This suggests the planner ignored the assigned axis and just did \'Morning vs Evening\' for both, or got confused. \\n\\nBut wait, the prompt says: \'Promised variation axes: Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep, Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\'. \\nThis implies a 2x2 matrix or two separate pairs? No, there are only two variants. \\nUsually, this means Variant 1 is (Morning + Lo-fi) and Variant 2 is (Evening + ASMR)? Or Variant 1 is Morning, Variant 2 is Evening? \\nThe assignment says: \\nVariant 1 — assigned axis: Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep \\nVariant 2 — assigned axis: Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds \\n\\nThis is a malformed test setup. Variant 1 is assigned a Setting axis. Variant 2 is assigned an Audio axis. \\nVariant 1 delivers a Morning Setting. \\nVariant 2 delivers an Evening Setting (in visuals) and vague \'Sound\' copy. \\n\\nThey are different (Morning vs Evening). So they pass Diversity in terms of being different images. \\nBUT, Variant 2 was *assigned* the Audio axis. Did it deliver on the Audio axis? \\nCopy: \'The Sound of Style.\' \'Immerse yourself in the sensory details...\' \\nVisual: Dark, moody, macro shot. \\nDoes this convey \'Lo-fi\' or \'ASMR\'? It\'s ambiguous. \\n\\nLet\'s look at EXECUTION. \\nVariant 2 Text Placement: \'centered horizontally in the lower third... overlaying the darker, blurred velvet surface.\' \\nText Treatment: \'glass-panel\'. \\nImage Prompt: \'blurred glass bottles... bokeh\'. \\nIf the background is busy bokeh, a glass panel might be hard to read or look cluttered. But it\'s a valid attempt. \\n\\nLet\'s look at BRAND again. \\nVariant 2 Body: \'Let the calm guide your hands as you curate your look.\' \\nIs \'curate your look\' accurate? You are applying nails. \'Curate\' usually refers to choosing the set. The kit is for *application*. \\nCompany Knowledge: \'guided fitting kit... application companion\'. \\n\'Curate your look\' is slightly off for the application step, but acceptable in a broad sense. \\n\\nHowever, the main issue is the mismatch between Assigned Axis and Delivered Content for Variant 2. \\nAssigned: Audio Vibe. \\nDelivered: Evening Setting (Visual) + Vague Sensory Copy. \\n\\nIf I pass this, the client gets one Morning ad and one Evening ad. The \'Audio\' variation is lost. \\nIf the goal was to test Setting AND Audio, we need 4 variants or a clearer split. \\nGiven only 2 variants, and Variant 1 is \'Morning\', Variant 2 *should* have been \'Evening\' IF the axis was Setting. But Variant 2 was *assigned* Audio. \\n\\nThis is a strategic failure in the brief/assignment. \\n\\nLet\'s assume the intention was: \\nVariant 1: Morning / Lo-fi \\nVariant 2: Evening / ASMR \\n\\nVariant 1: \\nCopy: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' \\nVisual: Morning. \\nAudio: Not visible, but implied \'quiet\'. \\n\\nVariant 2: \\nCopy: \'The Sound of Style.\' \\nVisual: Evening. \\nAudio: Implied \'ASMR\' via \'sensory details\'. \\n\\nAre they distinct? Yes. \\nDo they follow the axes? Partially. \\n\\nBut look at Variant 2 Copy again. \'The Sound of Style\' is a very weak headline for a nail product. It doesn\'t say what it is. \\nVariant 1 Headline: \'Your Morning, Manicured.\' -> Clear benefit + context. \\nVariant 2 Headline: \'The Sound of Style.\' -> Abstract. \\n\\nAlso, Variant 2 CTA: \'Find Your Set\'. \\nVariant 1 CTA: \'Shop the Morning Edit\'. \\n\\nThe copy in Variant 2 is significantly weaker and less on-brand (\'concise, sensory, aspirational\'). \'The Sound of Style\' is not concise or clear. \\n\\nI will flag `revise_copy` because Variant 2\'s copy is weak and doesn\'t effectively communicate the value prop or the assigned audio theme, making it a poor partner to Variant 1. The visual planner also confused the axes by changing the setting when assigned audio, but since the copy is the primary carrier of the \'Audio\' message in a static ad (or even video thumbnail), the copy needs to do more heavy lifting. \\n\\nActually, if the format is VIDEO, the audio *will* be heard. So the copy doesn\'t need to say \'Listen to this\'. \\nBut the *Headline* in the video (text overlay) still needs to be strong. \\n\'The Sound of Style\' is a bad headline for nails. \\n\\nVerdict: revise_copy. \\nNotes: Variant 2 copy is weak and abstract. \'The Sound of Style\' does not clearly connect to the product benefit (polished nails/easy application). It relies entirely on the audio track to make sense, which is risky for silent viewing or quick scanning. Rewrite Variant 2 headline to focus on the Evening/ASMR ritual benefit, e.g., \'Evening Ritual, Perfectly Applied.\' or similar, to match the visual setting and brand voice. Ensure the body copy highlights the tactile/ASMR nature of the kit more concretely than just \'sensory details\'.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep\", \"Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"The Nude Edit\", \"The Occasion Mix\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Morning Light, Manicured.\", \"The Rhythm of Application.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The creative brief explicitly lists two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' AND \'Visual Layout\'. However, both Variant 1 and Variant 2 use the exact same \'Flatlay Grid\' layout. The notes for Variant 2 even admit this (\'keeping the Visual Layout... identical to Variant 1\'). This means the \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\' axis is completely ignored, resulting in only one idea tested (Composition) rather than the required matrix of variations. To fix this, the copywriter must provide variants that correspond to the missing \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout, or the team must revise the plan to include those variants. Since the current copy is tied to the flatlay concept, it needs rewriting to suit the hand-held context, making `revise_copy` the correct action to trigger a full replanning of the missing quadrants.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The creative brief explicitly lists two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' AND \'Visual Layout\'. However, both Variant 1 and Variant 2 use the exact same \'Flatlay Grid\' layout. The notes for Variant 2 even admit this (\'keeping the Visual Layout... identical to Variant 1\'). This means the \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\' axis is completely ignored, resulting in only one idea tested (Composition) rather than the required matrix of variations. To fix this, the copywriter must provide variants that correspond to the missing \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout, or the team must revise the plan to include those variants. Since the current copy is tied to the flatlay concept, it needs rewriting to suit the hand-held context, making `revise_copy` the correct action to trigger a full replanning of the missing quadrants.\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Revision budget spent after 2 passes — handing the work over flagged\"}, {\"data\": {\"passed\": false, \"revisions\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"2 variants after 2 revisions — flagged for you\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The brief promises two variation axes (Setting and Audio), but only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the requested matrix. More importantly, Variant 2 is assigned the \'Audio Vibe\' axis but the copy and visual brief fail to differentiate it meaningfully from Variant 1 in a way that stands alone as a distinct creative concept without the video component (which is the format, but the static assets must still hold up). \\n\\nHowever, the critical failure is in the COPY for Variant 2 regarding BRAND guardrails. The body copy states: \'This disposable styling tool turns your nightly routine into a focused, sensory experience.\' While \'disposable\' is correct per company knowledge, the headline \'The Rhythm of Application\' and the focus on \'clicks and taps\' leans too heavily into the mechanical/technical aspect, violating the Brand Voice which should be \'polished, feminine, fashion-editorial\' and \'quietly confident.\' It sounds overly functional compared to the aspirational tone of Variant 1.\\n\\nFurthermore, there is a logical inconsistency in the VARIANT ASSIGNMENT. Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning... vs Evening...\' but only delivers Morning. Variant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe...\' but delivers an Evening setting. This means we are missing the \'Evening Vanity Prep\' variant for the Setting axis, and the \'Lo-fi Beats\' variant is conflated with the Evening setting. \\n\\nTo fix this: \\n1. The copywriter needs to rewrite to ensure we have distinct variants that actually map to the promised axes. We need a true \'Evening\' variant that contrasts with \'Morning\' on the Setting axis. \\n2. The current Variant 2 copy (\'The Rhythm of Application\') is weak on brand voice; it feels too instructional. It needs to be more aspirational (\'Editorial\', \'Sensory\').\\n3. Because the variants are not truly different along the assigned axes (one is Morning/Setting, the other is Evening/Audio mixed), they cannot stand in for each other, but they also don\'t fulfill the brief\'s structural promise. The copy needs to be restructured to clearly separate these concepts or combine them correctly if the strategy changed. Given the prompt asks to judge against the *promised* axes, the current output is a mismatch. \\n\\nSpecific fixes:\\n- Variant 1 (Morning): Copy is good. Keep.\\n- Variant 2 (Currently labeled Audio/Evening mix): The copy is too technical (\'clicks and taps\'). Rewrite to match the \'Luxury-inspired\' voice. Also, clarify if this is the \'Evening\' setting variant or the \'Lo-fi\' audio variant. If it\'s the Evening variant, the headline should reflect the setting/mood, not just the audio. If it\'s the Audio variant, the visual brief shouldn\'t rely so heavily on the \'Evening\' lighting if the axis is purely audio (though they are linked). \\n\\nPrimary reason for revise_copy: The variants do not adequately cover the promised axes (missing a pure Evening setting variant that isn\'t conflated with audio, or missing a clear distinction), and Variant 2\'s copy drifts from the \'fashion-editorial\' voice into functional description.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The brief promises two variation axes (Setting and Audio), but only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the requested matrix. More importantly, Variant 2 is assigned the \'Audio Vibe\' axis but the copy and visual brief fail to differentiate it meaningfully from Variant 1 in a way that stands alone as a distinct creative concept without the video component (which is the format, but the static assets must still hold up). \\n\\nHowever, the critical failure is in the COPY for Variant 2 regarding BRAND guardrails. The body copy states: \'This disposable styling tool turns your nightly routine into a focused, sensory experience.\' While \'disposable\' is correct per company knowledge, the headline \'The Rhythm of Application\' and the focus on \'clicks and taps\' leans too heavily into the mechanical/technical aspect, violating the Brand Voice which should be \'polished, feminine, fashion-editorial\' and \'quietly confident.\' It sounds overly functional compared to the aspirational tone of Variant 1.\\n\\nFurthermore, there is a logical inconsistency in the VARIANT ASSIGNMENT. Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning... vs Evening...\' but only delivers Morning. Variant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe...\' but delivers an Evening setting. This means we are missing the \'Evening Vanity Prep\' variant for the Setting axis, and the \'Lo-fi Beats\' variant is conflated with the Evening setting. \\n\\nTo fix this: \\n1. The copywriter needs to rewrite to ensure we have distinct variants that actually map to the promised axes. We need a true \'Evening\' variant that contrasts with \'Morning\' on the Setting axis. \\n2. The current Variant 2 copy (\'The Rhythm of Application\') is weak on brand voice; it feels too instructional. It needs to be more aspirational (\'Editorial\', \'Sensory\').\\n3. Because the variants are not truly different along the assigned axes (one is Morning/Setting, the other is Evening/Audio mixed), they cannot stand in for each other, but they also don\'t fulfill the brief\'s structural promise. The copy needs to be restructured to clearly separate these concepts or combine them correctly if the strategy changed. Given the prompt asks to judge against the *promised* axes, the current output is a mismatch. \\n\\nSpecific fixes:\\n- Variant 1 (Morning): Copy is good. Keep.\\n- Variant 2 (Currently labeled Audio/Evening mix): The copy is too technical (\'clicks and taps\'). Rewrite to match the \'Luxury-inspired\' voice. Also, clarify if this is the \'Evening\' setting variant or the \'Lo-fi\' audio variant. If it\'s the Evening variant, the headline should reflect the setting/mood, not just the audio. If it\'s the Audio variant, the visual brief shouldn\'t rely so heavily on the \'Evening\' lighting if the axis is purely audio (though they are linked). \\n\\nPrimary reason for revise_copy: The variants do not adequately cover the promised axes (missing a pure Evening setting variant that isn\'t conflated with audio, or missing a clear distinction), and Variant 2\'s copy drifts from the \'fashion-editorial\' voice into functional description.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 2}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 2 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep\", \"Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"2 validation errors for CopySet\\nvariants.0.body\\n  Field required [type=missing, input_value={\'hook_type\': \'Color Stor...a\': \'Shop the Red Edit\'}, input_type=dict]\\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/missing\\nvariants.1.body\\n  Field required [type=missing, input_value={\'hook_type\': \'Styling Pr...: \'View the Collection\'}, input_type=dict]\\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/missing\"}]',NULL,1,2,2,2,'qwen','2026-08-28 14:28:37','2026-08-28 14:28:37'),(46,16,'Merdeka Luxury Sale','generate','succeeded','2026-08-28 14:34:47',67031,'2 variants across 1 concept — 2 flagged for you','[{\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\", \"Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\"], \"concept_id\": \"31\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “The Merdeka Edit: Editorial Still Life”\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep\", \"Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\"], \"concept_id\": \"32\"}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Crew opened on “Ritual of Independence: The At-Home Salon”\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"brand-profile.md#6-31cbcb735850e93b\", \"brand-profile.md#5-5f27cb60970364d2\", \"brand-profile.md#7-016b66b1940d0913\", \"brand-profile.md#4-8bbfdbdb7a3cf224\", \"brand-profile.md#1-012d483e108749f6\", \"brand-profile.md#3-64701f86905de9d7\", \"brand-profile.md#2-350c5ab17ecc088f\", \"brand-profile.md#0-883977e0a77743e2\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep\", \"Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"chunks\": [\"brand-profile.md#4-8bbfdbdb7a3cf224\", \"brand-profile.md#6-31cbcb735850e93b\", \"brand-profile.md#3-64701f86905de9d7\", \"brand-profile.md#1-012d483e108749f6\", \"brand-profile.md#0-883977e0a77743e2\", \"brand-profile.md#5-5f27cb60970364d2\", \"brand-profile.md#7-016b66b1940d0913\", \"brand-profile.md#2-350c5ab17ecc088f\"]}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"Retrieved 8 brand chunks as ground truth\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\", \"Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\"], \"revising\": false}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Writing 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Merdeka, Curated.\", \"The Art of Arrival.\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Your Morning Manicure Moment\", \"The Sound of Self-Care\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 image briefs planned\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Reviewing 2 pairs on brand, diversity, execution\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The two variants do not differ along the assigned axes; instead, they both mix elements from both axes, resulting in redundant concepts.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold\' but the copy and image brief describe a \'deep royal blue background\' and \'royal blue velvet\'. It ignores its assigned axis and inadvertently uses the color from Variant 2\'s axis. \\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\'. The copy focuses on Songket, and the image includes both Songket and Marble. \\n3. Crucially, there is no variant that actually utilizes the \'Royal Blue & Silver\' color story or the \'Modern Minimalist Marble\' prop as the primary differentiator. We have one variant mixing Red/Gold with Blue Velvet, and another mixing Songket with Marble. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n- Rewrite Variant 1 to strictly adhere to \'Hibiscus Red & Gold\'. Remove references to blue velvet. The image should feature red/gold nails on a neutral or complementary background (e.g., cream, soft pink, or gold dust) to highlight the red/gold story.\\n- Create/Rewrite a second variant that strictly adheres to \'Royal Blue & Silver\'. The copy should evoke cool tones, silver accents, and royal blue hues. The image should feature blue/silver nails.\\n- Alternatively, if keeping the current props, ensure one variant is purely \'Songket\' (warm/traditional) and the other is purely \'Marble\' (cool/modern), with copy reflecting those specific moods. Currently, the \'axes\' are ignored in favor of a mixed aesthetic that doesn\'t demonstrate the promised variation.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The two variants do not differ along the assigned axes; instead, they both mix elements from both axes, resulting in redundant concepts.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold\' but the copy and image brief describe a \'deep royal blue background\' and \'royal blue velvet\'. It ignores its assigned axis and inadvertently uses the color from Variant 2\'s axis. \\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\'. The copy focuses on Songket, and the image includes both Songket and Marble. \\n3. Crucially, there is no variant that actually utilizes the \'Royal Blue & Silver\' color story or the \'Modern Minimalist Marble\' prop as the primary differentiator. We have one variant mixing Red/Gold with Blue Velvet, and another mixing Songket with Marble. \\n\\nTo fix this:\\n- Rewrite Variant 1 to strictly adhere to \'Hibiscus Red & Gold\'. Remove references to blue velvet. The image should feature red/gold nails on a neutral or complementary background (e.g., cream, soft pink, or gold dust) to highlight the red/gold story.\\n- Create/Rewrite a second variant that strictly adheres to \'Royal Blue & Silver\'. The copy should evoke cool tones, silver accents, and royal blue hues. The image should feature blue/silver nails.\\n- Alternatively, if keeping the current props, ensure one variant is purely \'Songket\' (warm/traditional) and the other is purely \'Marble\' (cool/modern), with copy reflecting those specific moods. Currently, the \'axes\' are ignored in favor of a mixed aesthetic that doesn\'t demonstrate the promised variation.\"}, {\"data\": {\"target\": \"copywriter\", \"revision\": 1}, \"agent\": \"system\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Revision 1 of 2 → copywriter\"}, {\"data\": {\"axes\": [\"Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\", \"Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\"], \"revising\": true}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Rewriting 2 variants, one per axis\"}, {\"data\": {\"headlines\": [\"Merdeka in Crimson & Gold\", \"Cool Tones on Marble\"]}, \"agent\": \"copywriter\", \"phase\": \"finished\", \"detail\": \"2 variants written\"}, {\"data\": {}, \"agent\": \"visual_planner\", \"phase\": \"started\", \"detail\": \"Planning 2 images around the finished copy\"}, {\"data\": {\"notes\": \"The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes (Setting: Morning vs. Evening AND Audio: Lo-fi vs. ASMR), resulting in four expected variants, but only two were submitted. More critically, the two submitted variants do not effectively differ along the assigned axes in a way that creates distinct marketing assets.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual\'. It correctly depicts morning. However, it ignores the Audio axis entirely.\\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\'. The copy (\'The Sound of Self-Care\') and image brief focus on \'quiet confidence\' and \'ASMR-like visual quietness\'. However, the image setting is \'Evening Vanity Prep\' (moody, low-light, candle). This variant has inadvertently swapped the Setting axis instead of just varying the Audio vibe within a consistent setting, or it has conflated the axes. \\n\\nBecause the prompt asks for a set of variants based on specific axes, and these two variants are essentially \'Morning/Visual\' vs \'Evening/Visual\' with no clear differentiation or execution of the \'Audio\' axis (which is difficult to visualize but should be reflected in the copy\'s rhythm or explicit mention of the soundscape experience if it\'s a video ad), they feel like incomplete executions of a 4-variant matrix. \\n\\nHowever, the primary failure is that Variant 2\'s copy \'The Sound of Self-Care\' and \'Immerse yourself in the quiet confidence... sensory details\' is vague and doesn\'t clearly communicate the product benefit (the mannequin kit) as well as Variant 1. It leans too heavily into abstract \'vibe\' without grounding it in the \'guided fitting system\' benefit as clearly as required by the brand voice which should be \'concise\' and \'sensory\'. \\n\\nActually, looking closer at the BRAND test: Variant 2 claims \'guided fitting system... make application feel like a personal luxury ritual\'. This is supported. But the headline \'The Sound of Self-Care\' for a visual medium (even if video) is weak unless the audio is the primary hook. If this is a video ad, the copy should reflect the audio experience more concretely or the visual brief should show headphones/audio cues. \\n\\nThe biggest issue is DIVERSITY/STRUCTURE. The user asked for variants based on two axes. Usually, this means a 2x2 matrix (4 variants). Only 2 are provided. If the intention was to provide just one example per axis, they are still weak because Variant 2 doesn\'t clearly distinguish itself as an \'Audio\' variant other than using the word \'Sound\'. \\n\\nLet\'s look at EXECUTION. Variant 2\'s text placement \'mid-left... shadows provide natural contrast\' is fine. \\n\\nLet\'s look at BRAND. Variant 1: \'disposable companion\' - correct. Variant 2: \'guided fitting system\' - correct. \\n\\nThe real failure is DIVERSITY. The variants are not just reworded, they are different settings. But the assignment for Variant 2 was \'Audio Vibe\', yet the visual and copy changed the Setting to Evening. This suggests the copywriter/visual planner misunderstood the assignment or failed to produce the full set. \\n\\nIf I must choose between revise_copy and revise_visuals: The copy for Variant 2 is weaker (\'The Sound of Self-Care\' is a bit cliché and less descriptive than Variant 1). Also, if the axis is Audio, the copy should perhaps reference the *experience* of the sound (e.g., \'Snap. Click. Calm.\') rather than just \'The Sound of Self-Care\'. \\n\\nI will flag `revise_copy` because the copy needs to better reflect the specific axis it was assigned (Audio) and the set is incomplete/structurally confused regarding the axes.\", \"verdict\": \"revise_copy\"}, \"agent\": \"director\", \"phase\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"Verdict: revise_copy — The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes (Setting: Morning vs. Evening AND Audio: Lo-fi vs. ASMR), resulting in four expected variants, but only two were submitted. More critically, the two submitted variants do not effectively differ along the assigned axes in a way that creates distinct marketing assets.\\n\\n1. Variant 1 is assigned \'Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual\'. It correctly depicts morning. However, it ignores the Audio axis entirely.\\n2. Variant 2 is assigned \'Audio Vibe: Lo-fi Beats vs. Ambient ASMR Sounds\'. The copy (\'The Sound of Self-Care\') and image brief focus on \'quiet confidence\' and \'ASMR-like visual quietness\'. However, the image setting is \'Evenin