mpeting with the subject. The product tube rests low in the right foreground, anchoring the composition but never entering the text zone.\"}','flagged','The set fails on DIVERSITY and partially on BRAND. All three variants are assigned the same axis (\'aesthetic treatment\') but are not actually different in treatment — they are all golden-hour editorial portraits with the same sand-colour negative space, the same warm directional light, and the same matte product placement. The only real differences are the subject\'s ethnicity and the CTA destination, which are not aesthetic treatments. The copywriter must rewrite the three variants so each genuinely explores a different aesthetic treatment (for example: Variant 1 = editorial portrait with shallow DOF; Variant 2 = functional marketplace still-life with product as hero; Variant 3 = candid motion/lifestyle shot showing humidity in action). The current Variant 2 copy is already marketplace-functional and its visual brief is marketplace-functional, but the other two variants must be rewritten to match a genuinely different aesthetic axis. Additionally, Variant 1\'s body copy uses the word \'glow\' (\'Your glow stays yours\'), which edges close to the \'glass skin\' trend the brand explicitly rejected in Jan 2026 — change this to a humidity-first phrasing such as \'Your skin stays you\' or \'No chalky mask, just clean protection in the heat\'. Variant 2\'s headline \'2 for RM149\' is fine for a Shopee/Lazada surface but the body copy says \'Watsons shelves or delivered\' while the CTA says \'Find it at Watsons\' — pick one channel and stick to it, and ensure the price claim is accurate (RM89 each means 2 for RM149 is a real bundle, but confirm it is a current live offer). Variant 3\'s copy is the strongest and most on-brand; keep its humidity-first angle but ensure its visual brief describes a genuinely different aesthetic from Variant 1 (e.g. a candid mid-motion shot of the subject walking outdoors in golden light, wi