 Bahasa Melayu) and doesn\'t reflect the \'humidity-first\' positioning that is the brand\'s competitive claim. Variants 1 and 2 are stronger but all three variants need rewrite to: (1) avoid glass-skin/dreamy aesthetic language, (2) incorporate natural code-switching, (3) emphasize humidity-first formulation, and (4) maintain plainspoken \'older sister\' voice. Once copy is revised, visuals will need replanning to match.',2,'2026-08-26 09:25:59','2026-08-26 09:25:59'),(43,19,'time_of_day_setting','7:45 AM. No time to wait.','Rush hour waits for no one, and neither should your skincare. Embun Serum absorbs in under 30 seconds, so you can layer sunscreen and makeup without the pilling drama.','Shop the AM essential on Shopee','{\"image_prompt\": \"A close-up, high-resolution shot of a young Malaysian woman in her late 20s applying a lightweight serum to her cheek in a bright, sun-drenched bathroom. The lighting is warm and golden, mimicking early morning sunlight streaming through a window. She is wearing a simple white tank top, hair tied back loosely, looking focused and efficient. Her skin appears dewy but not oily, highlighting the fast-absorbing nature of the product. The background is softly blurred, showing hints of a tidy vanity with a sunscreen bottle nearby, suggesting the next step in her routine. The composition leaves ample empty space in the top-left corner, free of any objects or facial elements, to accommodate text overlay.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'7:45 AM. No time to wait.\' and CTA \'Shop the AM essential on Shopee\' are placed in the top-left corner.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn to the woman’s face and the texture of her skin, which looks fresh and prepped but not greasy. The negative space in the upper left quadrant provides a clean, uncluttered canvas for the headline, ensuring the urgency of \'7:45 AM\' is readable against the soft morning light without competing with facial features.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned different axes (time, commute, skin concern), but they all describe the exact same product benefit: fast absorption and matte finish. \n\nSpecifically, Variant 3 is assigned the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis but the copy (\'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\') and visual brief focus entirely on texture balance and hydration, ignoring the \'humidity-first\' positioning required by the brand. More critically, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are functionally identical in their promise (speed/matte). Variant 1 focuses on \'absorbs in 30 seconds\' and Variant 2 focuses on \'keeps you matte\'. These are not distinct enough to justify separate ad spend; they are rewording the same \'fast/lightweight\' benefit. \n\nTo fix this:\n1. Rewrite Variant 3 to strictly follow the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis by highlighting the specific mechanism of \'sebum-humidity interaction\' mentioned in Company Knowledge, rather than just generic oily/dry balance. The current copy is too generic.\n2. Ensure Variant 1 (Time) and Variant 2 (Commute) have distinctly different hooks. Variant 1 should lean heavily into the \'30-second absorption\' speed claim for the morning rush. Variant 2 should lean into the \'humidity resistance/staying power\' during the commute, perhaps referencing the \'8 in 10 felt less greasy\' stat or the specific feeling of sweat/humidity trap, rather than just repeating \'matte\'.\n\nThe visuals are generally strong and executable, but they must be replanned once the copy distinguishes the benefits clearly.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(44,19,'commute_mode','LRT air-con? More like steam room.','Stop wiping your face at KL Sentral. Our humidity-first formula keeps you matte from the platform to the office, even when the train is packed tight.','Get commute-proof skin on Lazada','{\"image_prompt\": \"A medium shot of a diverse group of commuters inside a packed KL LRT train car. The focus is on a young Malay woman wearing a tudung, standing confidently amidst the crowd. While other passengers appear slightly flushed or are fanning themselves, her skin looks fresh, matte, and composed. The lighting is slightly cool and fluorescent, typical of public transport, with a subtle haze or condensation on the windows to emphasize the humidity. The background is busy but blurred to keep the focus on her serene expression. The bottom-right area of the frame is composed of the dark fabric of her outfit or the shadowed side of the train interior, providing a solid, uncluttered zone for text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"bottom-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'LRT air-con? More like steam room.\' and CTA \'Get commute-proof skin on Lazada\' are placed in the bottom-right corner.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer’s attention is immediately captured by the contrast between the chaotic, steamy background and the subject’s calm, matte complexion. The negative space in the bottom-right corner is deliberately kept dark and uniform, allowing the white text of the headline and CTA to pop clearly without interfering with the subject’s face or the busy LRT environment.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned different axes (time, commute, skin concern), but they all describe the exact same product benefit: fast absorption and matte finish. \n\nSpecifically, Variant 3 is assigned the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis but the copy (\'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\') and visual brief focus entirely on texture balance and hydration, ignoring the \'humidity-first\' positioning required by the brand. More critically, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are functionally identical in their promise (speed/matte). Variant 1 focuses on \'absorbs in 30 seconds\' and Variant 2 focuses on \'keeps you matte\'. These are not distinct enough to justify separate ad spend; they are rewording the same \'fast/lightweight\' benefit. \n\nTo fix this:\n1. Rewrite Variant 3 to strictly follow the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis by highlighting the specific mechanism of \'sebum-humidity interaction\' mentioned in Company Knowledge, rather than just generic oily/dry balance. The current copy is too generic.\n2. Ensure Variant 1 (Time) and Variant 2 (Commute) have distinctly different hooks. Variant 1 should lean heavily into the \'30-second absorption\' speed claim for the morning rush. Variant 2 should lean into the \'humidity resistance/staying power\' during the commute, perhaps referencing the \'8 in 10 felt less greasy\' stat or the specific feeling of sweat/humidity trap, rather than just repeating \'matte\'.\n\nThe visuals are generally strong and executable, but they must be replanned once the copy distinguishes the benefits clearly.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(45,19,'skin_concern_focus','Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?','It’s not just heat; it’s humidity clogging your pores while dehydrating your skin. This niacinamide serum balances the oil without stripping the moisture barrier.','Fix the balance at Watsons','{\"image_prompt\": \"A macro, beauty-style shot of a Malaysian Chinese woman’s face, split visually to show two different skin textures. One side (her right) shows a slight, realistic sheen on the T-zone, while the other side (her left) shows smooth, hydrated skin on the cheek. She is looking directly at the camera with a neutral, trusting expression. The lighting is soft, even, and clinical-but-warm, highlighting skin texture without harsh shadows. The background is a clean, muted beige or soft grey, providing a neutral canvas that emphasizes the skin condition. The right side of the frame, particularly the mid-right area, is kept clear of facial features, offering a clean zone for the explanatory headline and CTA.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\' and CTA \'Fix the balance at Watsons\' are placed in the mid-right area.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The visual strategy uses a split-focus or dual-texture approach to visually represent the \'oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks\' dilemma. The eye lands on the center of the face where the skin texture is most visible. The mid-center placement is avoided for text to prevent obscuring the skin details; instead, the text sits in the mid-right, leveraging the negative space created by the subject’s shoulder and the plain background, ensuring the educational message is distinct from the visual demonstration.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. All three variants are assigned different axes (time, commute, skin concern), but they all describe the exact same product benefit: fast absorption and matte finish. \n\nSpecifically, Variant 3 is assigned the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis but the copy (\'Oily T-zone, thirsty cheeks?\') and visual brief focus entirely on texture balance and hydration, ignoring the \'humidity-first\' positioning required by the brand. More critically, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are functionally identical in their promise (speed/matte). Variant 1 focuses on \'absorbs in 30 seconds\' and Variant 2 focuses on \'keeps you matte\'. These are not distinct enough to justify separate ad spend; they are rewording the same \'fast/lightweight\' benefit. \n\nTo fix this:\n1. Rewrite Variant 3 to strictly follow the \'skin_concern_focus\' axis by highlighting the specific mechanism of \'sebum-humidity interaction\' mentioned in Company Knowledge, rather than just generic oily/dry balance. The current copy is too generic.\n2. Ensure Variant 1 (Time) and Variant 2 (Commute) have distinctly different hooks. Variant 1 should lean heavily into the \'30-second absorption\' speed claim for the morning rush. Variant 2 should lean into the \'humidity resistance/staying power\' during the commute, perhaps referencing the \'8 in 10 felt less greasy\' stat or the specific feeling of sweat/humidity trap, rather than just repeating \'matte\'.\n\nThe visuals are generally strong and executable, but they must be replanned once the copy distinguishes the benefits clearly.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(46,20,'comparison_angle','Winter coat in July? Tak logik.','Global brands build for dry cold, so their creams sit heavy on our humid skin. Embun is a gel-cream that disappears fast—ceramide protection without the \'berat\' feeling.','Try the gel-cream texture here','{\"image_prompt\": \"A medium shot of a young Malaysian woman standing on a sun-drenched Kuala Lumpur street corner, looking visibly uncomfortable and fanning herself with a hand. She is wearing a thick, woolly winter scarf and a heavy coat that is clearly inappropriate for the weather, creating a visual absurdity. The background shows blurred tropical greenery and bright sunlight hitting her face, emphasizing the 32°C heat. Her expression is one of mild exasperation and sweat on her forehead. The lighting is harsh natural daylight. The composition leaves the upper right corner as clear blue sky or a plain light-colored wall, free of any objects or people, to serve as negative space for text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Winter coat in July? Tak logik.\' placed in the top-right corner. Call to action \'Try the gel-cream texture here\' placed in the bottom-center, overlaid on a semi-transparent dark bar for readability against the busy street background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn to the humorous, relatable struggle of the subject in the center-left, emphasizing the discomfort of heat. The top-right quadrant is kept intentionally empty and bright (sky/wall) to accommodate the punchy headline \'Winter coat in July? Tak logik.\' without obscuring the subject\'s expression.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The copy claims \'before harga naik back to normal,\' which implies a temporary price increase or that the current RM79 is a discount off a higher standard price. Company Knowledge states the product is RM79/50ml flat; there is no mention of a standard price higher than this, nor a promo structure that involves raising prices later. This is a forbidden claim. Additionally, the tone in Variant 3 (\'Merdeka promo won’t wait\') feels breathless and salesy, violating the \'never breathless\' guardrail in the Brand Voice section. Variant 1 and 2 are acceptable in voice and factuality, but because Variant 3 requires a rewrite of the offer/hook, the whole set needs `revise_copy` to ensure the visual planner can adjust the imagery (currently tied to a specific \'grabbing/shopping\' urgency) to match the new angle.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(47,20,'visual_metaphor','Like morning dew, not thick paint.','You know how heavy some barriers feel? Ours sinks in like *embun* on grass. Light squalane hydration that lets your skin breathe in 32°C heat.','See the vanish test','{\"image_prompt\": \"An extreme close-up macro shot of a drop of clear, lightweight gel-cream landing on smooth, hydrated skin. The texture is translucent and watery, not opaque or white. Surrounding the main drop are tiny, fresh dewdrops on blades of green grass in the immediate foreground, blurring into a soft bokeh background of morning light. The lighting is soft, diffused, and ethereal, mimicking early morning golden hour. The image conveys freshness, lightness, and absorption. The top third of the frame is composed of out-of-focus green and light yellow bokeh, providing a clean, uncluttered canvas for text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-center\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Like morning dew, not thick paint.\' placed in the top-center. Call to action \'See the vanish test\' placed in the bottom-right, aligned with the edge of the skin texture.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The focal point is the macro detail of the product texture interacting with skin in the center. The metaphor of \'dew\' is visualized through high-key lighting and water droplets. The top-center area is kept clean, showing only the out-of-focus background of grass/nature, allowing the headline \'Like morning dew, not thick paint.\' to sit clearly above the action.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The copy claims \'before harga naik back to normal,\' which implies a temporary price increase or that the current RM79 is a discount off a higher standard price. Company Knowledge states the product is RM79/50ml flat; there is no mention of a standard price higher than this, nor a promo structure that involves raising prices later. This is a forbidden claim. Additionally, the tone in Variant 3 (\'Merdeka promo won’t wait\') feels breathless and salesy, violating the \'never breathless\' guardrail in the Brand Voice section. Variant 1 and 2 are acceptable in voice and factuality, but because Variant 3 requires a rewrite of the offer/hook, the whole set needs `revise_copy` to ensure the visual planner can adjust the imagery (currently tied to a specific \'grabbing/shopping\' urgency) to match the new angle.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(48,20,'cta_urgency','Merdeka promo won’t wait.','Stock up on local-made logic before the sale ends. Jom grab yours at RM79 before harga naik back to normal.','Shop before it’s gone','{\"image_prompt\": \"A dynamic, slightly angled overhead shot of a hand quickly grabbing a bottle of Embun barrier cream from a wooden table. Next to the bottle is a smartphone displaying a Shopee checkout screen with a \'RM79\' price tag clearly visible but slightly out of focus. A small Malaysian flag pin or confetti is scattered nearby to hint at the Merdeka theme without being overpowering. The lighting is bright and indoor, typical of a home setting. The composition places the hand and product in the mid-right, leaving the top-left area as a clean, light-colored wooden surface or blurred background space for the headline.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Merdeka promo won’t wait.\' placed in the top-left. Call to action \'Shop before it’s gone\' placed in the bottom-center, bold and urgent.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye lands first on the product bottle held in a hand, positioned in the mid-right, creating a sense of immediacy and \'grabbing\' action. The left side of the frame features a blurred background of a Shopee/Lazada interface or a calendar with a red circle, creating context for the sale. The top-left corner is reserved for the urgent headline \'Merdeka promo won’t wait.\' ensuring it is the first thing read before the eye moves to the product.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test. The copy claims \'before harga naik back to normal,\' which implies a temporary price increase or that the current RM79 is a discount off a higher standard price. Company Knowledge states the product is RM79/50ml flat; there is no mention of a standard price higher than this, nor a promo structure that involves raising prices later. This is a forbidden claim. Additionally, the tone in Variant 3 (\'Merdeka promo won’t wait\') feels breathless and salesy, violating the \'never breathless\' guardrail in the Brand Voice section. Variant 1 and 2 are acceptable in voice and factuality, but because Variant 3 requires a rewrite of the offer/hook, the whole set needs `revise_copy` to ensure the visual planner can adjust the imagery (currently tied to a specific \'grabbing/shopping\' urgency) to match the new angle.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(49,21,'demonstration_method','Watch it vanish.','Most creams sit on top; Embun’s gel-cream texture disappears into humidity-heavy skin. See the difference between sitting and sinking in.','Shop the Gel-Cream','{\"image_prompt\": \"Extreme macro close-up of Malaysian skin texture with visible pores, illuminated by soft, diffused natural daylight to highlight humidity without harsh shadows. A dollop of translucent white gel-cream is being gently pressed into the skin by a fingertip, captured at the exact moment the product transitions from a visible bead to a absorbed sheen. The surrounding skin area is clean and uncluttered. The lighting emphasizes the \'disappearing\' effect rather than glossiness. High resolution, shallow depth of field focusing strictly on the absorption point.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-center\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Watch it vanish.\' and CTA \'Shop the Gel-Cream\' are placed in the upper third of the frame, centered horizontally, over the blurred background of the forehead or cheek area where no product or finger is present.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn immediately to the center of the frame where the cream physically interacts with the skin. The macro lens forces focus on the texture change—from opaque white gel to transparent sheen. The negative space in the top-center is deliberately kept empty of skin texture or product residue, providing a clean, high-contrast background for the headline \'Watch it vanish.\' and the CTA.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 3 are not distinct; they both rely on the exact same \'demonstration of absorption\' concept (Variant 1: \'Watch it vanish... See the difference\'; Variant 3: \'Watch how quickly it vanishes\'). The assigned axis for Variant 3 was \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy and visual brief simply repeat the macro-absorption demo from Variant 1, merely adding a graphic layout. This is the \'one idea reworded\' failure mode.\n\nTo fix this:\n1. Keep Variant 1 (Demonstration Method) as is—it effectively shows the texture change.\n2. Keep Variant 2 (Cast Demographic) as is—it effectively targets the KL heat context.\n3. Rewrite Variant 3 completely. Since the axis is \'text_overlay_style\', the copy should lean into a bold, typographic, or comparative angle that doesn\'t require a macro skin demo to make sense. For example, focus on the \'Gel vs. Cream\' distinction using a split-screen product shot or a text-heavy kinetic typography approach, rather than repeating the \'vanishing\' claim. The current Variant 3 copy (\'We fixed the texture...\') is weak and redundant.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(50,21,'cast_demographic','Light enough for KL heat.','Formulated for Malaysian skin that battles 32°C days, this barrier cream hydrates without the heavy feel. It’s the one step that actually disappears.','Get Yours on Shopee','{\"image_prompt\": \"Medium shot of a young Malaysian woman with glowing, hydrated skin, wearing a casual modern outfit suitable for 32°C weather. She is outdoors in a bright, sun-drenched urban setting suggestive of Kuala Lumpur, with bokeh highlights indicating heat haze or sunlight filtering through leaves. She holds the Embun jar loosely in one hand near her shoulder, looking confidently at the camera. Her skin looks fresh, not oily. The background is intentionally overexposed and blurred to create a light, airy atmosphere. Natural, high-key lighting.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Light enough for KL heat.\' and CTA \'Get Yours on Shopee\' are placed in the top-right corner, overlaying the bright, blurred background sky or foliage, ensuring high legibility against the light tones.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The composition balances the human element (the subject\'s face) with the environmental context (heat/humidity). The subject looks directly at the viewer, creating a connection, while holding the product near the jawline. The negative space is created by the out-of-focus background of a KL streetscape or bright window, specifically in the top-right quadrant. This ensures the text \'Light enough for KL heat.\' sits on a bright, airy background that visually reinforces the concept of \'lightness\' and \'heat\'.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 3 are not distinct; they both rely on the exact same \'demonstration of absorption\' concept (Variant 1: \'Watch it vanish... See the difference\'; Variant 3: \'Watch how quickly it vanishes\'). The assigned axis for Variant 3 was \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy and visual brief simply repeat the macro-absorption demo from Variant 1, merely adding a graphic layout. This is the \'one idea reworded\' failure mode.\n\nTo fix this:\n1. Keep Variant 1 (Demonstration Method) as is—it effectively shows the texture change.\n2. Keep Variant 2 (Cast Demographic) as is—it effectively targets the KL heat context.\n3. Rewrite Variant 3 completely. Since the axis is \'text_overlay_style\', the copy should lean into a bold, typographic, or comparative angle that doesn\'t require a macro skin demo to make sense. For example, focus on the \'Gel vs. Cream\' distinction using a split-screen product shot or a text-heavy kinetic typography approach, rather than repeating the \'vanishing\' claim. The current Variant 3 copy (\'We fixed the texture...\') is weak and redundant.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(51,21,'text_overlay_style','Gel-Cream, Not Heavy Cream.','We fixed the texture so it absorbs fast instead of greasing up your day. Watch how quickly it vanishes into the skin.','See the Texture','{\"image_prompt\": \"Studio product photography of the Embun barrier cream jar next to a deliberate, thin smear of the gel-cream texture on a smooth, matte surface. The smear is wide and translucent, demonstrating its lightweight nature compared to a thick cream. The lighting is crisp and clinical but warm, highlighting the water-content of the gel. The background is a clean, neutral beige or soft white, completely devoid of clutter. The composition is asymmetrical, with the product and smear occupying the left half, leaving the right half entirely empty and uniform for text overlay.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Gel-Cream, Not Heavy Cream.\' and CTA \'See the Texture\' are placed in the mid-right section, utilizing the empty negative space provided by the clean background, ensuring the text is the dominant focal point in that zone.\", \"composition_notes\": \"This variant uses a split-composition or graphic-heavy approach to emphasize the \'Gel-Cream, Not Heavy Cream\' distinction. The image features a stylized product shot where the texture is smeared across a surface to show its thinness. The left side of the frame contains the product smear and jar, while the right side is kept as a solid, clean negative space (either a solid brand color or a very blurred, neutral background). This stark division allows the bold typography to sit clearly on the right without competing with visual details.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Variants 1 and 3 are not distinct; they both rely on the exact same \'demonstration of absorption\' concept (Variant 1: \'Watch it vanish... See the difference\'; Variant 3: \'Watch how quickly it vanishes\'). The assigned axis for Variant 3 was \'text_overlay_style\', but the copy and visual brief simply repeat the macro-absorption demo from Variant 1, merely adding a graphic layout. This is the \'one idea reworded\' failure mode.\n\nTo fix this:\n1. Keep Variant 1 (Demonstration Method) as is—it effectively shows the texture change.\n2. Keep Variant 2 (Cast Demographic) as is—it effectively targets the KL heat context.\n3. Rewrite Variant 3 completely. Since the axis is \'text_overlay_style\', the copy should lean into a bold, typographic, or comparative angle that doesn\'t require a macro skin demo to make sense. For example, focus on the \'Gel vs. Cream\' distinction using a split-screen product shot or a text-heavy kinetic typography approach, rather than repeating the \'vanishing\' claim. The current Variant 3 copy (\'We fixed the texture...\') is weak and redundant.',2,'2026-08-27 03:11:32','2026-08-27 03:11:32'),(52,22,'The specific \'heavy\' pain point being liberated from (e.g., sticky sunscreen layer, clogged pores under tudung, makeup sliding off)','Tudung hairline, finally breathing.','Sweat trapped under the scarf makes your skin feel heavy and sticky by noon. Our humidity-first gel-cream absorbs fast so you stay fresh, not greasy.','Shop the Kulit Sihat starter','{\"image_prompt\": \"A close-up, high-resolution shot of a young Malaysian woman wearing a soft, breathable cotton tudung in a neutral beige tone. The focus is strictly on her forehead and hairline area where the fabric meets the skin. Her skin appears dewy but matte, with visible texture that looks healthy and hydrated, not greasy. She has a subtle expression of relief, eyes slightly closed or looking down peacefully. The background is a blurred, bright indoor office setting with soft natural light coming from a window, suggesting mid-day. The lighting is soft and diffused to highlight skin texture without harsh shadows. The composition leaves significant negative space on the right side of the frame, away from her face.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-right\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Tudung hairline, finally breathing.\' and the CTA \'Shop the Kulit Sihat starter\' are placed in the empty space to the right of the subject\'s face.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the texture of the skin at the hairline, validating the \'breathing\' claim. The negative space on the right provides a clean canvas for the text, ensuring it does not overlap with the subject\'s features or the tudung fabric.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test on two counts. First, it promotes the wrong product for the stated pain point. The copy describes washing away grit and avoiding tightness (\'Wash away the grit without the tightness\'), which aligns with the Embun Gel Cleanser, but the CTA is \'Grab the bundle on Lazada\'. While the bundle contains the cleanser, the campaign theme \'The Great Unburdening\' and the other variants focus on the leave-on products (Serum/Gel-Cream) solving humidity issues. More critically, the copy violates the VOICE GUARDRAILS: \'No fear-based copy. We do not tell people their pores are shameful.\' The phrase \'feeling like your pores are clogged with heat and humidity\' leans into shame/fear rather than the brand\'s \'warm, knowledgeable older sister\' voice. It should focus on the relief of freshness, not the disgust of clogging. \n\nAdditionally, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are strong, but Variant 3 needs a complete rewrite to match the \'humidity-first\' benefit of the leave-on products (Serum or Gel-Cream) rather than pivoting to a cleanser angle, or if it must be a cleanser angle, the CTA and product claims must be strictly aligned with the Cleanser product page facts (pH 5.5, amino-acid surfactant) and the tone must be lifted from \'clogged/shameful\' to \'refreshing/reset\'. Given the mix-up in product focus across the set (V1/V2 are leave-on, V3 is wash-off), the copywriter needs to realign the product strategy for V3 or swap it for a variant that actually tests a different axis for the hero serum/gel-cream.',2,'2026-08-28 07:41:47','2026-08-28 07:41:47'),(53,22,'The visual demonstration of absorption (e.g., tissue test, water droplet bead-up, time-lapse fade)','Watch it vanish.','No white cast, no sticky residue—just a lightweight gel-cream that sinks right in. Your skin gets the barrier support it needs without the heavy feel.','See the texture on Shopee','{\"image_prompt\": \"An extreme macro shot of a dollop of translucent, blue-tinted gel-cream being applied to skin. The texture is visibly watery and lightweight, spreading easily without white streaks. A finger is gently smearing the product, showing how it thins out and disappears into the skin pores instantly. The lighting is bright and clinical but warm, emphasizing the clarity and hydration of the gel. There is no face visible, only the skin surface and the product interaction. The background is a clean, out-of-focus white or pale blue, creating a minimalist aesthetic. The left side of the frame is kept completely clear of any skin or product action.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Watch it vanish.\' and the CTA \'See the texture on Shopee\' are positioned in the clear area on the left.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The visual hook is the disappearing act of the gel, proving the \'no sticky residue\' claim. The macro perspective creates intimacy with the product texture. The left-side negative space allows the short, punchy headline to stand out without competing with the detailed texture shot.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test on two counts. First, it promotes the wrong product for the stated pain point. The copy describes washing away grit and avoiding tightness (\'Wash away the grit without the tightness\'), which aligns with the Embun Gel Cleanser, but the CTA is \'Grab the bundle on Lazada\'. While the bundle contains the cleanser, the campaign theme \'The Great Unburdening\' and the other variants focus on the leave-on products (Serum/Gel-Cream) solving humidity issues. More critically, the copy violates the VOICE GUARDRAILS: \'No fear-based copy. We do not tell people their pores are shameful.\' The phrase \'feeling like your pores are clogged with heat and humidity\' leans into shame/fear rather than the brand\'s \'warm, knowledgeable older sister\' voice. It should focus on the relief of freshness, not the disgust of clogging. \n\nAdditionally, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are strong, but Variant 3 needs a complete rewrite to match the \'humidity-first\' benefit of the leave-on products (Serum or Gel-Cream) rather than pivoting to a cleanser angle, or if it must be a cleanser angle, the CTA and product claims must be strictly aligned with the Cleanser product page facts (pH 5.5, amino-acid surfactant) and the tone must be lifted from \'clogged/shameful\' to \'refreshing/reset\'. Given the mix-up in product focus across the set (V1/V2 are leave-on, V3 is wash-off), the copywriter needs to realign the product strategy for V3 or swap it for a variant that actually tests a different axis for the hero serum/gel-cream.',2,'2026-08-28 07:41:47','2026-08-28 07:41:47'),(54,22,'The setting of the \'before\' state (e.g., office AC vs outdoor heat, post-commute sweat, evening wind-down)','Post-commute reset.','Stepping off the LRT feeling like your pores are clogged with heat and humidity? Wash away the grit without the tightness and let your skin breathe again.','Grab the bundle on Lazada','{\"image_prompt\": \"A medium shot of a diverse Malaysian woman standing on an LRT platform or just stepping off a train. She is holding a tote bag and looks slightly flushed from the heat, wiping her brow with a handkerchief or tissue. The background shows the blurred motion of a train or the busy station environment, conveying the \'post-commute\' humidity and grit. The lighting is slightly warmer and more contrasted to mimic outdoor/station artificial light, highlighting the sweat on her skin realistically but respectfully. She is looking towards the camera with a tired but relatable expression. The upper left corner of the frame is open sky or station ceiling, providing clear space.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Post-commute reset.\' and the CTA \'Grab the bundle on Lazada\' are placed in the upper left corner.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The scene establishes the \'before\' state: the physical discomfort of humidity after a commute. The eye lands on her expression and the action of wiping sweat, creating immediate empathy. The top-left zone uses the less cluttered background (ceiling/sky) to ensure the text is legible against the busier lower half of the image.\"}','flagged','Variant 3 fails the BRAND test on two counts. First, it promotes the wrong product for the stated pain point. The copy describes washing away grit and avoiding tightness (\'Wash away the grit without the tightness\'), which aligns with the Embun Gel Cleanser, but the CTA is \'Grab the bundle on Lazada\'. While the bundle contains the cleanser, the campaign theme \'The Great Unburdening\' and the other variants focus on the leave-on products (Serum/Gel-Cream) solving humidity issues. More critically, the copy violates the VOICE GUARDRAILS: \'No fear-based copy. We do not tell people their pores are shameful.\' The phrase \'feeling like your pores are clogged with heat and humidity\' leans into shame/fear rather than the brand\'s \'warm, knowledgeable older sister\' voice. It should focus on the relief of freshness, not the disgust of clogging. \n\nAdditionally, Variant 1 and Variant 2 are strong, but Variant 3 needs a complete rewrite to match the \'humidity-first\' benefit of the leave-on products (Serum or Gel-Cream) rather than pivoting to a cleanser angle, or if it must be a cleanser angle, the CTA and product claims must be strictly aligned with the Cleanser product page facts (pH 5.5, amino-acid surfactant) and the tone must be lifted from \'clogged/shameful\' to \'refreshing/reset\'. Given the mix-up in product focus across the set (V1/V2 are leave-on, V3 is wash-off), the copywriter needs to realign the product strategy for V3 or swap it for a variant that actually tests a different axis for the hero serum/gel-cream.',2,'2026-08-28 07:41:47','2026-08-28 07:41:47'),(55,25,'Occasion Pairing (e.g., Corporate Chic vs. Weekend Brunch)','From Boardroom to Bar','Transition seamlessly from corporate chic to weekend ease with a curated selection designed for your social calendar. Elevate every moment with luxury-inspired nail fashion that adapts to your mood.','Shop the Collection','{\"image_prompt\": \"A split-composition editorial shot featuring a stylish woman\'s hands in two distinct settings to illustrate versatility. On the left side, a hand rests on a sleek, dark marble desk surface next to a silver laptop and a minimalist leather notebook, showcasing nails in a sophisticated, glossy deep burgundy or classic nude shade suitable for corporate environments. On the right side, the same hand style is shown holding a ceramic coffee cup against a soft, sunlit linen tablecloth with blurred greenery in the background, showcasing nails in a playful, vibrant coral or shimmering champagne finish suitable for weekend leisure. The lighting transitions from cool, crisp office light on the left to warm, golden-hour natural light on the right. The overall mood is polished, aspirational, and seamlessly transitional, emphasizing the ease of changing styles. No text is included in the image generation.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-center\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'From Boardroom to Bar\' and the CTA \'Shop the Collection\' are placed in the upper center of the frame, hovering over the transition point between the two scenes but ensuring high contrast against the background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the contrast between the two hand settings (office vs. cafe), establishing the narrative of versatility. The top-center area is kept relatively neutral with soft focus backgrounds in both halves to ensure the white or high-contrast text remains legible without obscuring the detailed nail art or the specific props that define the occasions.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Although Variant 1 is assigned \'Occasion Pairing\' and Variant 2 is assigned \'Color Story Focus\', they are functionally identical in structure and messaging. Both variants rely on a binary \'this vs. that\' comparison (Boardroom vs. Bar; Whisper vs. Roar) and use nearly identical split-composition visuals to demonstrate duality. Variant 2 does not actually focus on the color story as a standalone aesthetic choice; it merely swaps the occasion labels for mood labels while keeping the exact same comparative framework. To pass, Variant 2 must be rewritten to focus purely on the palette (e.g., \'The Power of Neutrals\' or \'Bold Statements\') without relying on a contrasting \'other\' option in the same frame or copy. The copywriter needs to create a distinct angle for Variant 2 that celebrates a single color story\'s versatility or mood, rather than comparing two opposing ones.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(56,25,'Color Story Focus (e.g., Nudes & Neutrals vs. Bold Accents)','Whisper or Roar?','Explore the duality of our collection: serene nudes for quiet confidence and bold accents for striking self-expression. Choose the color story that defines your day.','Find Your Shade','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-fashion, close-up macro shot of two distinct sets of press-on nails displayed on elegant, matte-finish mannequin hands or stylized model fingers, arranged side-by-side to emphasize contrast. On the left, a set of serene, creamy nude nails with a subtle satin finish represents quiet confidence. On the right, a set of bold, electric blue or fiery red nails with a high-gloss, dramatic shape represents striking self-expression. The background is a split-tone studio setting: soft beige velvet on the left and deep, moody charcoal or black silk on the right. The lighting is dramatic and directional, highlighting the texture and shine of the nails while casting soft shadows that add depth. The composition is symmetrical but contrasting in color temperature and intensity, embodying the \'Whisper or Roar\' duality. No text is included in the image generation.\", \"placement_zone\": \"bottom-center\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Whisper or Roar?\' and the CTA \'Find Your Shade\' are placed in the bottom center of the frame, anchored below the visual display of the two contrasting nail sets.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer\'s eye lands immediately on the stark color contrast between the nude and bold nails in the center of the frame. The bottom-center zone utilizes the negative space provided by the dark silk/velvet background, ensuring the text is clear and does not compete with the intricate details of the nail designs above it. This layout reinforces the choice presented in the copy.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. Although Variant 1 is assigned \'Occasion Pairing\' and Variant 2 is assigned \'Color Story Focus\', they are functionally identical in structure and messaging. Both variants rely on a binary \'this vs. that\' comparison (Boardroom vs. Bar; Whisper vs. Roar) and use nearly identical split-composition visuals to demonstrate duality. Variant 2 does not actually focus on the color story as a standalone aesthetic choice; it merely swaps the occasion labels for mood labels while keeping the exact same comparative framework. To pass, Variant 2 must be rewritten to focus purely on the palette (e.g., \'The Power of Neutrals\' or \'Bold Statements\') without relying on a contrasting \'other\' option in the same frame or copy. The copywriter needs to create a distinct angle for Variant 2 that celebrates a single color story\'s versatility or mood, rather than comparing two opposing ones.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(57,26,'Setting Atmosphere (e.g., Sunlit Morning Quiet vs. Evening City Glow)','Morning Light, Quiet Mind.','Let the sun guide your hands as you shape your own style with the NailIt Mannequin Kit. This is your considered ritual, unhurried and entirely yours.','Shop the Ritual','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-key, soft-focus close-up of a woman\'s hands resting on a white linen surface, bathed in warm, diffused morning sunlight streaming through a sheer curtain. The scene features the NailIt Mannequin Kit positioned elegantly beside a cup of tea, with soft shadows creating a serene, meditative atmosphere. The lighting is natural and airy, emphasizing tranquility and the tactile quality of the skin and the mannequin\'s smooth surface. The composition is balanced but leaves significant negative space in the upper left quadrant, free of any objects or harsh shadows, to accommodate text overlay. The mood is peaceful, introspective, and luxurious.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Morning Light, Quiet Mind.\' and the CTA \'Shop the Ritual\' are placed in the upper left corner of the frame, where the sunlight creates a bright, uncluttered background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer\'s eye is first drawn to the sunlit hands and the mannequin kit in the center-right, establishing the ritualistic context. The gaze then travels naturally to the top-left negative space, which is intentionally kept bright and empty to ensure the headline and CTA are legible and harmonious with the \'morning light\' theme.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: \'Setting Atmosphere\' and \'Narrative Voiceover Tone\'. However, Variant 1 only varies the Setting (Morning/Sunlit) and Variant 2 only varies the Tone (Confident/Dark). There is no cross-variation or distinct separation that creates a robust test of these axes. More critically, Variant 2\'s copy (\'I Define My Own Shine\', \'Claim Your Independence\') leans too heavily into aggressive empowerment language, which clashes with the brand voice of \'quietly confident\' and \'luxury beauty stylist\'. The brand is about \'considered ritual\' and \'polished fashion\', not political-style declaration. \n\nSpecific fixes required:\n1. Rewrite Variant 2 Copy: Soften the tone to match \'quietly confident\'. Instead of \'Claim Your Independence\', focus on \'Self-Defined Style\' or \'Curated Confidence\'. Ensure it sounds like a fashion editor, not an activist.\n2. Clarify Variation Axes: To properly test the axes, we need variants that isolate the variables better or combine them. For example:\n   - Variant A: Morning Setting + Introspective Tone (Current V1 is close, but ensure tone is introspective).\n   - Variant B: Evening Setting + Confident Tone (Current V2 has the tone, but the setting is just \'dark background\', not explicitly \'Evening City Glow\' or similar atmospheric cue).\n   - The current V2 image prompt describes a \'charcoal or midnight blue\' background, which is generic studio lighting, not necessarily an \'Evening City Glow\' atmosphere as promised in the concept. The copy and visual need to align on the specific \'Evening\' atmosphere if that is the intended axis contrast to \'Morning\'.\n\nSince the copy in V2 is off-brand (too aggressive) and the variation strategy is weak (leading to poor diversity), the copy must be revised first. The visuals will then need to be replanned to match the new, more nuanced tones and specific atmospheric settings.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(58,26,'Narrative Voiceover Tone (e.g., Introspective Whisper vs. Confident Declaration)','I Define My Own Shine.','No appointments, no compromises—just bold independence styled on my terms. I claim this moment with NailIt.','Claim Your Independence','{\"image_prompt\": \"A bold, high-contrast medium shot of a confident woman looking directly into the camera lens with a slight, empowered smile. She is holding the NailIt Mannequin Kit in one hand like a trophy or a statement accessory, positioned at chest level. The background is a deep, rich charcoal or midnight blue, providing a dramatic contrast that makes her skin tone and the product pop. The lighting is directional and slightly harder than Variant 1, creating defined highlights on her face and the product, conveying strength and modern luxury. The right side of the frame is kept dark and devoid of distracting elements to serve as a canvas for the text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-right\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'I Define My Own Shine.\' and the CTA \'Claim Your Independence\' are overlaid on the right side of the image, aligned vertically in the mid-right section against the dark background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The subject\'s direct gaze anchors the viewer\'s attention immediately, conveying the \'Confident Declaration\' tone. The product is held prominently but not obscuring the face. The dark, empty space on the mid-right provides a strong visual counterweight to the subject on the left, ensuring the bold white text stands out sharply without competing with facial features or complex details.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: \'Setting Atmosphere\' and \'Narrative Voiceover Tone\'. However, Variant 1 only varies the Setting (Morning/Sunlit) and Variant 2 only varies the Tone (Confident/Dark). There is no cross-variation or distinct separation that creates a robust test of these axes. More critically, Variant 2\'s copy (\'I Define My Own Shine\', \'Claim Your Independence\') leans too heavily into aggressive empowerment language, which clashes with the brand voice of \'quietly confident\' and \'luxury beauty stylist\'. The brand is about \'considered ritual\' and \'polished fashion\', not political-style declaration. \n\nSpecific fixes required:\n1. Rewrite Variant 2 Copy: Soften the tone to match \'quietly confident\'. Instead of \'Claim Your Independence\', focus on \'Self-Defined Style\' or \'Curated Confidence\'. Ensure it sounds like a fashion editor, not an activist.\n2. Clarify Variation Axes: To properly test the axes, we need variants that isolate the variables better or combine them. For example:\n   - Variant A: Morning Setting + Introspective Tone (Current V1 is close, but ensure tone is introspective).\n   - Variant B: Evening Setting + Confident Tone (Current V2 has the tone, but the setting is just \'dark background\', not explicitly \'Evening City Glow\' or similar atmospheric cue).\n   - The current V2 image prompt describes a \'charcoal or midnight blue\' background, which is generic studio lighting, not necessarily an \'Evening City Glow\' atmosphere as promised in the concept. The copy and visual need to align on the specific \'Evening\' atmosphere if that is the intended axis contrast to \'Morning\'.\n\nSince the copy in V2 is off-brand (too aggressive) and the variation strategy is weak (leading to poor diversity), the copy must be revised first. The visuals will then need to be replanned to match the new, more nuanced tones and specific atmospheric settings.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(59,27,'Composition Style (e.g., Minimalist Flat Lay vs. Artistic Shadow Play)','The Art of the Flat Lay','A curated colour story arranged with editorial precision. Elevate your at-home ritual with our Buy 5 Free 5 collector’s edit.','Shop the Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-angle, minimalist flat lay photograph featuring a curated arrangement of premium press-on nail sets in soft nude and blush tones. The nails are arranged in a precise, geometric grid on a textured, matte cream paper background. To the side, a single dried pampas grass stem and a small, open velvet jewelry box add organic contrast without clutter. The lighting is soft, diffused, and even, eliminating harsh shadows to emphasize the clean lines and color harmony of the products. The composition leaves significant negative space in the top-left quadrant, keeping the area completely free of objects or textures to allow for text overlay. The mood is calm, organized, and editorially chic.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'The Art of the Flat Lay\' and the CTA \'Shop the Edit\' are placed in the empty cream space at the top-left. The body copy sits just below the headline, aligned left.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the geometric precision of the nail sets in the bottom-right and center, appreciating the color story. The gaze then travels naturally to the clear, uncluttered top-left zone where the headline resides. The negative space here is intentional and stark, providing a clean canvas that contrasts with the detailed product arrangement below.\"}','flagged','FAIL: DIVERSITY. The set fails the diversity test because there are only two variants provided, but the concept implies a 