 but the body copy claim is the main brand risk. \nAlso, the set is incomplete if the goal was to test all axes, but judging just these two: Variant 2\'s copy is the blocker.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(60,27,'Prop Styling (e.g., Silk Textiles vs. Modern Architectural Elements)','Soft Touch, Sharp Finish','Draped in silk to highlight the luxury-inspired gloss of each set. Claim your complimentary sets in this limited styling mood.','View Collection','{\"image_prompt\": \"A close-up, eye-level still life shot focusing on the tactile interplay between luxury fabrics and glossy nail tips. A piece of champagne-colored silk satin is draped loosely across a smooth, white marble surface, creating soft, flowing folds. Resting atop the silk is a single NailIt press-on set, its glossy finish catching the light. The lighting is warm and directional, creating gentle highlights on the silk\'s weave and the nails\' curvature, evoking a sense of touch and comfort. The right side of the frame is dominated by the soft, out-of-focus folds of the silk, while the left side features the sharper focus of the product and a patch of smooth marble. The top-right area is kept visually quiet with blurred, light-toned fabric to accommodate text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-right\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Soft Touch, Sharp Finish\' and the CTA \'View Collection\' are positioned in the upper-right quadrant. The body copy is placed directly beneath the headline, ensuring readability against the lighter, blurred silk background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer\'s attention is initially captured by the sharp detail of the nail set resting on the soft silk in the mid-left area, emphasizing the texture contrast. The eye then moves to the top-right, where the softer focus and lighter tones of the draped silk provide a natural, non-distracting background for the headline. This layout balances the sensory appeal of the props with clear communication space.\"}','flagged','FAIL: DIVERSITY. The set fails the diversity test because there are only two variants provided, but the concept implies a matrix of variations (Composition Style AND Prop Styling). More critically, the two variants do not sufficiently differentiate along their assigned axes to justify separate ad spend; they feel like minor tweaks of the same \'editorial still life\' idea rather than distinct creative directions. \n\nFAIL: BRAND/CLAIMS. Variant 2 Body Copy states: \'Claim your complimentary sets in this limited styling mood.\' This is misleading and potentially violates claim guardrails. The offer is \'Buy 5 Free 5\', not \'complimentary sets\' generally available or unconditionally free. \'Complimentary\' implies no purchase necessary or a gift with no strings, whereas \'Free 5\' is contingent on buying 5. This phrasing is ambiguous and could be seen as deceptive. It should clearly reflect the \'Buy 5 Free 5\' mechanic or use approved language like \'collector’s edit\' as seen in Variant 1.\n\nFIX: \n1. Rewrite Variant 2 body copy to accurately reflect the \'Buy 5 Free 5\' offer without using misleading \'complimentary\' language that implies unconditional freebies. \n2. To pass the Diversity test, we need distinct variants. Since only two are shown, ensure they are radically different. Variant 1 is \'Minimalist Flat Lay\'. Variant 2 is \'Silk Textiles\'. These are different props, but the composition description for Variant 2 is weak on the \'Architectural\' contrast promised in the axis definition. However, the primary failure is the copy claim in Variant 2 and the lack of clear distinction in value proposition between the two. \n3. Actually, looking closer at the prompt\'s variation axes: \'Composition Style (e.g., Minimalist Flat Lay vs. Artistic Shadow Play)\' and \'Prop Styling (e.g., Silk Textiles vs. Modern Architectural Elements)\'. \nVariant 1 uses \'Minimalist Flat Lay\' (Composition) and \'Pampas grass/Velvet\' (Props). \nVariant 2 uses \'Close-up/Eye-level\' (Composition) and \'Silk\' (Props). \nThe issue is that Variant 2\'s headline \'Soft Touch, Sharp Finish\' is good, but the body copy claim is the main brand risk. \nAlso, the set is incomplete if the goal was to test all axes, but judging just these two: Variant 2\'s copy is the blocker.',2,'2026-08-28 09:10:36','2026-08-28 09:10:36'),(61,28,'Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)','Matte: Quiet Luxury','A soft, non-reflective finish that feels modern and understated. Let the colour story speak without the shine.','Shop the Matte Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A hyper-realistic, high-fashion editorial close-up of a woman\'s hand resting gently on a textured, raw beige linen surface. The nails feature a sophisticated, deep taupe matte press-on set with zero reflection, emphasizing the soft, light-absorbing finish. The lighting is soft, diffused, and natural, creating gentle shadows that highlight the texture of the skin and the fabric without creating any specular highlights on the nails. The composition is minimalist and airy, with the hand positioned in the lower-right quadrant, leaving the upper-left area completely clear and uncluttered for text overlay. The mood is calm, understated, and quietly luxurious.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Matte: Quiet Luxury\' and CTA \'Shop the Matte Edit\' are placed in the upper-left corner against the neutral linen background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer\'s eye is drawn first to the tactile contrast between the smooth matte nails and the rough linen texture in the lower right. The negative space in the top left is intentionally kept empty and uniform in tone to ensure the headline is legible and feels part of the editorial layout.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\' and \'Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\'. However, only two variants were produced, and they do not cover the assigned axes comprehensively or distinctly as a set.\n\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Matte\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis entirely (it defaults to a neutral/minimalist look, not explicitly \'Office Chic\').\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Evening Glam\' and \'Glossy\', but conflates the axes. It does not provide a counterpart for \'Office Chic\' or a \'Matte Evening\' option.\n\nTo properly test these axes, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least two variants that clearly isolate the differences. Currently, we have one Matte variant and one Glossy/Evening variant. We are missing an \'Office Chic\' variant (which could be Matte or Glossy) and the comparison is uneven. \n\nSpecific fixes required:\n- Rename/Refine Variant 1 to explicitly hit the \'Office Chic\' mood if it is meant to represent that axis, or keep it as \'Matte\' and add a \'Glossy Office\' variant.\n- Create distinct variants that isolate the variables. For example:\n  - Variant A: Matte + Office Chic\n  - Variant B: Glossy + Office Chic\n  - Variant C: Matte + Evening Glam\n  - Variant D: Glossy + Evening Glam\n  \nSince the prompt implies a limited set of \'variants under review\' (likely expecting a smaller number like 2-4), the copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to ensure the assigned axes are actually varied. If the intention was to show just two extremes, the axes assignment in the brief was misleading. Assuming the brief\'s promise of \'Finish focus\' AND \'Styling mood\' is the goal, the current copy fails to demonstrate the \'Office Chic\' mood entirely. \n\nAction: Rewrite copy to include an \'Office Chic\' variant. Ensure the headlines clearly reflect the specific axis being tested (e.g., \'Office: Polished Precision\' vs \'Evening: High Shine\').',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(62,28,'Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)','Evening: High Shine','Maximum gloss for a look that catches the light from dusk till dawn. Elevate your night-out styling with this curated set.','Shop Evening Glam','{\"image_prompt\": \"A glamorous, low-light editorial shot of a woman\'s hand holding a sleek, black satin clutch or a crystal champagne flute. The nails feature a high-shine, glossy burgundy or deep red press-on set that catches and reflects ambient city lights or candlelight, creating sharp, elegant specular highlights. The background is dark and moody, with bokeh effects of distant evening lights to suggest a night-out setting. The hand is positioned in the lower-right, angled to maximize the reflection on the nails. The upper-left area is dark and out of focus, providing a clean, high-contrast zone for white text overlay. The mood is sophisticated, vibrant, and celebratory.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Evening: High Shine\' and CTA \'Shop Evening Glam\' are placed in the upper-left corner against the dark, blurred background.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The focal point is the gleam on the glossy nails in the lower right, which communicates the \'High Shine\' axis immediately. The dark, blurred background in the top left provides maximum contrast for the white headline, ensuring readability while maintaining the evening glamour aesthetic.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two variation axes: \'Finish focus (Matte vs. Glossy)\' and \'Styling mood (Office Chic vs. Evening Glam)\'. However, only two variants were produced, and they do not cover the assigned axes comprehensively or distinctly as a set.\n\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Matte\' but ignores the \'Styling mood\' axis entirely (it defaults to a neutral/minimalist look, not explicitly \'Office Chic\').\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Evening Glam\' and \'Glossy\', but conflates the axes. It does not provide a counterpart for \'Office Chic\' or a \'Matte Evening\' option.\n\nTo properly test these axes, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least two variants that clearly isolate the differences. Currently, we have one Matte variant and one Glossy/Evening variant. We are missing an \'Office Chic\' variant (which could be Matte or Glossy) and the comparison is uneven. \n\nSpecific fixes required:\n- Rename/Refine Variant 1 to explicitly hit the \'Office Chic\' mood if it is meant to represent that axis, or keep it as \'Matte\' and add a \'Glossy Office\' variant.\n- Create distinct variants that isolate the variables. For example:\n  - Variant A: Matte + Office Chic\n  - Variant B: Glossy + Office Chic\n  - Variant C: Matte + Evening Glam\n  - Variant D: Glossy + Evening Glam\n  \nSince the prompt implies a limited set of \'variants under review\' (likely expecting a smaller number like 2-4), the copywriter must rewrite the headlines and body copy to ensure the assigned axes are actually varied. If the intention was to show just two extremes, the axes assignment in the brief was misleading. Assuming the brief\'s promise of \'Finish focus\' AND \'Styling mood\' is the goal, the current copy fails to demonstrate the \'Office Chic\' mood entirely. \n\nAction: Rewrite copy to include an \'Office Chic\' variant. Ensure the headlines clearly reflect the specific axis being tested (e.g., \'Office: Polished Precision\' vs \'Evening: High Shine\').',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(63,29,'Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep)','The Sunday Reset','Transform a quiet afternoon into a polished, editorial moment of self-care with the disposable Mannequin Kit as your temporary application companion. This guided ritual turns simple press-ons into a curated expression of calm.','Shop the Ritual','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-angle, editorial-style flat lay photograph set on a textured, cream-colored linen tablecloth bathed in soft, natural Sunday morning light. In the center-right, a disposable beige plastic mannequin hand rests gently next to an open box of curated press-on nails, positioned as a temporary tool rather than a permanent accessory. To the left, a ceramic mug of tea and an open lifestyle magazine create a sense of quiet leisure. The lighting is bright, airy, and diffuse, evoking calm and cleanliness. The composition leaves the top-left quadrant completely empty and uncluttered, with no objects overlapping into that space, ensuring a clean canvas for text overlay.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'The Sunday Reset\' and the CTA \'Shop the Ritual\' are placed in the upper left corner.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the soft textures of the linen and the neutral tones of the mannequin hand and nail kit in the center-right. The negative space in the top-left provides a calm, uncluttered area for the headline, reinforcing the \'quiet afternoon\' mood described in the copy. The visual hierarchy moves from the text (context) to the product setup (action).\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep) and Prop Styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent). However, the submitted variants do not cover these axes correctly or distinctly.\n\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Setting: Sunday Reset\' but ignores the prop styling axis entirely (using generic linen/magazine props).\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Prop Styling: Floral Accent\' but ignores the setting axis (it is a generic beauty shot, not specifically \'Pre-Event Prep\').\n3. Crucially, there is no variant for \'Setting: Pre-Event Prep\'. The copy for Variant 2 mentions \'occasion styling\' in the body implicitly via \'content-worthy\', but the headline \'Botanical Elegance\' and the visual brief focus on the flowers, not the event preparation context. \n4. There is no variant for \'Prop Styling: Minimalist Tray\'. Variant 2 uses a tray, but it is styled with flowers, so it doesn\'t represent the \'Minimalist\' pole of that axis distinct from the \'Floral\' pole.\n\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to produce four distinct variants (or at least two that clearly represent the missing poles) that actually differ in meaning and context:\n- Variant A: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Calm, solitary, clean lines).\n- Variant B: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Floral Accent (Calm, solitary, soft/organic).\n- Variant C: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Focused, sharp, ready-for-action, clean).\n- Variant D: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Floral Accent (Glamorous, celebratory, lush).\n\nCurrently, we have one \'Sunday\' variant and one \'Floral\' variant. They are not comparable along the same axes, and half the promised variations are missing. The copy must be rewritten to ensure each variant explicitly targets one specific combination of the assigned axes.',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(64,29,'Prop styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent)','Botanical Elegance','Frame your at-home manicure with fresh blooms and the guided precision of the disposable Mannequin Kit. It is the perfect styling tool for a content-worthy beauty ritual that feels both luxurious and intentional.','View Sets','{\"image_prompt\": \"A close-up, shallow depth-of-field shot featuring a minimalist white marble tray holding the disposable mannequin hand and a set of glossy press-on nails. Fresh, delicate white jasmine or orchid blooms are artfully scattered around the tray, adding a touch of organic luxury and botanical elegance. The background is a soft, out-of-focus pale sage green, creating a fresh and intentional aesthetic. The lighting is crisp and high-key, highlighting the sheen of the nails and the smooth texture of the disposable kit. The bottom-center area of the frame is kept clear of flowers and product edges, providing a smooth, blurred background zone for text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"bottom-center\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Botanical Elegance\' and the CTA \'View Sets\' are placed at the bottom center.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The focal point is the interplay between the structured geometry of the tray/mannequin and the organic curves of the flowers in the mid-ground. The eye lands on the product styling first, validating the \'content-worthy beauty ritual\' claim. The bottom-center placement zone utilizes the blurred background space, ensuring the text is legible without obscuring the detailed prop styling above it.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises variation along two axes: Setting (Sunday Reset vs. Pre-Event Prep) and Prop Styling (Minimalist Tray vs. Floral Accent). However, the submitted variants do not cover these axes correctly or distinctly.\n\n1. Variant 1 covers \'Setting: Sunday Reset\' but ignores the prop styling axis entirely (using generic linen/magazine props).\n2. Variant 2 covers \'Prop Styling: Floral Accent\' but ignores the setting axis (it is a generic beauty shot, not specifically \'Pre-Event Prep\').\n3. Crucially, there is no variant for \'Setting: Pre-Event Prep\'. The copy for Variant 2 mentions \'occasion styling\' in the body implicitly via \'content-worthy\', but the headline \'Botanical Elegance\' and the visual brief focus on the flowers, not the event preparation context. \n4. There is no variant for \'Prop Styling: Minimalist Tray\'. Variant 2 uses a tray, but it is styled with flowers, so it doesn\'t represent the \'Minimalist\' pole of that axis distinct from the \'Floral\' pole.\n\nTo fix this, the copywriter needs to produce four distinct variants (or at least two that clearly represent the missing poles) that actually differ in meaning and context:\n- Variant A: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Calm, solitary, clean lines).\n- Variant B: Setting = Sunday Reset, Prop = Floral Accent (Calm, solitary, soft/organic).\n- Variant C: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Minimalist Tray (Focused, sharp, ready-for-action, clean).\n- Variant D: Setting = Pre-Event Prep, Prop = Floral Accent (Glamorous, celebratory, lush).\n\nCurrently, we have one \'Sunday\' variant and one \'Floral\' variant. They are not comparable along the same axes, and half the promised variations are missing. The copy must be rewritten to ensure each variant explicitly targets one specific combination of the assigned axes.',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(65,30,'Recipient framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle)','The Ritual, Reserved for You','Indulge in a curated moment of luxury-inspired nail fashion, designed entirely for your personal styling pleasure. Elevate your at-home beauty routine with a set that celebrates self-expression.','Shop the Self-Care Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A serene, high-key lifestyle portrait of a young woman with glowing skin and minimal makeup, seated in a sun-drenched, minimalist bedroom. She is holding her hands up near her face, admiring a fresh, glossy manicure with neutral-toned press-on nails. The lighting is soft and natural, creating a dreamy, ethereal mood. The background is intentionally blurred and uncluttered, featuring soft white linens and a hint of a silk robe, ensuring the upper left quadrant remains empty and out of focus to accommodate text overlay. The composition focuses on the subject\'s peaceful expression and the detailed finish of the nails, conveying a sense of private luxury and self-care ritual.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'The Ritual, Reserved for You\' and CTA \'Shop the Self-Care Edit\' are placed in the top-left corner.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The viewer\'s eye is drawn first to the woman\'s face and hands in the center-right, establishing the emotional connection to self-care. The top-left area is deliberately kept as negative space (blurred background/linens) to ensure the headline and CTA are legible without competing with the subject\'s features.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two axes of variation: Recipient Framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle) and Visual Composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the required axes effectively.\n\n1. Missing \'Bestie Bundle\' Variant: There is no variant that addresses the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both current variants focus on the self/personal styling.\n2. Lack of Distinct Variation: \n   - Variant 1 is a Hand-Held Portrait focused on Self-Gift. This is valid for one axis combination.\n   - Variant 2 is a Flat Lay. However, its copy (\'Curated Details, Elevated Style\', \'complement your occasion styling\') is still framed around personal use/self-expression, not gifting to a bestie. It fails to utilize the \'Recipient Framing\' axis.\n   - To pass, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least clear differentiation along the promised axes. Currently, we have one Self/Portrait and one Self/Flatlay. We are missing the \'Bestie\' framing entirely.\n\nAction: The copywriter must create variants that explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' audience (e.g., headlines about sharing, gifting, dual sets) to satisfy the Recipient Framing axis. Once the copy reflects the distinct audiences, the visuals can be adjusted to match the Flat Lay vs. Portrait composition requirements for each audience.',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(66,30,'Visual composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait)','Curated Details, Elevated Style','Discover the artistry in every finish with our signature press-on sets, arranged for your visual inspiration. A polished look awaits, ready to complement your occasion styling.','View the Collection','{\"image_prompt\": \"A sophisticated, editorial-style flat lay photograph taken from directly above. The composition features a curated arrangement of NailIt press-on nail sets in their premium packaging, arranged artfully alongside fashion accessories such as a gold bracelet, a silk scarf, and a small vase with a single stem of dried flowers. The surface is a textured marble or stone table in neutral tones. Lighting is bright and even, highlighting the textures and finishes of the products. The right side of the frame is left relatively open and uncluttered, with fewer objects, to provide a clean canvas for text overlay. The mood is polished, organized, and aspirational, emphasizing the \'curated detail\' aspect of the brand.\", \"placement_zone\": \"mid-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Curated Details, Elevated Style\' and CTA \'View the Collection\' are placed in the mid-right area.\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye lands first on the central cluster of products and accessories, appreciating the styling and color story. The mid-right zone is cleared of major objects, using the negative space of the marble surface to host the text. This ensures the copy does not obscure the product details while maintaining a balanced, magazine-edit aesthetic.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The concept promises two axes of variation: Recipient Framing (Self-Gift vs. Bestie Bundle) and Visual Composition (Flat Lay vs. Hand-Held Portrait). However, only two variants were submitted, and they do not cover the required axes effectively.\n\n1. Missing \'Bestie Bundle\' Variant: There is no variant that addresses the \'Bestie Bundle\' recipient framing. Both current variants focus on the self/personal styling.\n2. Lack of Distinct Variation: \n   - Variant 1 is a Hand-Held Portrait focused on Self-Gift. This is valid for one axis combination.\n   - Variant 2 is a Flat Lay. However, its copy (\'Curated Details, Elevated Style\', \'complement your occasion styling\') is still framed around personal use/self-expression, not gifting to a bestie. It fails to utilize the \'Recipient Framing\' axis.\n   - To pass, we need four variants (2x2 matrix) or at least clear differentiation along the promised axes. Currently, we have one Self/Portrait and one Self/Flatlay. We are missing the \'Bestie\' framing entirely.\n\nAction: The copywriter must create variants that explicitly target the \'Bestie Bundle\' audience (e.g., headlines about sharing, gifting, dual sets) to satisfy the Recipient Framing axis. Once the copy reflects the distinct audiences, the visuals can be adjusted to match the Flat Lay vs. Portrait composition requirements for each audience.',2,'2026-08-28 09:42:51','2026-08-28 09:42:51'),(67,33,'Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix','The Nude Edit','Five curated sets in a seamless monochromatic palette for an understated, luxury-inspired look. Perfect for gifting or elevating your daily beauty ritual with quiet confidence.','Shop the Nude Bundle','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-end, minimalist flat-lay composition on a smooth, warm beige stone surface. Five distinct press-on nail sets are arranged in a precise, symmetrical grid pattern. The nails feature a monochromatic nude palette ranging from pale ivory to deep taupe, creating a seamless gradient effect. Soft, diffused natural light comes from the top left, casting gentle, soft shadows that emphasize the texture of the packaging without cluttering the scene. The mood is serene, luxurious, and organized. The bottom-right quadrant of the image is intentionally left as empty negative space on the stone surface, free of any product or shadow, to accommodate text overlay.\", \"placement_zone\": \"bottom-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'The Nude Edit\' and CTA \'Shop the Nude Bundle\' are placed in the bottom-right corner.\", \"text_treatment\": \"bare\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the structured grid of nude tones in the center-left, establishing the \'Monochromatic Nude Palette\' axis. The layout is a strict Flatlay Grid to isolate the composition variable. The bottom-right area is kept completely clear of product to allow the headline and CTA to sit directly on the stone texture. A \'bare\' text treatment with a subtle outline is chosen to maintain the understated, quiet confidence of the brand voice, avoiding heavy graphical elements that would distract from the luxury aesthetic.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The creative brief explicitly lists two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' AND \'Visual Layout\'. However, both Variant 1 and Variant 2 use the exact same \'Flatlay Grid\' layout. The notes for Variant 2 even admit this (\'keeping the Visual Layout... identical to Variant 1\'). This means the \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\' axis is completely ignored, resulting in only one idea tested (Composition) rather than the required matrix of variations. To fix this, the copywriter must provide variants that correspond to the missing \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout, or the team must revise the plan to include those variants. Since the current copy is tied to the flatlay concept, it needs rewriting to suit the hand-held context, making `revise_copy` the correct action to trigger a full replanning of the missing quadrants.',2,'2026-08-28 14:28:37','2026-08-28 14:28:37'),(68,33,'Bundle Composition: Monochromatic Nude Palette vs. Bold Occasion Mix','The Occasion Mix','A bold curation of five statement finishes designed to match your most vibrant social calendar. Embrace high-fashion self-expression with this complete wardrobe accessory kit.','Shop the Bold Bundle','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-end, minimalist flat-lay composition on the same smooth, warm beige stone surface as Variant 1 to ensure layout consistency. Five distinct press-on nail sets are arranged in the same precise, symmetrical grid pattern. However, these nails feature a bold, high-contrast occasion mix: vibrant emerald green, deep ruby red, metallic gold, electric blue, and glossy black. The lighting remains soft and diffused from the top left to match Variant 1 exactly. The mood is vibrant yet controlled and editorial. The bottom-right quadrant of the image is intentionally left as empty negative space on the stone surface, free of any product or shadow, to accommodate text overlay.\", \"placement_zone\": \"bottom-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'The Occasion Mix\' and CTA \'Shop the Bold Bundle\' are placed in the bottom-right corner.\", \"text_treatment\": \"soft-gradient\", \"composition_notes\": \"This variant isolates the \'Bundle Composition\' axis by keeping the Visual Layout (Flatlay Grid) identical to Variant 1. The eye lands on the striking contrast of the bold colors against the neutral stone, immediately communicating the \'Bold Occasion Mix\'. The text sits in the same bottom-right negative space as Variant 1. A \'soft-gradient\' text treatment is used here instead of \'bare\' to provide slightly more contrast against the potentially busier visual weight of the bold colors nearby, ensuring legibility while maintaining the editorial feel. This allows for a clean A/B test of composition preference.\"}','flagged','The set fails the DIVERSITY test. The creative brief explicitly lists two variation axes: \'Bundle Composition\' AND \'Visual Layout\'. However, both Variant 1 and Variant 2 use the exact same \'Flatlay Grid\' layout. The notes for Variant 2 even admit this (\'keeping the Visual Layout... identical to Variant 1\'). This means the \'Visual Layout: Flatlay Grid vs. Hand-Held Fan Display\' axis is completely ignored, resulting in only one idea tested (Composition) rather than the required matrix of variations. To fix this, the copywriter must provide variants that correspond to the missing \'Hand-Held Fan Display\' layout, or the team must revise the plan to include those variants. Since the current copy is tied to the flatlay concept, it needs rewriting to suit the hand-held context, making `revise_copy` the correct action to trigger a full replanning of the missing quadrants.',2,'2026-08-28 14:28:37','2026-08-28 14:28:37'),(69,31,'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver','Merdeka in Crimson & Gold','A bold hibiscus red paired with metallic gold accents captures the warmth of the season. Style this curated set for a polished, editorial look that celebrates local pride through quiet confidence.','Shop The Merdeka Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-fashion editorial still life featuring a set of press-on nails in deep hibiscus red with metallic gold accents, arranged artfully on a rich, textured traditional Songket fabric. The Songket should display intricate gold-thread weaving patterns typical of Malaysian heritage textiles, providing a luxurious and culturally significant backdrop. Soft, warm directional lighting highlights the metallic sheen of the nails and the tactile depth of the woven fabric. The composition is tightly cropped to emphasize texture and color contrast, with the upper right quadrant left intentionally empty and out of focus to accommodate text overlay. The mood is proud, warm, and sophisticated.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-right\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Merdeka in Crimson & Gold\' and CTA \'Shop The Merdeka Edit\' are placed in the upper right corner.\", \"text_treatment\": \"glass-panel\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the vibrant red nails against the complex gold weave of the Songket. The text sits in the blurred, negative space of the upper right, ensuring legibility without obscuring the detailed textile pattern. A glass-panel treatment is chosen to add a modern, polished layer that contrasts with the traditional texture underneath, reinforcing the \'editorial\' aesthetic while protecting the text from the busy background.\"}','flagged','The variants fail the DIVERSITY test because they do not adhere to the assigned variation axes. \n\n1. Variant 1 was assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\' but describes Red/Gold nails on Songket. It ignores the \'vs\' component entirely.\n2. Variant 2 was assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\' but describes Blue/Silver nails on Marble. It ignores the \'vs\' component entirely.\n3. Crucially, the variants mix up the axes. Variant 1 uses the Songket prop (from Axis 2) and Variant 2 uses the Blue/Silver color (from Axis 1). This results in two completely different concepts (Red/Songket vs Blue/Marble) rather than a controlled test of specific axes. \n\nTo fix this:\n- If testing Color Story: Keep the prop constant (e.g., Marble) and show Red/Gold in one variant and Blue/Silver in the other.\n- If testing Styling Prop: Keep the color constant (e.g., Red/Gold) and show Songket in one variant and Marble in the other.\n\nAdditionally, Variant 2\'s headline \'Heritage Woven\' contradicts the visual brief of \'Modern Minimalist Marble\' which has no weaving. The copy must match the specific visual it is paired with.',2,'2026-08-28 14:35:53','2026-08-28 14:35:53'),(70,31,'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble','Heritage Woven, Modernly Worn','Resting against traditional Songket fabric, these press-on nails bridge heritage and contemporary style. Elevate your at-home beauty ritual with a manicure that honors texture and detail.','View The Collection','{\"image_prompt\": \"A minimalist, cool-toned still life featuring a set of press-on nails in royal blue with silver chrome finishes, resting on a sleek, white modern marble surface with subtle grey veining. The lighting is crisp and bright, creating sharp, clean reflections on the silver nail accents and the polished stone. The composition uses strong geometric lines, with the nails positioned in the lower left third, leaving the upper left area as clean, uncluttered white marble space. The mood is contemporary, chic, and serene.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"Headline \'Heritage Woven, Modernly Worn\' and CTA \'View The Collection\' are placed in the upper left corner.\", \"text_treatment\": \"bare\", \"composition_notes\": \"The visual weight is anchored in the bottom left by the blue/silver nails, allowing the eye to travel up to the clean marble expanse. The text occupies this negative space in the top left. Since the background is a uniform, light marble with low visual noise, a \'bare\' text treatment with a subtle outline is sufficient for legibility and maintains the airy, minimalist vibe requested by the \'Modern Minimalist\' axis. This avoids unnecessary graphical elements, keeping the look high-end and uncluttered.\"}','flagged','The variants fail the DIVERSITY test because they do not adhere to the assigned variation axes. \n\n1. Variant 1 was assigned \'Color Story: Hibiscus Red & Gold vs. Royal Blue & Silver\' but describes Red/Gold nails on Songket. It ignores the \'vs\' component entirely.\n2. Variant 2 was assigned \'Styling Prop: Traditional Songket Fabric vs. Modern Minimalist Marble\' but describes Blue/Silver nails on Marble. It ignores the \'vs\' component entirely.\n3. Crucially, the variants mix up the axes. Variant 1 uses the Songket prop (from Axis 2) and Variant 2 uses the Blue/Silver color (from Axis 1). This results in two completely different concepts (Red/Songket vs Blue/Marble) rather than a controlled test of specific axes. \n\nTo fix this:\n- If testing Color Story: Keep the prop constant (e.g., Marble) and show Red/Gold in one variant and Blue/Silver in the other.\n- If testing Styling Prop: Keep the color constant (e.g., Red/Gold) and show Songket in one variant and Marble in the other.\n\nAdditionally, Variant 2\'s headline \'Heritage Woven\' contradicts the visual brief of \'Modern Minimalist Marble\' which has no weaving. The copy must match the specific visual it is paired with.',2,'2026-08-28 14:35:53','2026-08-28 14:35:53'),(71,32,'Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep','Morning Light, Manicured.','Start your day with a considered beauty ritual using our disposable self-service manicure mannequin kit. It is the perfect companion for easy personal styling before you step out.','Shop the Morning Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A high-key, airy bedroom scene bathed in soft, diffused morning sunlight streaming through sheer white curtains. In the foreground, slightly out of focus to create depth, is a clean white duvet. The focal point is a mid-ground composition on a light oak bedside table: a sleek, open NailIt mannequin kit with a practice hand displaying a fresh, nude-pink manicure, next to a ceramic cup of steaming coffee and a single sprig of eucalyptus. The lighting is natural, bright, and optimistic, evoking a sense of calm readiness. The right side of the frame features significant negative space on the white wall or table surface, free of clutter, faces, or strong shadows, specifically reserved for text overlay. The mood is serene, fresh, and aspirational.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-right\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Morning Light, Manicured.\' is placed in the upper right quadrant against the bright, empty wall space. The body copy sits directly below it, left-aligned within that same vertical column. The CTA \'Shop the Morning Edit\' is positioned at the bottom of this text block, separated by a small margin.\", \"text_treatment\": \"bare\", \"composition_notes\": \"The eye is drawn first to the sunlit product setup (the mannequin and coffee), establishing the \'morning ritual\' context. The text occupies the clean, bright negative space on the right, ensuring high legibility without needing a heavy background panel. The \'bare\' treatment works here because the background is uniformly light and uncluttered, maintaining the airy, editorial aesthetic of the brand voice.\"}','flagged','Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The body copy claims the kit is for \'easy personal styling,\' which contradicts the Company Knowledge defining the product as a \'disposable self-service manicure mannequin kit\' used for \'application.\' The mannequin is a practice tool/companion for applying press-ons, not a styling tool itself. This is a functional misrepresentation. Additionally, the phrase \'manicure mannequin kit\' in Variant 1 is clunky and lacks the \'luxury beauty stylist\' voice required; it should be referred to as the \'NailIt Mannequin Kit\' or \'application companion\' as per the brand profile. Variant 2 is acceptable, but since Variant 1 requires a rewrite of the core value proposition, the whole set must be revised to ensure consistent voice and accuracy.',2,'2026-08-28 14:43:51','2026-08-28 14:43:51'),(72,32,'Setting: Morning Light Bedroom Ritual vs. Evening Vanity Prep','Evening Prep, Polished.','Wind down with a luxury-inspired nail fashion ritual at your vanity tonight. Use our guided fitting kit to create a polished look on your own schedule.','Shop the Evening Edit','{\"image_prompt\": \"A low-key, intimate evening vanity setting illuminated by the warm, golden glow of a vintage-style table lamp and soft ambient candlelight. The background is a deep, textured charcoal or navy velvet curtain, creating a luxurious, moody atmosphere. On the dark wood vanity surface, the NailIt mannequin kit is centrally framed, showcasing a bold, glossy red or deep burgundy manicure on the practice hand. Surrounding the kit are subtle luxury cues: a crystal perfume bottle, a silk hair scarf, and a jewelry dish. The lighting creates rich contrasts and deep shadows, emphasizing the gloss of the nails and the texture of the materials. The left side of the frame offers a dark, uniform area of the velvet backdrop or shadowed wall, completely free of objects or facial features, providing a high-contrast canvas for white text.\", \"placement_zone\": \"top-left\", \"text_placement\": \"The headline \'Evening Prep, Polished.\' is placed in the upper left quadrant against the dark, shadowed background. The body copy flows beneath it, maintaining left alignment. The CTA \'Shop the Evening Edit\' anchors the bottom of this text group.\", \"text_treatment\": \"glass-panel\", \"composition_notes\": \"The visual weight is on the right (the lit vanity and product), balancing the text on the left. Because the background is dark and textured, a \'glass-panel\' treatment is chosen to ensure the white text remains crisp and legible without blending into the shadows or competing with the velvet texture. This adds a layer of premium sophistication consistent with the \'luxury-inspired\' brand voice, distinguishing it from the morning variant\'s minimalism.\"}','flagged','Variant 1 fails the BRAND test. The body copy claims the kit is for \'easy personal styling,\' which contradicts the Company Knowledge defining the product as a \'disposable self-service manicure mannequin kit\' used for \'application.\' The mannequin is a practice tool/companion for applying press-ons, not a styling tool itself. This is a functional misrepresentation. Additionally, the phrase \'manicure mannequin kit\' in Variant 1 is clunky and lacks the \'luxury beauty stylist\' voice required; it should be referred to as the \'NailIt Mannequin Kit\' or \'application companion\' as per the brand profile. Variant 2 is acceptable, but since Variant 1 requires a rewrite of the core value proposition, the whole set must be revised to ensure consistent voice and accuracy.',2,'2026-08-28 14:43:51','2026-08-28 14:43:51');
